      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>VALIDS</head>
        <p>VALIDS</p>
        <p>BOVRIL imparts the delicious flavour cm FRESH MEAT to even the most tasteless dish and should always be used for Flavouring and Enriching Soups, Gravies, Hashes, and Made Dishes.</p>
        <p>BOVRIL</p>
        <p>- FOR -</p>
        <p>ents.</p>
        <p>price has hitherto been fourpence per unit, but from the present time, after an hour and a half,the charge is to be twopence per unit.l hear that some I have most excellent reasons for saying that Mr. other London vestries are taking steps to follow Gladstone has his own idea of the only possible the example of Shoreditch. So great is the effect point upon which the Liberal party can return to electricity is producing upon the returns of some power. He has been invited to make a pronounce- of the gas companies that one of them has had to ment on the subject, and he has very politely de- raise its charge for gas in order to maintain its rate dined to be drawn into any letter or notification, of dividend. But lam still correct in saying that he has privately told one of his friends in the course of conversation We have by no means heard the last of Tu-Lien- that, in his judgment, the House of Lords question Wan. Much dissatisfaction prevails in Conserve- is the only one upon which the Liberals have any ti tre circles with regard to the existing position of chance of securing a sufficient majority to enable affairs. The thin cynicism of Lord Salisbury's dis- them to gain office. missal of certain aspects of the question as of no importance, following, as; his declaration did, upon the equally singular absence of all reference to the matter in the Queen's Speech, is a general subject of conversation in Parliament and its precincts. It is the Premier's own supporters who are his most unsparing critics. Feelings of great uneasiness, of distrust, almost of anger, animate the bosoms of many staunch Conservatives just now,and although party allegiance will scarcely be shaken, it is evi- dent that members of the Government for a week or two to come must prepare themselves for some plain speaking in quarters that have hitherto been loyally dumb or loudly eulogistic.</p>
        <p>iips.PITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>Some people are talking freely about the late Mr. Villiers and his pension, and questions are al- most certain to be asked about the matter in the House of Commons. He was thought to be a poor man, and the legacy he had received, though he did not touch it, hardly bears out that view. On the supposition that be must have paid income tax on the interest accruing from the legacy, it is argued that successive Governments must have been well aware that he dii not need the second-class pen- sion he has been receiving for more than thirty y ears. Tha subject is naturally a delicate one, but it is better to obtain correct iuforraation than to allow the remarks which are being made to continue unchecked.</p>
        <p>Echoes of Wolverhampton still reach us. Inflated by their triumph, the Conservatives have resolved to make an assault on Sir Henry Fowler's seat, and a private meeting is to be held to morrow to select a candidate. Probably Sir A. Hickman thinks his own seat secure, but it certainly will be assailed, if not by Mr. Thorne, then by someone else. The Liberals are fully aroused. It has transpired that at one part of the counting Mr. Thorne was 300 votes ahead, and he received Sir A. Hickman's con- gratulations. The late boxes from the extremes of the division altered the result. A testimonial is being got up to Mr. Thorne.</p>
        <p>There is no authority for reports alleging a deci- sion on the part of the Government to drop the in- quiry as to the abuses of money-lending. lhe mat- ter remains in the hands of the principal Govern- ment Whip (Sir William Walrond),who has received no instructions on the subject. Whether the Com- mittee receive further evidence or not, however,the majority bave,l understand,heard enough to enable them to support a recommendation which would have a very impoitant effect upon the relations of the more exacting usurers and their victims. This recommendation is to the effect that the law should give judges discretion in deciding whether, and how far, they should order the payment of debts proved to include an extravagant rate of interest.ln the meantime the Treasury authorities have refused Mr. Farrow's claim for the payment of out-of-pocket expenses, so that it is doubtful at the taresent mo- ment whether the Committee, if reappointed, will again have that gentleman's very valuable assist- ance.</p>
        <p>The progress of the Siberian Railway is causing anxiety to many city merchants. When completed, it will open up a large new field of trade, but it will upset many of the existing carrying arrangements in the East. They are taking time by the forelock, and will at an early date hold a conference to dis- cuss the effects of the opening of the railway upon Eastern trade. The railway is still a long way from completion, and will not connect Europe and the Yellow Sea for some years to come; but that British firms are already beginning to count the consequences argues that the disturbance it will produce in trade will by and by be great.</p>
        <p>-- London has been for some time rather nervous, about the threat of an influenza epidemic,and upon the back of this alarm comes another as to an East End epidemic of whooping cough among chil i - dren. The influenza is chiefly prevalent n the north of London, but it finds victims nevertheless in all quarters. So far it is of a milder type than formeriy,and the mortality, while a measure of its i severity, sno measure of its extent. The outbreak of whooping cough which has now occurred in the East End is reported by medical men to be more severe than they have know* for many years. Some of them are discarding the old methods of treating whooping cough, and adopting a mode directed to the destruction of the germs, quinine being the chief medicine employed. The new pro- cess is said to be eminently successful,and no doubt medical journals will speedily put the profession in possession of the results of the experiment. Our ever-changing climate gets the blame of both the influenza and the whooping cough, but the London climate is not more changeable than that of the British Isles generally.</p>
        <p>The statement made in the north of England lately,by a member of Parliament,that he nad been informed by the Admiralty that the four discharged unionists at Portsmouth had been reinstated, must have been founded on some misunderstanding. They are still out of work, and the local indignation is now overflowing in public meetings.attended by the local member and men of all political views. The !lotion of the Admiralty is freely criticised, and an inquiry is called for. Any such inquiry will be an investigation into the action of the board itself, in connection with the cancelling of the fair wages re- tr=dution,and consequent successive reductions from the rate of wages. Mr. Goschen is not going to ap- point a committee to sit in judgment upon himself but his cancelling of the resolution and dismissal of the men will lead to a hot parliamentary debate, out of:which:it is difficult to see bow be can coma with credit.</p>
        <p>The experiment at Shoreditch, by whi6' the dust and refuse of the parish is consumed, instead of coal, in connection with its electric light installa- tion, has been so remarkably succe:-sfol that the price of electricity has already been greatly re- duced. The price up till now has been i.ixpence per unit for two hours ;it is now to be sixpence per unit for tbe first hour au d a half. After two hours the</p>
        <p>Mr. Healy, by the way, is not always quick to wit. He was telling the House on Wednesday that Ireland ought to be allowed to make her own roads, her own harbours, her own piers. He forgot for the moment that it is one of the customs of the Commons to indulge in laughter when the word " piers" is mentioned, the point of the joke being that the Lords also are designated by a noun of identical pronunciation Mr. Healy failed to recall the tradition, even when there was an encouraging repetition of the laughter which plainly told him to try again. The hon. mem- ber simply looked puzzled,and a disappointed House lapsed into sulky silence.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. H. Reid, the Premier of New South Wales,</p>
        <p>as a pleasant way of meeting an awkward ques- tion wit b a bland and tactful answer. Asked by an hon. Me mber whether be bad "received any notice of gray disaffection amongst the Free Trade party," •be Premier imperturbably replied; " I believe that occasionally there is a small ripple on he smiling surface of our full content, but nothing more."</p>
        <p>General Booth occupied himself during his voy- age to America in arranging the contents of a large Salvationist Hymnal, which is shortly to appear. Salvationists have felt the want of what may be called a treasury of hymns, as distinct from aucces- cessive volumes containing selections. Hence the coming publication, which will contain ru ore than 700 hymns.</p>
        <p>I you wish to go sborping, and never regret it. When you ask for an article see that you get it." —Referee.</p>
