      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>SAFE THINGS TO SAY ABOUT THE BABY.—Ah, isn't he sweet? Doesn't it look like its father? May I kiss the dear little darling? How fat it is ! Oh, the cute little dear Look at its pretty little toes! I don't think I ever saw a finer child of its age. How bright it is! I never saw so young a bady with such an intelligent expression. Mayn't I hold it just for a minute? (Everybody is expected to say something pleasant when they see a new baby, and any one of these remarkes is war- ranted to give perfeet satisfaction to the happy mother.) THE TROUBLE.—" I used to have a terrible time finding the things I put away," said Mrs. Smythe; "but till recently I have overcome the difficulty by keeping a note-book in which I entered the place whenever an article was laid away." "Why, that seems just perfect !" said Mrs. Tomkins ; "hut what's the matter recently?" "Why, I've put away the horrid note-book, too!"</p>
        <p>THE MAN FOR TETE PLACE.—Awkward Youth: " Sa-ay, can't yer give me a job on this ere paper! I doan't 'spose I kin learn to be a editor, but I'm told there is other sorts o' work in newspaper offices." Mr. Beatemall (great editor) : "You might suit in some capacity, perhaps. Do you know anything about bookkeeping?" "No." "Are you good at figures ?" " Ah, yes, I'm good at tiggers.' "How much are nine and seven!" " Lemme see. Put down nine, and then put down seven alongside of it, and that makes ninety-seven." "Well, sir, you would not do for the counting- house ; but I see no reason why you should not rise to proud eminence in the circulation depart- ment."</p>
        <p>A BROAD HINT.—An elderly lady fell a victim to the popular craze, and on one occasion, arrayed in full cycling regalia, visited a garrulous old crone to whom she occasionally despatched parcels of broken food and so on. "Good morning, Snsan," said the lady. "I have brought you a small parcel containing tea and sugar, and you will also find a nice warm flannel petticoat." "Thank ye, me teddy," repliea the old dame, scanning the portly donor from top to toe. "I thank ye for the tea and sugar, but I would rather not take the flannel petticoat." " Not—take—the —petticoat! " itera- ted the surprised lady. " W—Why ?" "Well, me teddy, beggin' yer pardon for being so bold, but seeing as 'ow yer leddyship wants the petticoat wosser than meself." The lady did not linger long. JUST WHAT HE EXPECTED. —A fellow We all know at the club, by name Jaspar, is a man who at times manages to be genuinely funny. Strolling down the street the other day, he came un- expectedly upon a crowd gathered together just outside a chemist's shop. The centre of attraction proved to be a lady with a wasp-like waist, who, for some reason or other, had elected to swoon there. Tenderly—very tenderly—they carried her into the shop of the chemist, who proceeded to do what he could for the inanimate one. Jaspar, meanwhile, had taken a turn up the road. Re- turning from this after a space of about 10 minutes, he looked into the shop and called out: "How is she now?" "Oh," replied the chemist, it she's coming to." "Ah ! ' replied Jaspar. "Come in two, has she, poor thing? pit what I was afraid of." -</p>
        <p>_ DREADING A SLIP.—Mrs. Swayback was in tears. "But, mother," said her daughter, "you couldn't desire a finer man than Mr. Trivvet for a Mon-in-law, and, of course, you expected me to get married some time. Mrs. Swayback's sobs broke out afresh, and for a time she declared that nothing could recompense her for the loss of her child, and refused to be comforted. Then she applied a hand- kerchief vigorously to her eyes, and asked: "When is the wedding to be?" "In about six months, mamma, dear." "Six Months "exclaimed Mrs. Swayback. What on earth does the procrastinator mean by putting it off that long? Mary Ann, I don't believe he intends to marry you at all, so I dont ! If he did he'd insist on having the wedding come off inside six weeks at the furthest."</p>
        <p>AN ELEPHANT'S REVENGE.—One day a tall, raw- boned youth bought a bag of gingerbread-nuts, and by way of tantalising an elephant, kept offer- ing a handful to him, and then eating them him- self. The animal was nettled by this injurious treatment. Presently the young man's attention was attracted by a noise behind him, and he turned his head to look. In an instant the elephant extended his trunk to within two inches of his tormentor's ear, and trumpeted loudly into it. The effect was electric; the youth started, turned deadly pale, his hat tumbled off, and for a moment he regarded himself as a dead man. This was a generous revenge on the part of the elephant, who finished the fallen bag of nuts without a scruple. He might have drawn the offender for- ward and killed him on the spot, but he was too noble to retaliate further, and, by the humorous twinkle in his eye, it was evident that he privately enjoyed the fright he had given. AN UNLITCKLY MAN.—Not long since in a village in the north of Scotland, the parish minister meet- inc., a farm servant who is a member rf h: flock, the following conversation ensued: Parish Minister; "Well, John, and how are things doing with you? I hope you are keeping well." Farm Servant: " Hech, sir, it's work I hae to dae ; nae rest frae morn tae nicht ; work an' work, an' no' a minute's peace for me." Parish Minister : " Well, John, we must all do our share in the work of this world. Remember, it is only the preparation for a better world, where there will be no more work to be done." Farm Servant: " Weel, sir, that may be ft• the likes o' you, but Pm no' sae sure that there Via be naething for me to dae in the other world. It will he the same thing there, and I'll be told, 'Julio, clean the sun,' ',John, hang oot the moon,' 'John, light the stars;' an' so on. I've nae doubt they'll find always something for me to do, unlucky man that I am 1"</p>
