      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>EXAMINER ',TWAY, rusw v 11, 189 R</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER ',TWAY, rusw v 11, 189 R</p>
        <p>(Copyrig)t.)</p>
        <p>m the bud." Mr. Hughes shuddered, and made I another unsuccessful attempt to put in a word. He</p>
        <p>was following Mrs. Treherne closely as she hurried towards the carriage. "My daughter Constance," he heard her saying, "is a reliable, quiet character. But Gwendoline is spirited—oh! here they both are. Good-bye dears, I must not wait a moment. Mr. Hughes has kindly offered to take charge of you.,, _ _</p>
        <p>She kissed them both hurriedly, and whispered to each: "Be economical," then added out loud, as she entered the carriage: "Yon must consult Mr. Hughes about everything, remember, dears—about everything." Here Mr. Hughes stepped forward, stern resolu- tion depicted on his face. But before he had time to utter a word the horses had started off at a brisk trot, and Mrs. Trehenae was waving her adieux. With a strong sense of indignation Mr. Hughes watched the carriage out of sight, then glanced round with considerable interest at the two girls whose guardianship had been thus unexpectedly forced upon him. Gwendoline was tall, graceful and dark, with vivacious, fascinating manners, and a face that possessed the irresistible charm of ever- varying expression. Constance was small and slight, with fair hair and large expressive brown eyes. Though not possessing the striking personal attractions of her sister, there was a distinctly pleasing element in her sweet and thoughtful face. Both were quite conscious of the fact that their mother had probably strained matters in order to obtain Mr. Hughes's acquiescence to her request; and they mentally decided not to allow him to imagine that they would take advantage of his good-nature. In silence and with quiet dignity they re-entet. d the hotel and returned to their TOMS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WHEEL OF FATE</head>
        <p>THE WHEEL OF FATE</p>
        <p>BY MRS. BAGOT HABIB, Author of "Love's Revenge," "Bianca," "The</p>
        <p>Silver Lining," &amp;c., Sz.e.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER I.</p>
        <p>"Yes, Constance, you can fasten up the rugs; I shall certainly not require them to-day. It is so very warm. And, Gwendoline "—here Mrs. Treherne turned towards her elder daughter—"go downstairs quickly and ask the hotel proprietor if he is quite sure that the carriage will be here in time. " _ _</p>
        <p>"But I have asked him twice already!" was the reply, spoken in a tone of mild expostulation. "Both times he said he was quite certain that it would be here punctually." "Really, Gwendoline, you should not take every- thin, you hear for granted," rejoined her mother with °a shade of annoyance. "Ask him a third time. One cannot feel too positive when so much pends on punctuality." Gwendoline rose with reluctance to do as her mother directed.</p>
        <p>"Now, Constance," continued Mrs. Treherne as the door closed, "you can sew that button on my love; and when you have finished that there are ,everal other little things that you can do for me." Momentary silence followed. Mrs. Treherne hrought out her keys and tried to discover which one fitted her hand boor, ; then, having satisfactorily ived the question, she immediately began to talk ,w,tin, this time in an aggrieved voice.</p>
        <p>-"I wish your uncle S-tewart had not chosen this particular moment for being ill," said she ; "I was looking forward to enjoying a complete rest here. It is really too disappointing to have to return home after being in Switzerland only a fortnight; and I do not feel absolutely sure of the wisdom of leaving you two girls here without a chaperon An anxious ring was now perceptible in her voice. "The people we know here are, tif course, only casual acquaintances. Happily, Vilnite is a trust- worthy maid; and your father will probably be able to join you in a week or two. I hope Gwendoline will act discreetly and with circum- spection, and not laugh so cheerfully as she usually does. You, Constance, will be discreet, lam sure of that."</p>
        <p>"And I am quite sure that Gwendoline will be too, mother. You need not be anxious about us. We are quite capable of taking care of ourselves for a few days; we ought to be, at our age." "Yes, I supposc that girls of twenty-two and twenty-three ought to possess a little common- sense."</p>
        <p>"The proprietor assures me that the carriage will be here punctually," said Gwendoline, entering, "and he prays that Madame will not be anxious on the subject.' " "That sounds satisfactory ; and now, Gwendoline" —Mrs. Treherne stopped, and glanced round; she had a genius for finding occupation for other people—"go quickly, dear, and tell White that if she has fastened on the labels and packed my bonnets she can come and speak to me." In answer to her mistress's summons White—a spare, prim-looking maid—soon appeared. "You will be most careful of the young ladies ? " observed Mrs. Treherne, her. "Yes, mum." "And wait upon them mspoesatkinatgteln.intipvreleyssi—veeslpyectoi- ally if they catch colds?" "Yes, mum." "Your master will probably join them ne,xt week. Meanwhile, of course, you understand—' . "Oh, mother! pray do not be go very anxious about us," interrupted Gwendoline, fearful of what Mrs. Treherne might say next. " I can assure you we shall be quite safe." "I am not anxious, in the strictest sense of the word," said Mrs. Treherne with a tinge of annoy- ance; then turning to the maid she added authori- tatively: "I have said all I wished, White ; and—my bonnets of course are carefully packed ? " "Yes, mum," was the answer, spoken inn manner that said. "Did you ever know me not pack care- fully? " _ _</p>
        <p>Alter White had retired no one spoke for a few minutes; then Mrs. Treherne cheerfully exclaimed: "I see my way out of the difficulty! I will ask Mr. Hughes, the chaplain, to take charge of you— to assume, in fact, a religious protectorate over yo just for the time being, until your father arrives. "u "But, mother!" gasped Gwendoline in astonish- ment, "we know nothing about him, and he knows still less—if that is possible—of us!" "He is a nice, quiet, unsophisticated man!" con- tinued Mrs. Treherne, speaking with greater decision, and not deigning to notice her daughter's remarks. Then rising, she added: "I shall go at once and speak to him. I know he is a well- connected, respectable person." "But mother, we are such perfect strangers to each other ! " interposed Constance. "My dear, that is not of the slightest consequence. Anyone with ordinary powers of divination can see at a glance that we are reople of recognised social standing. 1 shall not refer to your father's posi- tion, naturally, for more reasons than one. It would be most foolish to allow it to be known that girls with--er—with prospects were staying here without a chaperon." "Perhaps Mrs. Hughes may not like her husband being asked to take charge of us, mother," said Constance ; "and I am sure it would be much pleasanter for us to be quite independent of every- body." "My dear child, don't talk foolishly," said Mrs. Treherne in a conclusive manner. "Mrs. Hughes is a homely, pleasant person, and will certainly be pleased to think her husband is of use. Without further parley Mrs. Treherne put on her veil, drew on her gloves, and then departed to find Mr. Hughes and to inform him of the high honour—according to her own manner of thinking —destined to befall him. He was comfortably ensconced in a lounge chair on the verandah, book in hand—the personification of a man with a clear conscience enjoying a well- merited holiday. He had come to Switzerland with the full determination to face as little exertion as possible, and to enjoy himself in a mild, unex- citing manner. He listened with polite, though mild, attention to Mrs. Treherne's rambling explanation of her brother's serious illness, and the anxiety into which it had plunged the family. But when she reached that stage of her troubles which referred to the sudden curtailment of her visit to Switzerland he became at once interested. "You must, of course, feel disappointed," he said sympathetically. "There is nothing more neces- sary to people than a complete rest and change once a year. As you are leaving in such haste, perhaps I might be of some little—er—service to you. If so, I hope you will have no hesitation in mention- ing the matter." "You are very kind," replied Mrs. Treherne in a tone of relief, "and I am sure you speak from a genuine desire to assist me." "Yes, quite so," smilingly concurred the clergy- man.</p>
        <p>"You know my two daughters ? " she said rather abruptly. "Yes." Mr. Hughes's face became slightly over- clouded. "They are remaining here." "Here? Alone? At Les Avants I " There was concentrated surprise in his voice. Mrs. Treherne glanced critically at him, and asked herself the question—" Does he look a thoroughly trustworthy person?" An emphatic "Yes " arose in reply ; so without further hesitation she plunged into the subject which occupied her mind.</p>
        <p>"I am sure I can leave my daughters safely in your charge," she said rather hurriedly. "My charge? I don't—quite—quite—under- stand," stammered Mr. Hughes in evident conster- nation.</p>
        <p>"Yes—your charge repeated Mrs. Treherne, utterly ignoring any psssible wish he might have to decline the responsibility ; "and I am sure you will kindly see that no harm happens to them. Their father will join them in a week or ten days. Entre nous, they are attractive, and nothing would annoy me more than their becoming embroiled in any silly love affairs—you understand? Good-bye " —she extended her hand effusively, but without ceasing to speak for an itstant—" so many, many thanks. Tell Mrs. Hughes, please, I so much regretted not having the pleasure of saying good- bye to her. I see tha carriage is waiting for me ; there cannot be a moment to lose! So good of you to offer to be of nee. Sympathy is always so welcome." Here Mr. Hughes made a vain effort to speak. He had been waiting with his lips apart for some moments, ready to rush in with expostu- lations on the first available chance; but no such opportunity was given him. Mrs. Treherne rambled on, without apparently waiting to take breath. "I am sure the dear girls will be safe under your itharge,7 she continued "kindly nip all flirtatious</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHAPTER 11.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER 11.</p>
        <p>"And so ycri have positively consented to take charge of those two attractive girls, of whom we know nothing—absolutely nothing ! " gasped Mrs. Hughes, sinking into a chair, and regarding her husband with unqualified indignation and surprise. "The position' was forced upon me," he replied in an explanatory voice. "Forced upon you!" repeated his wife. "Are you, then, incapable of saying ' no ' to a presumptu- ous request ? "</p>
        <p>"But, Mrs. Treherne did not give me an oppor- tunity of saying anything." "Then you should have made an opportunity," she said with decision.</p>
        <p>"But before I had time to recover from my surprise at the request, and could fully grasp the difficulties of the situation, Mrs. Treherne said good-bye, got into the carriage, and drove away! No one could have launched a request more pre- cipitately; and her manner of assuming I had said yes was—was—quite unnerving. What could I do? I could not raise my voice and call after her: I cannot take charge of your daughters.' It would have been unseemly." "Naturally it would. But had I been there I should have acted with more decision," persisted his wife; "and it is of no use your trying to explain away your own thoughtlessness. Of course, you must have acquiesced either by look or deed when Mrs. Treherne brought up the subject. It is perfectly incomprehensible to me how you could have acted so foolishly, especially considering that we came here for rest and quiet, and to be free from all responsibilities. Had I been present I should have risen to the occasion and utterly declined to have those two stylish girls left in our charge. Supposing they are flirts? Mr. Hughes shuddered. The thought was terrible. He had a deep-rooted fear of flirts. "And supposing they insist on strolling about the grounds after dusk, notwithstanding our remonstrances," continued Mrs. Hughes in the same strain, noticing with satisfaction that she was making an impression, "and supposing they shew a preference for talking to the—the male visitors at the hotel? " She almost whispered the last few words.</p>
        <p>"fiat, my dear, that would be too dreadful—so compromising for us! How I wish you had been present when Mrs. Treherne approached the sub- ject! If her daughters behave in the manner that you suggest, I fear that we shall be placed in a very awkward position. I—l—really do not know how I ought to act under the circumstances. I should not feel justified in allowing them to take moonlight walks alone; and yet I do not exactly see how I could accompany them." "Of course you could not accompany them, William," replied his wife decisively. 'lf you did so, and it became known at Twiddenham, you would forfeit all your influence over the young and frivolous members of the congregation. Everyone would say that you had been flirting." "My dear! they could scarcely say that!" re- joined her husband in consternation.</p>
        <p>_ many would say it. You know how eagerly some people seize the smallest opportunity to say things that injure others. A little sprinkling of truth is all that is requisite, in their opinion, to cover the most unkind slander."</p>
        <p>"I am afraid you are right, in a measure; but I should merely accompany the Miss Tr*ernes in the capacity of chaplain," he argued. "Taking moonlight walks with frivolous and handsome girls is not a chaplain's duty," briefly observed Mrs. Hughes. "Perhaps not," agreed her husband, leaning back meditatively. He was a thin, gaunt man, with a kind and sym- pathetic face. It was a plain face, yet so full of character that it offered a pleasant study. He could not be more than forty-five, judging from his general appearance; and his wife appeared about three years younger. Both bore the stamp of un- questionable respectability, and were exactly the class of people who regarded duties and responsibilities as matters not to be lightly under- taken, and certainly never shirked. "Now, tell me,. Maria," Mr. Hughes began, speaking slowly, "what line of conduct would you suggest our adopting with regard to the two Miss Trellemes ? Their father will probably join them next week—meanwltile---" He stopped and looked interrogatively towards his wife. "We shall have to take an interest in their occupations, and see that they keep respectable hours," she observed practically. "It would scarcely be considered aecessary, though, for me to—er—conduct them—" Mr. Hughes came to an abrupt standstill; then added hurriedly—"to their rooms at night ?" "Of course not I " exclaimed his wife. "It would be the talk of the hotel if Jou did. You don't seem in the least capable of understanding your position or of acting discreetly." "I confess I feel very much at sea in the matter. The position is one of great novelty to me, and I feel strangely incapable of grasping its details. It seems to me that there are remarkable difficulties in my acting as temporary guardian to these two London girls. If they were only district visitors, or Sunday-school teachers, or old workhouse men and women, I should feel so much easier in my mind. I always know intuitively what they want to talk about; but these London girls have quite different types of mind. I should imagine they prefer talking about pictures, and music, and the theatre and opera—things, my dear, that you and I know nothing about. Yet we must do our duty by these Miss Trehenaes, and appear interested in them generally." _ _ .</p>
        <p>"I suppose" we must. But, William, don't let them suspect that you are not a man of learning and experience. Try and appear calm, dignified, and somewhat haughty before them. As for con- versation, surely you can talk cleverly about your life at Oxford, and the great preachers of the day; and you 'know those two men I often hear you mention to Mr. Bennett--you could bring their names up." "Carlyle and Ruskin, you mean, perhaps, Maria." "Yes; and for my part I shall impress upon the Miss Trehernes that there is nothing so charming as a quiet, reticent manner. I shall certainly ask them to be punctual for table d'h6te. Their habit of entering the salle-a-manger after everyone is seated attracts too much notice. It caused every man to glance round at them yesterday. If they only dressed badly and were plain, how much easier our path of duty would be." "Yes, it would," concurred Mr. Hughes. "If you notice, their general bearing is admirable." "Oh, yes," said his wife shortly. "But I should strongly advise you, William, to take as little personal notice of them as possible. If they con- sult you specially on any matter, let me know what it is about before you give them an answer." "I will, Maria, you can rely on that." Then he added with emphasis: " It will be a great relief to me when their father joins them, and relieves me from all responsibility." "I warmly echo those sentiments," said his wife fervently. "But if you are circumspect and dis- creet I do not think any positive harm will come of your having them left under your charge; and perhaps we shall even be enabled to influence them for good. Judging by their style of dress, I should not imagine that they were religious. Those large, stylishly trimmed hats of theirs have a dis- tinctly worldly look." "I, am afraid you .are right—still—" Mr.</p>
        <p>Hughes did not finish the sentence, but rose up and ' looked out of the window. "It has left off raining, Maria," he added cheerfully; "we'll go out for a walk at once. There's just an hour before dinner."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER 111. A few days after Mrs. Treherne's sudden depar- ture from Les Avants, her two daughters were ' ensconced in a shady nook bordering the woods near the hotel.</p>
        <p>Constance was leaning back reading a novel in a desultory, non-interested manner; and Gwendoline was sketching. "We are managing ' to live very happily here by ourselves," said the latter, breaking in upon the silence. "I am sure mother need not trouble herself so much about us. Father will join us as soon as he can; meanwhile, we are quite safe here. As for exchanging Switzerland for a stupid, fashionable English watering-place, I am entirely against such a suggestion. Are you not, Constance?" "Yes—you know how I dislike English seaside places. I think they are the dullest and most uninteresting places in the world. I would far rather pass the summer in London than go to one. You simply ruin your complexion without gaining any benefit in return." "I shall write and tell mother that we are managing admirably alone, and that there is a dearth of bachelors at the hotel. The only man who approaches eligibility is that French artillery officer with a bullet in his arm. The woman who marries him will have to spend a part of every day talking about that bullet. My interest in it has evaporated already." ClNvendoline eyed her sketch critically, then continued : "But I am very sorry for him. It must be so sad to be a semi. invalid at his age; and he is so handsome and bright. What a funny little gushing woman his mother "I wish you would not talk to them so much," said Constance in a tone of reproof.</p>
        <p>"Then I won't in future; but they are really interesting people. Madame has known some of the greatest artists of the age, and has an amusing way of discussing them. However, as she possesses an eligible son, it would probably be wiser not to talk to her. Wiser, at least, from mother's point of view, though from my own—bah It's lowering to the dignity of womanhood to always imagine that, a man must have matrimonial projects on hand if he makes himself agreeable to a girl. I think it would be a good plan if you were to write mother a chatty letter, describing how we pass our days. You can dwell upon the fact that I do nothing scarcely but sketch, and am the most circumspect of all circumspect spinsters. Mother has a great opinion of what you say." Constance smiled. "It seems strange that my opinion should be taken before yours, Gwendoline." 'lt does, when one comes to think of it. But I really do not mind, for you certainly are an excel lent and wise person. As for myself, I feel at the present moment as far removed from any inclination to flirt as the North Pole is from the South. The more interested I become in my painting the more sedate I grow. As for you, Con, I don't believe you could flirt if you tried."</p>
        <p>w "No; and I hai;t3 not the slightest desire to try. I should like to know what Mr. and Mrs. Hughes think of our being left on their hands. I believe they imagined that we expected to be asked to spend our days at their side!" „ ._</p>
        <p>"Our quiet display of independence has sur- prised them, I think. I saw Mr. Hughes eyeing us curiously during breakfast." "Hush ! " exclaimed Constance. "Here he comes. _I hope he won't see us."</p>
        <p>Mr. Hughes was advancing slowly, a umtlitative, look on his face and a book under his arm. Truth- fully speaking, he was beginning to feel that he had not done his duty with regard to Gwendoline and Constance. He could not quite decide in his own mind if it was his fault or theirs that they had only exchanged a few passing remarks since Mrs. Treherne's departure. He was half afraid that his manner had lacked cordiality, and he was feeling somewhat conscience-stricken on the subject. So the moment he caught a glimpse of them he. decided to seize the present opportunity for shewing that, so far as he was concerned, his feelings were, strictly speaking, friendly and cordial. "How do you do ? " he observed, walking- up to. Gwendoline. "You have chosen a very pretty spot for sketching." "This is our usual resort when it is very warm," she answered brightl</p>
        <p>"It is a charmingly shady nook," said Mr. Hughes, glancing round.. Then he shook hands with Constance, and his, heart began to soften towards these clever, independent girls. "May I look at your sketch ? " he said, returning to Gwendoline.</p>
        <p>"I am drab! q scarcely worth looking at now. I only began `lll morning." "So far it looks very promising !" Ile stepped back and eyed it critically, then added: "You are no mere amateur, I see. Probably you are very fond of painting." "I love it—intensely," she replied with warmth. "Do you exhibit? " "Yes. I had a small picture at the Academy last year. It was skyed, unfortunately, so of course you did not notice it." "I very seldom go to the Academy. During our short visits to London I prefer spending -my time attending the Exeter Hall meetings and hearing the. leading preachers of the day. What church do you, attend, may I ask?" "Oh, I attend several. Would'you like to hear how I usually spend my Sundays?" "Yes, I should." There was a deeply interestedt ring in Mr. Hughes's-voice. "I go to Matins at All Saints', Margaret-street," Gwendoline began. "The moment they are over. I hurry off to Mr. Stopford Brooke's church, and get there in time for the sermon. After that. I. go home to lunch. When I have finished my. luncheon I start off immediately to a hospital and give out books to the patients, and I generally remain and take part in the patients' service, which is held by a Low Churchman. I always go to Mr. Haweis's church in the evening. I am trying. not to, be narrow-minded, you see." "I am afraid your religious views will become. —er--confused, said Mr. Hughes, looking per- plexed; and he at once proceeded to shift his ground. "You have not forgotten your Catechism, /PPE)," he said impressively. "I find so few grown-up people retain any knowledge of it. "I believe I could say. it backwards. Shall I "No—no! it would lose so much of ita meaning, he hastily observed. ' . _ _ .</p>
        <p>A smile flitted across Gwendoline's face. Then Mr. Hughes turned his attentioa to Constance, and made inquiries about the invalid at home and had a little chat concerning general topics. After that he nodded a friendly farewell and con- tinued his walk.</p>
        <p>I like him" said Gwendoline, in her usual decided way, the moment he was beyond the range of hearing. "Ye has character, though it has been dwarfed by a humdrum, monotonous life. Shall we ask him and his wife to join us for tea in the woods to-morrow ?"</p>
        <p>"She is so oppressively self-satisfied," replied Constance, "and has a way of imparting her know- ledge to others that is distressingly aggavating. She always appears to be proclaiming: 'This is my opinion, and I am a conscientious Christian.'‘" "Yes; she has an all-absorbing confidence in herself and her own opinions; and, do you know, I think it is one of the pleasantest things to have. Confidence in one's own abilities is the centre-point of success and happiness. People who lack self- confidence must be miserable, and can never hope to do good work. It stands to reason that this must be so. Why, then, should we begrudge poor Mrs. Hughes this comfortable doctrine? She believes in herself, she believes in her husband, and she also believes that the world was made for them, not they for the world—a very pleasant theory to possess, one which must greatly add to the pleasure of living." "Oh, Gwendoline) don't preach. It's appalling to be preached at on a hot day like this—it gives one a headache. Anyhow, if we do ask the Hughes for tea to-morrew you must not take your turn at making the tea. Imagine what Mrs. Hughes would say if she saw you putting the tea-leaves into cold water and leaving them to boil up in the kettle! " "A sensible, time-saving arrangement, and not to be desised when one is trying to catch sunlight effects." Gwendoline spoke with her usual imper- turbable good humour. "If they do come I shall make the tea myself,'" observed Onstance conclusively.</p>
        <p>(To be continua)</p>
      </div>
