      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>SOCIAL PROBLEMS.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL PROBLEMS.</p>
        <p>MINERAL ROYALTIES.</p>
        <p>By JOSEPH RYDER, General Secretary of tbc Land Nationalisation Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>ARTICLE XV.</head>
        <p>ARTICLE XV.</p>
        <p>Although we speak of a mineral lease, or a lease of mines. the contract Is not in reality a lease at all in the sense in which we speak of an agricultural lease. There is no fruit —that is to say, there is no increase ; there is no sowing or reaping in the ordinary sense of the term, and there are no periodical harvests. What we call a mineral lease is really. when properly considered, a sale out-and-out of a portion of the land. It is liberty given to a particular individual, for a specific length of time, to go into and under the land, and to get certain things there if he can find them,and to take them away just as if he had bought so much of the soil. The Late LORD CICINCPLLOR CAIRN*. The owners of minerals in the less favoured mines are forced in a losing trade to make concessions to their lessees. though they are able, if they hold out for their full legal rights, to see all the capital of the tenant made valueless be- fore the loss begins to fall on themselves In respect of the royalty. SIR ISAAC LOWTHIAN BELL.</p>
        <p>-- We are working out as fast as we can the foundations of our prosperity as a manufacturing nation. J. S. JEANS, Secretary to the Iron and Steel InStitnte. I have already referred to the character of the land- lords' claim to own the bowels of the earth with all the mineral treasures. And if it be impolitic to allow pri- vate property in the surface of earth, it is tenfold more absurd to permit private property in the mineral de- posits which lie under it. Indeed there is no other part of the British landlord system which so emphatically manifests the supinesss aud indifference of the nation upon great and important questions. This indifference is not confined to the mantle' workers of the country. In fact the great body of capitalist employers are apt to look with deep suspicion upon those who, like myself, assail the landlord's claims to the mineral royalties. Said Sir Isaac Lowthian Bell, himself a very eminent repro. sentative of the mining masters, " I have not in my ex- perience, met a single lessee who dissociates the right of the landlord to the minerals, any more that he would question his title to the surface." Mining lessees and the masters in the iron and steel industry have for a long time agitated for an im- provement in the present arrangements for settling the rents and royalties on minerals, and in the course of their agitation they have disclosed some grave abuses aud some serious grievances. But they have gone no further, as a general rule, than to suggest the establish- ment of a siding scale by means of which the paymenta to the landlord should be determined by the market price of the minerals. Now, although this would be a much fairer and snort, satisfa:ltory plan than the present, it would in no wise settle the difficulty. For the real heart of the evil lies in the fact that individuals are empowered to levy a tax upon the mining and kindred industries for their own private purposes, and in return render absolutely no service. Obviously tbat tax should be reduced as much as possible. But its extinction is of infinitely greater moment than its mere diminution. But, in pass ' leg I hope it will he understood that I do not desire to 'confiscate landlords' incomes. They are fully entitled to what the late venerable Professor Newman (for many years a vice-president of the Land Nationalisation Society) called " merciful consideration." For they are no worse than other men, and do not deserve to be punished by Impoverishment simply because they hap- pen to belong to the landed minority. The nationalisa- tion of the minerals, it is safe to say, will never be accomplished by the sweeping spoliation of a class. But this phase ef the question I will at present defer. Tan Lon:moan TRIM/TH.</p>
        <p>Rents, royalties, and wayleaves on minerals vary in different parts of the country, and it is im- possible to state their exact amount. Although all such receipts are chargeable with income tax, mines are assessed upon their annual value of profit, without any &amp;Auction being made for royalties or simi- lar charges. The lessee therefore pays the whole of the income tax and deducts from the royalties, Ste., the in- come tax he has paid thereon. Therefore there is only one way of finding their amount—v)z., by getting that information from the lessees or the landlords. No won- der, thin, that the Royal Commissioners reported that upon this part of their inquiry they were " met with serious difficulties." At the best they express the belief that the figures they obtained and summarised are "sub- stantially reliable." Igive the totals here :</p>
        <p>- BovaWes.— Wayleaves. Coal Ironstone and iron or 561.122 14,781 Other metals 87,068 •••</p>
        <p>Total 4,656,543 ... 216.697 Thus a total of £4,873240 is obtained. In the opinion, however,of many experts this is considerably below the actual truth. Mr. C. M. Percy, of Wigan, an eminent authority,some years ago estimated that the coal royal- ties and wayleaves alone amounted to more than five and a half millions sterling. To show that the Commis- sioners egures are not absolutely reliable,l will mention one instance, It was given in evidence that the best gas coal in Scotland paid a royalty, in some cases' of as much as 2s. 6d. a ton. But the Commission Report places the maximum coal royalty in Scotland at it. 3d. a ton. An inaccuracy of this kind obviously discounts the Report. A FINS OF £25,000. Some remarkable evidedce was given by a deputation of members of Parliament that waited on Mr. Childers when be was Liberal Home Secretary. Mr. Cony beare, representing the Cornish miners, showed that mine landlords there possessed and exercised powers as abso- lute as those of feudal times. There are heavy fixed rates and dues on the output, paid in very many cases not from profits, but from calls upon the sharehrlders, the mines themselves being worked at a loss. Enormous charges are made for surface damage to land, as much as £lOO per acre for land whose annual value is only El. When the lease of one mine was renewed for the short term (as such things go) of 21 years, the landlord ex- acted a fine of £25,0011 for the renewal. In the case of the Devon Great Consols Mine the Duke of Bedford levied a fine of £20,000 for renewal. . .</p>
        <p>The late Mr. Stephen Mason, then representing a division of Lanarkshire, mentioned the case of the Duke of Hamilton, who draws £114,000 a year from his Lan- arkshire minerals, or the wages of fully 1,800 miners ;, not because he laboured, and not because he invested j any capital in productive labour, but solely because he I was the descendant of an old-timed grantee of the land under which the minerals were afterwards discovered. Mr. Mason gave another instance of hardship under the existing law. A certain company spent £BO,OOO to get at a seam of coal. They reached it, but found that rent and royalty would together absorb every penny of preld. The landlord would, nevertheless, have his en- tire pound of flesh according to the bond. Ele would not abate the royalty named in the contract. Conse- quently the machinery had been standing idle for four years. A TREE IS KNOWN ST ITS PEWITS. It is a frequent complaint among lessees that they are made to pay for "shorts "—that is, they have to pay royalty en coal or ore that they have not been able to . get. The Wigan Coal and Iron Company have paid as mach as £60,000 on this head. The Barrow Hematite Steel Company had a share sapital of £2,000,000. They paid £126,000 a year in various dues to three noble lords—Devonshire, Buccleuch and Muncaster—but for years not a penny beyond that could be paid in dividends. How true is it that rent is. the first charge upon every industry ! The landlord °ernes first ; the poor shareholder cornea after him. And this difficulty of adjnsting the royalties to the variations of the market is one of the gravest obstacles which the • coal, iron and steel trades have to encounter. As Sir leaac Lowthian Bell said, by holding out for thzir full legal rights the landlords can see their tenants' capital vanish before they themselves feel the pinch of depres- lion. Surely this is the exact opposite of what justice demands. For, in strict equity, rent should not arise at all until the workers in the industry have been re- munerated, It is bad enough for the earnings of labour- to be diminished by the endowment of idleness; but it Is doubly bad that the non-producer should have a prior Ildaim to the produce on pain of stopping production alto- 'ether.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>UNDAIPLOYND LABOUR AND CAPVI'AL.</head>
        <p>UNDAIPLOYND LABOUR AND CAPVI'AL.</p>
        <p>I would fle-r-e--tiliO-t-e- the declaration of M:Forsyth,. an eloquent land reformer, in 1835, a time of depression. "Out of the eighty blast-furnaces in Cumberland forty are at this moment standing idle, and the others are but partially employed. There are many causes which might have the effect of keeping these forty blait-fur- nacea idle. They might be idle for want of capital, they might be idle for want of men willing to work. Wall. the Cumberland furnaces are put out,not because a any lack of capital, for only within the last week or two a company of employers there were willing to sink .00,000 in raising iron ore, and ware only prevented from doing so by the landlord's ultimatum that he would not reduce his royalty of Zs. Bd. a ton on the ore which might be raised. The Company found that with this charge they could not raise ore as cheaply as it could be imported from Spain, and they therefore aban- doned their project. At the same time there were thou- sands of willing men unemployed and in want." Land unused, capital waiting for investment, labour waiting for employment. Such are the natural fruits of the all-powerful veto of the lords of the soil.</p>
        <p>EPPS'S COCOA.—GRA.Trinn. AND Comvoarnro.—•• By a thorough knowledge of the natural laws which governithe operations of digestion and nutrition, and by a careful application of the fine properties of well-selected COCOA, Mr. Bpi:Rl has provided for our breakfast and supper a delicately flavoured beverage which may save us many heavy doctors' bills. It is by the judi,dous use of such articles of diet that a constitution may be gradually built up until strong enough to resist every tendency to disease. We may escape many a fatal shaft by keeping ourselves well fortified with pure blood and a properly nourished frame."—" Civil Service Gazette."—Made simply with boiling water or milk—Sold only in packets and pound tins, by Grocers, labelled— .' JAMES V 14.9 &amp; Co.. ltd.. Homceopathio Chemists,London." —Also makers of Epp, Coconine or Cocoa-Nib Extraet : A thin beverage of full flavour, now with many beneficially taking the place of tea. Its active principle being a gentle nerve stimulant, supplies the needed energy without uodul3, exciting the system,- o</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MT DEAR NELLII4</p>
        <p>The long ulster coat is coming to the front vtry Much again. It is remarkable chiefly for the im- mense width of its revers, which are invariably faced with handsome fur. For long journeys and open-air driving these paletots are delightful. For walking they are impracticable. The fur bolero or Eton, which is still much seen in Paris rinks, and the short basqued coat, are for business-like pur- poses! but the cape, of a more or less elaborate description, is the right thing for the carriage and for calls. Where possible cape and toque should correspond. This is not difficult to ensure, and gives a finished effect to the wearer, who may then Wear with these a quiet or not very new gown. and Bo escape criticism.</p>
        <p>A fashionalle travelling coat is what is termed a princess coat and is in fawn cloth fastened at the side with a fancy lacing and gilt knobs and velvet strings. There is a high stand up collar, which with the lapels is in checked silk and lace pleating.</p>
        <p>A very elegant walking costume, and one that is at once- brilliant and subdued in colouring, has that. The vigogne is Italian red with a perfectly plain skirt, and the very slightly full bodice has a vest and throatlet of oriental embroidery in which gold predominates. The collar is cut into the shape of leaves, one may almost say of a calyx roma(' the throat lined with maize satin, em- broidered and veined with red silk and gold thread. The trimming on corsage and sleeves is dark red velvet crossed with zigzags of narrow gold braid and with lines of the same on either side but not touching, and the pointed belt is made to match. The buttons are small ones.</p>
        <p>Hats are now worn very much tilted on the left.- side and the most fashionable are felt with beef- eater crowns and are very simply trimmed, there being instead of a band a bow at the left-side from which starts up a plume while round the hat resting on the brim of the hat as a simple drapery. A becoming hat is in crinkled satin, in a soft grey, and in a fashionable shape, curving away off the face at one side. It is trimmed with white plumes up from the left side from under a bow of B. suitable material or from under a buckle, or the lot -night be trimmed with a bit of light ieru lace draping the brim below on the buckle but showing the hat shape at the edge over the Lehead. At the left there can be be rosettes of lace and orange glace. Although the long Empire wrap is generally used for young children, the paletot shape is more fashionable for girls of ten to sixteen, and a use- ful model of this is of fawn cloth, with large brown horn buttons, and brown fur. The back is arranged in two box-pleats, the front buttons across to the left, and simulates a double-breast, and there are triple capes of cloth, simply turned up and stitched at the edge. The fur collar and cuffs are wadded, and lined separately, :and sewn on with as few Stitches as possible, otherwise the fur has a flat, hard effect. Each seam should be thoroughly pressed, and if beaver-backed cloth is employed, !ining is unnecessary, but a strip down each front great finish. If soft woollen frocks are worn, the sleeves are terribly troublesome to get off and on unless lined with silk, and if expense is a eon. eideration, the shot polonaise silks are admirable for the purpose. The black gown is indispensable in England, where it is permissible at so many functions, and gnerally becoming to the typical Englishwoman. 5-narisians favour the admixture of bright colour- but here the light neutral tints in silk, re- 've,2l with jet, are more in favour, or velvet in some deep rich tint which does not afford any B u.trtling contrast. One of these fashionable t oilettes of black vigogne, relieved with silver- Fey cloth, braided black and is a decidedly useful 'nuclei for ordinary wear: Dark brown, with pale triokw cloth, petunia, with mauve or green, or fattou, with fawn or antelope, are equally satis- art' 7. and the high cuirasse belt is delightfully on a slender figure. Your loving J.Ebrzaz.</p>
        <p>ITh "al TAUtvlresses say Critehley's " Sx.sucni 01,081 st unequalled for im; parting a beautiful finish to starch Wn;l Ea" 11. de" eilil" Cold or boiling water starch. Mr.—, Coru arora, Gl ' 8..., wife will not finish our linen without your .. -koss.' • I may ~i,,_ , .Prom Mrs.—, Bradford 3—Dear sir,—_ liil4 ' '“"' " 'n'ornt you that I have tried many so-called should be re-11 Glazes and Polishes but find none equal to yours." It used in every house. Sold at id, 3d., and 6d., by EZit , starch sellers. II not in stock will procure it. T. Blackburn, Proprietor.— A dot. itx.mx ,n. .--. ""v white boron Cement for Pottery,Poree- fp Chemist B.—Advt. ,</p>
