      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WOMEN'S CFI AT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CFI AT.</p>
        <p>Ace )rdiete to present arrangements, the Queen will arri ee it Ci ez on the afternoon of the 11th of next , all to. pie . rations for Her Majesty's receeeion, incerding guarus of honor, escorts. See., being similar to those oi lest year. Chniez is remarkably healthy. 4, well as pretty,but at present this particuler spot is ont visited by the comparatively few, who have learned : prefer its quietude to the gayer conditions of life Nice, and its freedom from the presence of cro gads ot health and amusement seekers has been one at the chief reasons of the selection of the town as the scene of the Queen's annual spring holiday, for complete rest com- bined with charming surroundings, are precisely the conditions which fulfil Her Majesty's ideal. Music-makine; folks were greatly honored by the Queen last year, and, needles to say, Madame Albani was among those most favored. Almost dilectly after her first appearance on the London stage, Madame Albani was commanded to Windsor to sing to the Queen, who professed herself delighted with her voice. Ever since that time the Sovereign has made a point of hear- ing the great singer continually, both in England and Scotland. Her Majesty is particularly fond of Scotch music, and and among her favourite songs may be mentioned " Charlie is my Darling," " Caller Herrin," " The Land o' the Leal," and " Auld Robin Grey." The latter Is often sung by some of the great cantatrices before the Queen, in preference to the modern songs of greater power and execution. Madame Patti still remains terribly &amp; pressed by her recent loss,and has,it is understood, arranged to spend some weeks quietly in Wales,ere she again resumes pro- fessional engagements. Craig-y -Nos, the Welsh home of the prima donna, is far more luxurious than any State residence in Europe,if we except some of the Russian Dalaces,but amidst all this luxury the Diva herself lives with sybarite simplicity. She seldom touches meat,ex- cept that which has been plainly roasted, and she attri- butes the long preservation of her voice in its pristine freshness, to her avoidance of pastry-made dishes. She is too,excessively a bstemious,and yet teetotalers cannot claim her for their own,for to every tumbler of water she drinks a teaspoonful of whiskey is added. At Craig- y-Nosea still kept up the almost absolete custom of ring- ing a curfew heleand at midnight when the mistress of the house is herself lying down to sleep, she presses an electric button, and a gong rings loudly the signal " Lights out "for the Diva has a nervous horror of fire. The Royal Humans Society, which was founded in 1774, has, according to the Secretary awarded no fewer than 30,000 medals to life-saving heroes. Among the most famous recipients of awards, appears the name of Alexander 1., Emperor of all the Russia, for saving one of his subjects from drowning. The youngest hero is Robert Knox, who last year rescued two hays from a watery grave. Among the women, Grace Darling's is of course, the most heroic act of all. Speaking of Grace Darling, the house in which the heroine was bcru, and the house in which she died, are still standing, and occupied, in the charming village of Bamburgh. She lies buried in the still churchyard, and for the third time since her interment in 1842, the canopy memorial above her ashes,has heen restored. The coast is a wild one, but there has been something strangly fatal in the fortunes of the tombs of one whose praises once rang through Europe. The great advance that has taken place in the methods of lighthouse illumination, is strikingly shown in a com- parison of Smeaton's light of 1759, with the present installation. The chandelier of Smeaton's far-famed tower in the Eddystone was lighted in 1759 by tallow candles of-67 modern standard candle power,unaided by any optical apparatus, while the power of the beam from the present tower is equal, to 80,000 candles The beacon light which claims to be the most power- ful in the world, however, is that at Penmere, in Finis- term. The new lighthouse is situated on the promontory of Penmare,where there already existed an old specimen illuminated by oil lamps, which was quite insufficient to protect the shipping on that dangerous coast. The tower supporting the lantern rives to a height or over 200 feet, and is constructed entirely of ieranite. At its base lies the engine house containing dynamos generating a cur- rent which produces a light of three million candle power while in foggy weather the engine works a powerful siren. The light is visible to mariners for five seconde, every half minute. In the daytime in clear weather the lighthouse is visible at a distance of 19 miles. At night its powerful rays have a range of 60 miles. It is a trite saying that not half the world knows how the other half lives.—Women in all grades of life like to display their charms, to the best advantage, and those who have ample means, and realise how hard it is to dress without spending money, often marvel how women in a lower social scale contrive to buy the dainty fashionable hats and gowns, in which they appear. It is managed in this way. In the majority of our large cities there is a depot, where misfits and garments that have been worn but are in perfectly good condition can be sold oe commission for a fair price. The buyers get a good go rntent cheap, and the sellers find&amp; slight return for the in,•lley they have spent. Women with elastic dress aliowances aeon tire of their gowns, cloaks, and headgear, and their less fortunate sisters, who have to keep up a respectable appearance upon small means are only too glad to take advantage of the opportunities offered them of acquiring the discarded garments at, frequently, considerably less that half price. The storm collar, in connection with the newest coats and capes, prepared for spring wear is happily con- spicuous by its absence, it place being taken by frills of pleated silk which stand out like quills upon the fretful porcupine. The arrangement is filled in with puffings of tulle, which makes a cloudy setting of whiteness for the face. In some instances the ends of the tulle are finished with lace, but more frequently they are left un- trimmed in a drift of waving snowiness. Belts, just now, are being supplied with'almost every style of costume,and these, often delightful additions to our toilette, bid fair to retain their popularity for some considerable time to come. We are though, no longer permitted to show the whole of the article. We must thread it in some way through the coat or blouse, with which it is worn. -Old newspapers sewn together and covered with tur- key red twill, or cheap cretorme,make very effective bed spreads for poor people, and moreover they are very warm. I remember an Etou master once telling me that he was out with the Eton volunteers, and a cold wind arose suddenly, and all the boys were shivering. Their master was in despair, for no shelter was available. At last be bethought himself of the warmth that there is in paper, and he stopped at a little booth in the village, bought up all the newspapers,and made every boy wrap one round his body underneath his uniform, with the re- sult that not a single volunteer in his company,took even the smallest chill that day. Prune Meringue.—Boil oise pint of rnilk,thicken it with the beaten yolks of two eggs, add two teaspoonfuls of sugar (granulated)and one cupful of prune pulp—bailed and mashed—and stir all together. Place in a glass dish pile upon it the whites of two eggs, beaten to a stiff froth, to which has been added one tableepoonful of granulated sugar and a few drops of vanilla. S3rve cold.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>" MADGE."</head>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
        <p>CEMENT WORKER'S NARROW ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>TOLD BY HIMSELF.</p>
        <p>A reporter of the " West Bent Advertiser " heard tha story of a narrow escape from being a cripple for life, from Mr. Arthur Court. a'young man, twenty-one years of age, residing at 12, Albert-terrace, Bwanscombe. This is Mr. Court's story : " I have been working for some years in what is called the stoke-bole, at Messrs. J. B. White and Son's cement factory, and in the year 18951 was suddenly seized with a pain in my feet and ankles. At first it was nothing to speak of, and I took no notice of it, but instead of get- ting better, it gradually got worse. I tried various remedies,,but these had no effect, and I visited a doctor, who gave me advice, but did not seem to do me any good. I got worse and worse, and used to hobble down to the works on sticks, until at last I was compelled to lay up altogether. I suffered intense pain, and it took me over half-an-hour to walk down the road, a distance of a hundred yards. I had to go upstairs on my hands and knees." .</p>
        <p>" Did not you seek any further advice?" " Yes," replied Mr. Court, " for boside3 rubbing my legs with various embrocations, I paid a visit to a Lon- don institution, and I was told I was suffering from muscular rheumatism, but did not experience a bit of good." " But you are all right now ?" "Yes,' am glad to say I am. After I had been suffer- ing for about a year,a fellow-workman spoke to me of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People,and told me that a friend of his at Hull,a member of the Shipwright's Society,who had suffered from paralysis for a long time, had been cured by them. The doctors had given him up as incurable,and as it was thought he could no lon- ger werk,he was nresented by the Society with a dona- tion of £5O. He is now at wcrk,and has been for some time. I determined to try the Pills. I took three of 'the Pills at a dose,and after thafirst box was finished I felt somewhat better, and persevered. As I continued taking the Pills, the pain gradually disappeared until it was gone altogether.</p>
        <p>AN EXPLANATIOIL—Dr. Williams' Pink Pills act directly on the blood, and thus it is that they are so famous among all classes of people, from the lowest to the highest in the land, for the cure of indigestion. uremia and rheumatism, scrofula, chronic erysipelas, and to restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health. They are alio a splendid nerve and spinal tonic, and thug have cured many cages of paralysis, locomotor ataxy, neuralgia, Sc. Vitus' dance and ner- vous headache. They are now obtainable of all chemists, and from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 15s. 9d., but are genuine only 'with full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People.</p>
      </div>
