        <head>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Imob-caps were accommodated on one side of the hall, and the men in suits of black corduroy and brass but- tons, which for so long have been to the wearers, the Occupied i t t seeda t st that Mr. must gave their services A meeting of this committee was held in the hind. eof Board Room at the Workhouse,on Friday morning, was by no means large Mr. G. Pilling presiied,and there were also present: they provided an exceptionally god evening's enter- h The pauperism number of in a: Vrtai bsr ttritis t hn Messrs. William Houghton J.P., A. J. Jolley A. " lament. Jolley, William Hickman, S. Davies, J. Billings, T. Theroceedino were presided over by Mr. A. J. p Jolley.- Amongst those of the general public present Halton, with Mr. 3, C. Sutton (clerk), Mr. S. Sutton wre Mrs. Clay, MEISSTS. George Pilling, G. Thompson,T. (assistant clerk), Mr. Tunstall (assistant overseer), B. Malkin, S. Davies (members of the Board of Gear- and Mr. Forster (assistant overseer at Penketh.) diens), Mr. C. H. Ward (workhouse master), Miss Ward THE ASSESSMENT OF A BRICKFIELD. (s,,,noohnistress), and the Rev. H. V. Pigot (chaplain), Mr. PEMBERTON. builder, appealed on behalf of Messrs Mrs. Ward, the matron, wee prevented from attending Mainwaring and Pemberton, against the assessment of through illness. The first item was a musical drill exer- land near Wash-lane, Latchford, which was assessed at cise by a number of the school children at the Work- L3O gross,and £27 IN. rateable—Mr.Pemberton pointed house. They were ha charge of Miss Ward who accom- out that bricks were being made on that land but none 'waled them on the piano, and the manner in which the were being sold.—The Clerk : But they will be ultim- children gave their performance showed that consider- ately sold,and then there will be a beneficial occupation, able pains had heen taken in coaching them. A violin —Mr. Tunstall : You might just as well argue that a lioio entitled " Tarantella" was afterwards very cleverly farmer should not pay rates, because he grew crops to executed by Mr. Walter Brough. At a later stage of feed his cattle. Messrs. Mainwaring and Pemberton's the proceedings he c3ntributeci a secvid solo " Born- land is at present assessed as aricultural land, and con- ance et Bolero." Miss Hannah Brough's sweet con- sequently only pays half of the current rates. If the tralto voice was heard to great advantage in the land is re-assessed as a brickyard, you will have to pay monqS " Deane " and ' The Better Land," both of the rates in full. (Laughter.) The Chairman -: In that which were exceedingly well sung. Miss Alice Mor- case your rates will go up.—lt was ultimately decided rell was very successful in her recitations. The two that Mr. Owen should visit the land, and report to the pieces selected "Two little feet " and .4 The hut Hymn." committee, were both very suitable for the audl -md . . . . ....</p>
        <p>THE VALUER' REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr-Owen in his monthly statement reported that since the last'meeting he had made the following inspections and valuations.ln the township of Warrington:—Messrs Monks, Hall, and Company,Limited, additions to works and new offices, Atherton's Quay ; the alterations and the new offices are not yet in a complete state for assess- ment, and there is no beneficial occupation of them. I will bring these valuations forward in due course ; Mr. David Piekton, stables,Heath Side. These are assessed at gross £42 103., rateable £36 ; Mr. Pickton had lately purchased them, and they were new let, and were pre- vious to his purchase occupied by Mr. E. Isherwood at £32 103.. the owner paying the taxes. The assessment should therefore be reduced to gross £26, rateable nz. In the township of Latchford :—The Britannia Soap Works, Knutsford-road, Latchford, gross £25, rateable £2l. In the township of Rixton :—Mr. J. A. Hobson, Rye Park House, Rixton. NJ.r. J. A. Hobson tskes Rye Park House and a cottage adjoining with the garden and buildings from the Ship Canal Company at an annual rent of £3O, under an agreement to keep the buildings and fixtures in order and he lets off the house with cer- tain portion of garden and buildings and occupies the cottage himself. The assessment now stands gross £4O, rateable £34. Seeing that repairs had to be undertaken by the tenant the assessment should be on the house, gross £25, rateable £2l; and on the cottage gross £lO, rateable ES 10s. In the township of Haydock pool, St. Helens and South Lancashire Railway. The new railway has now been assessed in the adjoining township of St. Helens. Mr. Sutton and he (the valuer) had considered the matter and had decided to suggest that for the present an assessment should be put upon the railway similar to that in St. Helens, which they understood was now under appeal. If the appeal was successful the amount could then be dealt with accord- ingly.lf the committee decided on this course the assess- ment would be 5962 yards, (3 miles, 31 chains) at £125 per mile.gross,2lo6 per milo,ratoable. Gross £423 rate- able £359 103.</p>
        <p>_lance, a... 1 were greatly appreciated. In addition, Miss Morrell contri- buted a song entitled " Do as they do in England," and was deservedly applauded. Mr. D. Fanshaw gave an effective rendering of such old favourites as " The Vil- lage Blacksmith " and " Off to Philadelphia." Mr. Baker sang, " The Trysting Tree "ire his usually good style. Mr. Grounds delighted the audience with his humorous rnusicol sketches, and ventriloquial entertain- ment. His excellent vocalisation of songs and his ability as a ventrilnquist make him a first-class entertainer. Mr. T. Brough discharged the duties of accompanist with taste and skill. At the close of the concert Mr. A. J. JOLLEY referred to the kindness of Mr. Thompson in arranging the con- cert, whose desire was to introduce and support a sym- pathetic feeling in the administration of the poor law. ge (A,. Jolley) had bad many opportunities of seeing Mr. Thompson's work on the Board of Guard ians,and all his colleagues recognised the earnest desire he had shown to do everything that was possible for the wel- fare of those whom he was responsible for. Oa every possible occasion he had been the champion of rational reform. (Applause.) Mr. S. Dams said he could not let the opportunity pass without proposing a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Thompson. and to the ladies and gentlemen who had so kindly assisted him in providing the excellent entertain- ment which they had just listened to. (Applause) Mr. PILLING seconded the proposition. Mr. FLNSHAVI responded on behalf of the artistes. Mr. THOMPSON said since he became a member of the Board, he had tried to servo the ratepayers, and whilst assisting the members of the Board to carry cut their duties, he had endeavoured to bring a little brightness and sunshine into the institution. (Hear, hear.) He pro :ceded to give some good advice to the inmates, and asked them to refrain from being peevish and unfriendly with their fellow inmates. He ha 1 received letters from five guardians. viz. : Messrs. William Houghton, J.P., Mr. John Dignum, Mr. J. Clarke, Mr. Billings, and the Rev. J. H. Riddette,apolegising for their non-attend- ance. In conclusion he referred to the absence of tne Mation,to the work done by Mr. Ward, and Miss Ward, and then proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman. Mr. MALKIN seconded, and the resolution was carried. Mr. JOLLEY having replied, the meeting dispersed.</p>
        <p>Nfr. A. J. JOLLEY moved, and Mr. ii3CIEMAN seconded the adoption of the report which was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CONCERT AT TIIE WARRINGTON WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT TIIE WARRINGTON WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>With the commendable object of enabling the in mates of the Workhouse to spend a pleasant hour, add to relieve what must be a dull and monoto- nous time to all who find a home within the Work- house walls, Mr. Thompson, a member of the Board of Guardians, kindly arraaged for a concert which was given in the Dining Hall at the Workhouse on Thursday last week.On the initiative of Mr. Thom- pson, concerts have been given on previous occasions, and these have been greatly appreciated by the in- mates. There would he about 150 present at the concert on Thursday evening, and they formed a , somewhat unusual concert audience. The women in their dresses of blue check material, and white</p>
      </div>
