      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Model 81.</head>
        <p>Model 81.</p>
        <p>I "ENGLAND IN DANGER FROM TRADES UNIONS."</p>
        <p>(BY MR. 11. WRlmim.)</p>
        <p>LONDON, January 15th.—An eminent person, who was lunching recently with Mr. Gladstone, asked him what was the greatest danger threatening Great Britain—was it France, Germany, Russia, or America ?'' " None of them," was the reply of the great English statesman "The only danger I fore- see is from the trades utions and their attendant strikes." _ . .</p>
        <p>Fl—linOw nothing as to the truth of the above cablegram, but it appears in the American press. Truth, or not the truth, it voices a feeling that seems to have grown with remarkable rapidity amongst a large portion of the English press and a not inconsiderable portion of the English manufac- turers. Of one thing we may be sure, England's position as a manufacturing nation is in this re- spect anything but unique. Germany and France during their short careers as manufacturing nations have known something of "trades unions and their attendant strikes." America also could a tale un- fold,and even Russia has had a slight experience. The experience of the British trade unionist has so far, happily, differed from his continental and American brothers inasmuch as his troubles have not had to be settled by calling out the military. ' One would think that the men of light and lead- ing did not trouble themselves about the past his- tory of the people, and were not aware of the mighty struggle that has hen to be car- ried on by the mill and factory workers of Lancashire and Yorkshire ever since the steam engine became a factor in the life of the worker. Even to-day, and after all this struggling, no sane man can say that anything more than the merest subsistence wage is paid to the factory "hand," and certainly no one can say that a living wage is paid. During a conversation I had recently with some of the leading unionists of this city of San Franciso, I advised that they throw up their hands and allow their employers to work out their own sweet will, that is, allow the pet of the political economist, competition, to take all that it could and would. One of the men, a deep thinking and slow speaking Norwegian, said "Well Whitham,that's all right as far as it goes, but you lose sight of the fact that strength and organization once lost can never be regained,and that these two features lost to the em- ployee make a despot of the employer. No man is fitted by nature to have unlimited power in his hands; as a proof of this look at the condition of the worker in those countries where government has unlimited control over the lives and actions of their peoples." My idea had been that if once the brake was taken off, the avarice of capital would rush the labour question tn a head, and that a very short time would suffice to bring about the social revolution every one seems to see ahead, Poverty in a nation can crawl along for ages without find- ing any relief. The trouble is not by any means that trade unionism drives trade away by encourag- ing strikes, but that it is so limited in its area, and does not succeed in its endeavours to become uni- versal. I remember once being told by a Warring- ton employer that "he certainly had no wish to reduce wages, and would not, if we could persuade the German workmen to form a union and get higher wages," Even international trade unionism would not cover the ground as may be seen by giv- ing a little thought to the success now attending the efforts of the American manufacturers. Yet, granting all this, the two watchwords of trade unionists must be the two that have given them what they have of political freedom: " Organize and Educate." A well organized and educated trade union needs no leader. It needs a mouthpiece, a steady and sober-minded executive to carry out its decisions. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Alfred Yarrow, a member of the great ship building firm of Yarrow and Co., has been pay- ing America a visit of some months duration. In a letter to the "Times " he says, that "if the striking English engineers would select a committee of four men to visit America, inspect the ship building plants there and make a report to the strikers he would gladly defray the expenses of their trip." Mr. Yarrow's object in making this proposal is to de- monstrate to the strikers that American iron and steel workers are paid better wages than men working in English yards, owing to the fact that " they do far more than proportionately better work,""lt seems, 'says Mr.Yarrow,"to be the rule for each man in America to do as much as he can, while at home, in England,every one is afraid of injuring his fellow workman, and does no more than he has to." Mr. Yarrow writes fairly, and in moderation, and so far as I have seen truthfully,concluding with these words,—" America will soon take first place in the world unless England bestirs herself and shakes off the attitude of indifference assumed thirty years ago when she was at the head in en- gineering industries." Mr. Yarrow's position as an employer is sufficient justification for he remarks he makes. He looks only to results an an owner of capital, and as an exploiter of labor. I have quoted Mr. Yarrow because, in my opinion,his words cover a truth he himself may not be aware of. Had .hi made his enquiries from a purely ethical standpoint he would have found that in America the workmen in all the large concerns are made up ala ost to a man of the foreign born. He would have found that the traditions that grow up in a boy during appren- ticeship and control his actions more or less during and through life are altogether wanting. He would have found that the American workmen's motto was " every man for himself, and the devil. take the hindmost." A firm believer in the "survival of the fittest," he deems himself the fittest in all cases, and brooks no interference on the part of his shop- mates in his mad rush for the almighty dollar. I have worked in a shop where a very large per cen- tage of the men, and there were thousands em- ployed, had no other object than to get hold of a certain amount of money with which to clear out, and sail tor their own European home, and I know that this state of things obtains through the whole country. The English workman is not idle, as Mr. Yarrow would imply, any more than his employer is idle. In America every man . is at his post at 7 a.m. The employer, his clerks and the foreman are all on deck when the i whistie blows. It is not so n England. More than this, in England, unless things have altered within the last ten years, when a man has given the years of his apprenticeship and the best years of his after life,he is not discharged because old age is creeping over him; an old man has a chance. It is is not so in America. I have never. yet met the firm that either respects a man for his long services, os con- tinues to employ a m.as for mere sentiment's sake. The rule is to get rid of old men, and to employ these who are neither men nor boys, but pay them boys' wages. I know two very large concerns in California that have recently discharged nearly all their employees, and now employ only big single lads. It is this knowledge that makes me think that the labor question in America is more intense more acute than elsewhere. The populations are isoasseg in character, hence there are neither pat- riotic motives, nor love of home to keep back the baser selfish motives that are. gradually, yet surely sapping at the very foundations of political and labour questions. The ruling idea is to get hold of the dollar. I should think I am fairly within the mark when I say that fully ninety per cent of the Pacific Coast population invest from Is to 4s every month in lottery tickets.. .Ad. ded to this we hay.s in San Francisco and its vicinity races all the year round, and there is no mistake about them being well attended. My conclusions are that men work, and are more or less willing to work for short periods of time in order to gain a determined sun?, and bet and gamble on every conceivable thing to get money without working for it, and that thereby the American masses are demoralized to an extent not known in any other country. There . is nothing in this for English employers to be envious about,but they want a free hand. They want to be allowed to do away with all the fundamental features of trade un- ionism.l need not recapitulate.what these are.l may just refer to one that seems to be a great trouble. Piece-work has always been a knotty subject. It carries on the face of it an appearance of fair play. It seems to the outF ider, to the inexperienced,quite enough to say that when a man knows how much he is going to receive for a given amount of work, helms a sufficient incentive to encourage him in</p>
        <p>Iwork, but what the fullest efforts of a first-class workman have been first gauged, and then a tner • made it a subject for investigation know this is n lt so. Take a machine shop or a large w•ggon wot There is not a piece of work given out as ta,k his efforts to earn a fair wage. And that the well- qualified, like the ill qualified are fairly dealt with. Those who have had a little experience,or who have subsistence wage has been forced on the men for the same amount of work as task work. I have seen men have four machines alloted to them. As oppor- tunity offered I have seen them take three and four more, and then earn a big wage, and I have seen in the short space of twelve months three reductions forced on them, leaving them with an increased number of machines to follow, and the original small wage to console. Of course, the general outcome has been that the man would have his original number of machines and but a labourer's wags. In all these cases the nationalities of the men were, Irish, Turks, Armenians, Swedes, and Germans. No wonder that workmen fight shy of piece work. They know that their employers are bat the more tools of the capital they are sup- ' posed to own, and that both capital and capitalist I must bend beneath the yoke of the unreasonable competition that is grinding us all into the dust.</p>
        <p>MR. KIER HARDIE AT WARRINGTON. Wycliffe Ball was well filled on Monday evening when Mr. Kier Hardie, of the Independent Labour Party, editor of " The Labour Leader" and formerly M.P., for West Ham, delivered an address on " Lessons from the Engineers "Lockout." Mr.Green,of Manchester,presidei He referred to the fact that he was one of the engineers who was locked out, and he said that in consequence of the active part he took as secretary of one of the en- gineers' committees, injunctions had) been issued by the employers that he must not start work in any federated shop. Since the engineers restarted work wages had been slightly reduced, and a further reduction might be expected in the summer. They might depend upon it that the victory of the employers would effect other branches of industry, and workers would probably sink into that state of degradation which existed at the be- ginning of the century. Trades Unionism had accom- plished much, but it had reached its apex, and politics would have to be discussed and trades unionists sent to Parliament if the condition of the vorkers of this coun- try was to be improved. (Applause.) Mr. KIER HARDIZ, who had a cordial reception, at the outset of his address traced the various issues raised dur- ing the progress of the recent engineer's dispute. Deal- ing with the argument of the employers that they could not afford to pay the old rate of wages, Mr. Hardie pointed out that Colonel Dyer was a director of the firm of Armstrong. Whitworth and Co. In 1896, the dividend paid by that firm was 30 per cent., which did not look much like bard pressure on the employers by the workers. The labourers received 17s. 10th a week, and yet with such a big dividend the directors wished them to believe that they could not afford to give more. The struggle was now over, and what were the lessons ? The time had come for a closer organisation of the forces of labour than there was at the present time. In like manner the time had come for a federation of all unions to which each union would be expected to belong. The engineering federation had the practical sympathy and support of every section of employers throughout the country, and unless the workers were wise in time and knit their forces together, before they were twelve months older, some other big union in the country would have to fight the same battle as the engineers had been engaged in. But supposing they had got all the unions en one side and the employers on the other h ove far were they towards the solution of the tabour problem? In every conflict between capital and labour, capital stood to win if only its owners had the courage to go on to ",r1 end. During the lockout he knew of cases pers.. ,'y where children died literally from lack of that support which if their parents had been able to give it them would have kept them alive. The worker during a strike or lockout had always to face poverty, sickness, and very often hunger, and the certainty of getting into debt. The employers had no suoh fear,and so the battle was unequal because the employers could always reckon on having hunger as an ally fighting on their side. There was a third party to the dispute,and that was the State. They were continually hearing that they had a democra- Itic form of Government. If the State was ruled by the II common people it ought to follow that in a great dispute like the one they had been passing throueli,all the forces lof the State should be employed on the side of the people. But when blacklees were being employed and Itrades unionists were trying to picket them,on what side were the forces of the State then ? The constabulary armed in some cases with sabres and revolvers, and mounted in some instances, were there to pro- tect the blackleg against the trades unionist. He held strongly that the State was bound to protect the blackleg if he desired to go to work, but he claimed equal freedom for the trade unionist, and if the State was bound to interfere and prevent unioni its from hindering blacklegs from going to work it ought to have interfered with Colonel Dyer and his friends, for preventing trades unionists from going to work. (Ap- plause). Because the works belonged to a number of capitalists the State would not interfere. The workers must try to change the ownership of the works. The great lesson of the lockout was the need to change the ownership of the land and capital from private into public property. That was socialism, and under social- ism those who performed useful service would receive Itheir reward, but those who were loafers in the c 3M- Imunity, whether at street corners or in palaces, would ' have to take the loafers reward,instead of living by the labour of others. Let them carefully consider what socialism meant. Socialists did not propose a general dividing of the wealth c,f the country, nor a general sharing out, as was often incorrectly stated. The speaker proceeded to explain what economic socialism was,and said socialism offered freedom to the working classes. Turning to the question of how it was to be brought to pass,he said every reform had been brought about by getting people to believe in it,and as soon as a sufficient number of people believed in socialism they would find a way of brining it to pass. It was coming about every day they lived. Every extension of muni- cipal enterprise was helping to bring socialism nearer. lEvery development of the co-operative movement was paving the way for socialisrn,and every man and woman ' who was converted to a belief in socialism was bring- ing its realisation one step nearer. The whole laws of this csuntry were framed and administered for the pro- tection of property. Socialists would gradually change the current of law until it protected humanity as being more valuable than property. (Hear, hear.) Socialists did not change their programmes. They did not expect to win all at once, but every election brought them a stage nearer, and they saw nothing te discourage them. It was just as reasonable to ask the Liberal Party to go over to the Conservatives as to ask the Independent Labour Party to go with the Liberals. He wondered why workingmen did not begin to see the game that was being played upon them by the two parties. Under the present parties labour was always out of power no matter who went in. His advice to them was to form their own party. (Hear, hear.) They felt th it the younger men in their midst would not be so easily taken in as their fathers had been. When the British workmen had learned to fight shoulder to shoulder—as nee had done during strikes and lockouts—for the return of their own members to Parliament, the days of strikes would be over. (Applause.) The lesson they learned from the engineers' lockout was thet the workers required to sink their differences and unite as they had never done be- fore. They still required to have in Parliament, on every Town Council and County Council, men who would not allow the cause of labour to seller. He would advise them to join a labour party which would be able to fight the cause of labour,and which would not delude the workers and leave them in the Position in which they found themselves to-day. (Loud ap- plause) Several questions were afterwards put by the audience, and answered by Mr. Kier needle. In answer to a ques- tion with reference to Home Rule, Mr. Hardie ;mid he was a Herne Ruler from principle but n)t from expedi- ency. With reference to the attitude of the Liberal piety towards Rome Rule,hesaidwtohaeirie was not nt'lleltaistins;al: 1,-acier at the Liberal Party who in the forefront of the provarnme.R was certain that the Liberal Party would shelve Home Rule, and that the Irish people would be advised to vote Tory at the next election. The Independent Labour Party was prepared to deal with the Irish question, but se long as the Irish people emtinued to vote for any man who called him- self Liberal, the Irish could not expect them to make advances.</p>
        <p>Dr. Kerusies,the headmaster of a large German school, has stated hs p-rsonal experience of the conditions which influence the working capacity of his pupils The best work of the week is done on Monday and Tuesday (after the Sunday holiday). Oa Tuesday afternoon the work bac already begun to deteriorate. Re stronc.tly ad. vices a holiday on Wednesday, in order to check the mental exhaiption,and to raise the standard of work for the second half of the week. The first two heurs of the day produce the best work; after the mid-day meal there is a marked fallina off.</p>
