      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>FAMOUSI</head>
        <p>FAMOUSI</p>
        <p>FAMOUS'</p>
        <p>TUE EXA M [NEE.</p>
        <p>FEBRUARY 11th, 1898.</p>
        <p>THE fourth session of the Parliament. fourteenth Parliament of QUEEN VICTORIA'S reign was opened on Tuesday. It would be a mistake to say, that this sessbn of Parliament has been opened under auspicious circumstances, for there has rarely been a time in recent years when so much doubt prevailed as to what the next few months might bring forth. The QUEBN'S SPEECH opens with the usual declarations that " rela- tions with other powers continue to be friendly," but it does not add the fact that there are elements in the international situation which may at any time produce undesirable results. Twelve months ago that political problem which was generally described as the " Eastern Question" or the "Eastern Crisis" filled the mind of statesmen with forebodings as to the future, but, as a matter of of fact, there are now two Eastern questions—a New Eastern question and a Far Eastern question. The Concert of Europe was at one time lauded to the skies, in certain quarters, as the great instrument for settling in a pacific manner all the . outstanding points of difference between the leading European nations, but it is now evident that the Concert has fallen into disrepute even amongst those who used to describe it as a" Federation of Nations " or as the " European Areopagus." It all events, one thing is now clear—the Concert of Europe does not settle anything. The original Eastern question is still a festering and dangerous wound in the political system of Europe. The island of Crete which the Powers especially took under their care is in a deplorable condition, The Chris-- tian inhabitants who, but for the intervention of the soldiers and ironclads of the Christian Gevernments, would now probably have been a free people, are suffering the greatest hardships from starvation and exposure. Our own Govern- ment has, it is needless to repeat, played .a most lamentable part in the repression of the Greek and Cretan Christians, and, if we may judge from the reference to the subject in the Qualm's Speech it does not hold out much hope of improvement in the future. The question of an autonomous 'government for the Island of Crete has, we are 'told, occupied the attention of the Powers, and it is hoped that the obstacles in the way will "before long be surmounted." About the Far Eantern Question as to w hich men's minds have been so keenly exercised lately there is, singular to say, no reference at all in the QUEIL'a Speech. This is, no doubt, to be accounted for by the fact that the Government had nothing to say that would afford any gratification to its own sup- porters. It is now clear beyond doubt that L 4rd SALISBURY has won no more diplomatic honours in the Far East than he has done nearer home. His efforts so far as China is concerned have been directed towards securing the opening of ports for the commerce of all nations, an aim which has had the approval of all parties in this country. But what is the result as shown by the debate on the Address? Russia and Germany and probably France have obtained some solid advantages in China,but the modest request put forward by Lord SALISBURY for the opening of Ta-Lien-Wan has been. rejected or deferred., The utmost concession that. he has been able to obtain seems to beithat when. Russia wants this port as a termination for its new railway across Siberia it will throw it open tce the commerce of the world. What MINISTER has obtained, is not a cor C hina but a. promise from Russia. ful, therefore, that there is gruni but deep, amongst the Tories amongst the Tory jingos. The Torii or other do not regard Russian pro"" violable sanctity. They remember has at one time or another, made •, in connection with ita advances</p>
        <p>our</p>
        <p>thling,</p>
        <p>especially [O3 somehow</p>
        <p>PNIAM</p>
        <p>ssion from it wonder- , not loud</p>
        <p>and</p>
        <p>113eS</p>
        <p>as of in- that Russia promises thtt</p>
        <p>various</p>
        <p>towards</p>
        <p>he</p>
        <p>Indian frontier in Central Asia, and that it has tot always been able to strictly adhere to those promises. Then there is the very suggestive case of the port of Batoum in the Black Sea. Batoum was, according to the Treaty of Berlin, to be an open port after Russia had taken it over, but Batoum is now as much closed to out- side traffic as any other Muscovite har- bour. These remembrances are not of a reassuring character. After the open threats of war by the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER and other MINISTERS, the Tories had quite naturally • expected something more substantial than a Russian promise and they are correspondingly dis- appointed. In regard to the probable domestic legislation the Queen's SpeechEcontains no announcement that was not expected. There is a promise of a measure for the organization of a system of local government in Ireland substantially similar to that which, within the last few years, has been established in Groat Britain. This extension of local government in Ireland is long over-due. It is now many years since Lord RANDOLPH CHURCHILL and other leading Tories, premised that, in matters of local government, Ireland should be treated on the principle of "similarity and simultaneity" but that promise has been broken over and over again. The details of the new local government bill will be closely scrutin- ised, for Irishmen have many reasons for b&amp;tig Suspicious in matters of this kind. Another im- portant measure will bo that "for facilitating the creation of municipalities in the administrative county of London," as the avowed object of Lord .AI,ISBURY in promoting this bill is to distroy the , London County Council, or, at all events,to reduce it from its commanding position as a representa- tive institution ror the whole of London outside the old Corperation. The London County Coun- cil has, as a matter of fact, been of far too Radical</p>
        <p>character to suit the Tories and the numerous monopol:sts who are drawing a vast amount of unearned wealth from the metropolis.</p>
        <p>TIIE sufferings of impoverished Irish aetress. residents in the West of Ireland received the attention of Parlia- ment on Wednesday in connection with an amend- ment to the Address moved by Mr. M. Dori TT. Mr. DAVITT, Mr. DILLON and other Irish members found a reasonable ground of complaint in the entire omission from the QUEMN'S Speech of any reference to the need for relief of these poor Irish peasants, while such matters as the decay of the sugar industry in theWestlndiea were Prominently mentioned. The ropily of Mr.GERALD BALFOCrIt,the Chief Secretary for Ireland, while not of an entirely unsympathetic nature can hardly be described as satisfactory by those who know the urgency of the case. Mr. BALFOUR admits that there is distress in the Wait of Ireland, but he appears to find consolation in the fact that the country is not desolated by famine to the extent that it was in 1317. If there b 3 any truth in the accounts that have been published, it is clear that . what the Irish peasants want more than anything ,‘ else is immediate relief. It may be true enough ' that remedies of a permanent character are needed if there is not to be a recurrence o`..' these perods , of distress, but the mere recognition of th tt fact will not tide the starving people over the present time of suffering. But it is just this immediate relief which the Irish SECRETARY is unwilling to provide except through the ordinary medium of ,the Poor Law and accompanied by sth-li con- , ditions and restrictions as will probably deprive it of nearly all its value.</p>
        <p>As was to be expected, the motion of Councillor</p>
        <p>Robrts in favour of inquiring into the county borough question was adopted unanimously. That Warrington will co oner or later become a county</p>
        <p>b)rough may, we suppose, be taken for granted. If population and rateable value continue to increase at the present rate it is not at all likely that War- rincon will consen:-. to %tire much longer in any subordinate capacity to the County Council. Prac-</p>
        <p>tieehy the only argument against this question be- ing faced sooner rather than later, is the fact that the Corporation has already so many irons in the fire. But this is an argument that cannot be over- looked. Th number of uncompleted projects the Corporation has now in hand is positively formid- able. There are the Bridge-street and other improvements the costliness of which was referred to at the Council meeting. Then there are the police buildings which cannot be long deferred. There is the small-pox hospital. Not much has been said about that lately, but if a case or two of small-pox were to breqk out it would have to be undertaken at panic haste and at panic Cost. There is the laying out of the new park and recreation grounds, and it may here be said that the condition in which such proposed recreation grounds as that off Orford-lane is left is not creditable to the Corporation. There is the Proposed abattoir in connection with which a depu- tatim visited St. Helens yesterday. These are all matters which,so to speak, lie close at hand. Later on there will be such tough subjects to tackle as the widening of the bridge, the development of electricity,and some other undertakings that could be mentioned. All of these are important and desirable in themselves, but they will certainly involve a considerable tax upon the resources of the Corporation and the energy and time of its leading officials.</p>
        <p>Some figures which were handed to the reporters</p>
        <p>at she Town Council meeting, but which were not submitted to no Council—perhaps because the ncreases of salaries passed through 80 easily— are of interest as they show a rapid growth in the business done by the Gas Depart- went of the Corporation. In the last three Cid a half years there has been a total increase in the gas produced of 43,144,600 feet, which at the net selling price of 2s 10d per 1,000 feet amounts to £6,112 3. The "penny-in-the-slot" meter system seems to have met with an extraordinary amoun of favour from gas consumers. The number of these Meters in use on March 31st, 1894, was 15, but on the 31st December, 1897, there were 2,850. The amount of copper collected from the slot meters is represent•A by the following figures :—March 31st, 1894,-£1 7s ; March 315t,1895,.£178 Os 61; March 31st, 1896, £1,139 14s 2d; March 31st, 1897, £2,391 98 31, and it is estimated that the total at the end of March this year will be about £3,400. It will be seen,t herefore,that the officials of the Gas Commit- tree have to deal with a weighty mass of the cur- d Coin of the realm. The number of accolnts Invered to customers using the ordinary meters :3';':114 522 in 1894 and 4,639 in 1897, so that there is an increase in the use of ordinary meters in spite of the fact that the slot meters are so much in vogue. The money passing through the hands of the cashier Department in payment of accounts due to the for the year ending 31st March, 1897, amounted to .£46,000, and for wages £lO,OOO.</p>
        <p>While one is dealing with figures relating to the Gas Department one might as well enumerate a few which will net be quite such pleasant reading for ratepayers ,though they do not,of couree,in any way</p>
        <p>...-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <p>reflect upon the present Gas Committa or its officials. They are taken from Parliamentary pa- pers which give a variety of information with re- spect to gas undertakings in all parts of the coun- try. It appears from these that the debt upon t!‘e Warrington gas undertaking in the shape or loam and the captalised value of annuities amounts to the enormous sum of £211,452. The heaviaess of this debt is seen by a comparison with the Widnes Corporation Gas Works in connection with which the amount borrowed is only £65,196. At . Widnes the Gas Department has an income of £21,097 from 181,287,000 feet of gas,while at Warrington gas con- sumers have paid £53,418 for 267,744,900 feet of gas. It will be seen from these figures that Widnes gets its gas at a considerably cheaper rate than War- rington. Nor does there seem to be any very bright prospect ot Warrington gas being cheapened to any material extent for along time to come. The gas undertaking here is hopelessly burdened with its heavy debt, which is,we suppose, mainly the result of the very peculiar bargain that was made when the Gas Works was bought by the Corporation.</p>
        <p>From a little pamphlet that has been sent us we notice that some very interesting and useful experi- ments in potato and tomato cultivation were Carried out last year at the Cheshire County Coun- cil's Agricultural and Horticultural School, Holmes Chapel. l'he fullest particulars are given of the seed and manure used,and of the methods of culture adopted, together, of course, with the results in the shape of crops. No better work than this could be carried on for the benefit of an agricultural county such as Cheshire. The report on the potato experi- ments is signed by Mr. G. S. Gordon, B.Sc. and that on tomato culture by Hr. W. Nield, F.R.H.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>DEATH OF DR. WFALL, WARRINGTON. A NOTABLE CAREER.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF DR. WFALL, WARRINGTON. A NOTABLE CAREER.</p>
        <p>About 6 o'clock on Tuesday night the death of Dr D.Cbamhers M' Fall, brigade surgeon, lieutenant- colon3l M S.R.P.,took place at his residence in Folly lane,Warrington.The deceased gentleman was up to the end of last year medial officer in charge of the Bth and 40th depot, .stationed at Warrington. Dr. M`Fall held the appointment at the Orford Barracks for about 16 years, bat owilig to failing health be had to retire at the end of last year. His condition then gave the family great anxiety, but since he seemed to improve. A. low weeks ago another change for the worse took place, and despite every medical attention he commenced to sink, and his death was not unexpected. Dr.:affairsi career n connection with the Army Medical service has been both long and interesting, lie first saw service as surgeon in the Baltic fleet, and while holding an appointment on James Watt passed through the Crimean war. Leaving the navy, Dr. M•Fall became connected with the 87th Royal Irish Fusiliers, the 34th Foot, known as the Border Regiment, and the 80th Royal Irish Fusi- liers, and with those regiments he served twenty years in India. He was actively engaged through the terrible scenes attending the Indian mutinnand for his services there was decorated. During his 20 years' work in India Dr. M‘Fall had to face at least three serious outbreaks of chole:a, and his admir- able efforts in helping to stamp out the epidemics were several times recognised and mentioned in the War Office despatches. Leaving India, Dr. M'Fell practically finished his active service with the army, and settled down to the routine work connected with several depOts. For several years he was in the Straits Settlements, Hong Kong, and then followed periods of office in Sandgate (Kent) and Norwich, where he had charge of the sth Royal Irish Lancers, the 21st 11asars, and the 2nd Dra- g .cin Guards clarity.; the time they were stationed there. Finally Dr, M`Fall was promoted to the Warrington depOt, where he concluded his services with the Army Medical Staff. Me deceased gentle- man was 66 years of age. He leaves a widow and three sons, all of age. Mrs. M•All is known to the literary world as Madame &amp;act' Grand,under which nom-de-plume she has succersfully published several remarkable novels, the most notable, " The 'Heavenly Twins" and "The Beth Book," being two of the most taiked-of volumes of their years. Dr. M'Fall was a man of very retiring disposition, and avoided as much as possible all publicity. He leaves behind him but meagre details and very few papers bearing upon a long and active career, and many of the deceased gentleman's varied associations will never be made public.</p>
        <p>The." Daily Chronicle •' says :—" Surgeon Lieut.- Colonel McFall,vvhose death took place at Warring- ton on Tuesday last, was, of course, the husband of the lady who prefers to be known as "Madame Sarah Grand." For some time past matrimonial incom- patibility has kept husband and wife apart, but despite this, and a shrewd suspicion that he figured not too favourably in some of his wife's books, the deceased officer was a reader and admirer of them, and had them all in a place of honour on his shelves. One of his sons, also in the army, was married a few weeks ago ; and another,Mr.Chambers McFall, is a journalist and illustrator.</p>
        <p>THE PARR HALL P-OPITLAR, CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Another of these concerts was given in Parr Hall, Warrington, on Saturday evening. There was a moderate attendance, and the concert also was of a medium character taking its predecessors as a stan- dard. One of the most popular of the artistes en- gaged, Mr. Walter Lawley, was unable to appear, but he sent a good substitute in Mr. Hanson. The great draw of the evening was intended to be the Brothers Benson,a humorus and musical trio. These gentlemen were of a type with which the Parr Hall Concerts are not familiar, their talents being of a music hall order, and their get up ala pierrot, About the best item they gave was the opening duet " Pussy's courtship," the feline caterwauling of the two "singers" deserving praise fonts realistic tone. Other items given by the Benson Brothers were "The harmmonophonic band,"and"Musical eccentri- cities," neither of which were so clever as the first, partaking more of the ridiculous. Mr. John Harri- rison was by far the most successful of the singers, and must rank as one of the many good baritones the popular concerts have brought before the Warrington public. His interpretation of ''The Desert" (Emanuel) was both vigorous and drama- tic, well deserving the encore which followed. Again in "The Wandering Minstrel," Mr. Harri- son's trained vocalisation was much admired. The contralto Miss Alice Whittaker is possessed of a not untuneful voice, and her singing of "In the Chimney Corner" and" Rory Harlin" wae tasteful. Mies Kilby did not seem in very good voice, but achieved a fair measure of success. As a humorist Mr. Lindon Wyatt contrived to amuse his hearers on each occasion. His selection" The Irish Band," was full of variety and clever musical mimicries. The tenor solos by Mr. Hanson were also productive of enjoyment. For" My Queen "he was recalled. His voice has a sweet tone and not being unneces- sarily strained, was heard to advantage. Miss Sophie Roberts accompanied with her usual grace and skill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EXTENSIVE ALTERATIONS. SALE! SALE ! !</head>
        <p>EXTENSIVE ALTERATIONS. SALE! SALE ! !</p>
        <p>On and after JANUARY 31st, previous to and during Alterations, THREE SHILLINGS IN THE E DISCOUNT. will be allowed off all PURCHASES for CASH ONLY, LARGE QUANTITIES OF GOODS AT LESS THAN HALF-PRICE. EXCEPTIONAL BARGAINS. J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53. and 55, BRIDGE-STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>PRESENTATION TO A CHOIRMASTER. After the morning service at the Church of the Sacred Heart, Liverpool-road, on Sunday, an inter- esting presentation took place, in the shape of a silver-mounted ebony baton, to the choirmaster (Mr. John Gibbing). Mr. G. Coggins, on behalf of the choir, made the presentation, and spoke in high terms of the interest their leader took in the work, and hoped he would long continue amongst them. He then asked him to accept the baton as a small token of their appreciation for him. In acknow- ledging the gift the recipient thanked all the sub- scribers, and promised to do his utmost to further their interests. The baton, which was prettily mounted in the form of a-shield, bore a neatly en- graved inscription. STOVBS.—Heating and Cooking.—OieLAND, Iron MO er, Bridge-street, Warrington. TOBACCONISTS Commenclng.—See Illustrated Guide 259 pages) 31. Tobacconists' Outfitting C0.,186,14u5t0n-road London. Hairdressers fltted..—tranager H. pirras.</p>
        <p>„•• • •- z</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>EX A MINER, FRIDAY, FEBRUARY 11 1898.</head>
        <p>THE EX A MINER, FRIDAY, FEBRUARY 11 1898.</p>
        <p>RUN IJORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>PICKWICK LITERARY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of the above society held in the Brunner Guild Hall on Tuesday evening a lecture was given by Mr. Millinger on " Sound." The lecture which was illustrated by diagrams proved very interest- ing and instructive. A vote of thanks was accorded to the essayist for his lecture and a similarcompliment was paid to the Chairman, Mr. Walter Millington.</p>
        <p>SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE, RtmcanN. The usual weekly concert was given in the Seamen's Institute, on Tuesday, before a fair attendance. Sens,,s were contributed by Mr. T. Woods and Miss Nelly Douglas. The accompanist was Mr.T. Forster. A vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. W. Shaw. missionary,for presiding, and a similar compliment was paid to the artistes for their services, and a pleasant evening waa thus brought to a close. BRUNSWICL MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Brunswick Mutual Improve- ment Society a very interesting meeting was held on Wednesday last. The members had arranged to have prize speaking competition, adjudicator, Rev. C, W. L. Christien. Prize, Hallam's " History of Europe in the 16th, 17th, and 18th centuries." There were three com- petitors, Messrs. Jones, Follicles, and Littler. Between Messrs. Fouldes and Littler there was little to judge. In the opinion of the adjudicator Mr. Littler was awarded the prize. DurroN Seneca. Bastin. At the monthly meeting of the Dutton School Board, held on Satur&amp;y, the Rev. L. D. Lyon Bennett presid- ing, a letter was read from tho Runcorn Board of Guar- dians inquiring when the new extension of the Dutton School would be complete. The Clerk was directed to reply that the contractrr (Mr. S. Appleton) was making good progress, but that the work bad not been com- pleted. The Board decided that the school be closed for one week to permit the contractor to take down the internal walls between the present school and the extension. FUNERAL OF A TIM BOAT INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>- On Sunday the remains of Mr. Edward Wichenden of Runcorn, who died on Tuesday, February lit were in- terred in the Halton Ceoptery. The deceased was well- known and respe.z.ted by all the men employed on the Bridgewater Canal. He was for over 20 years Tug In. spect sr at Top Locks. Thete followed his remains to the last resting place, Messrs. R. Rawlinsm, W. Byfield, G. Joyson, and 'f. Wi'hington, and alga a number of tug captains end enginaers. The Rev. Mr. Oliver per- formed the funeral service. Sr. EDWARDS' BRANCH OF THE LEAGUE OF Tug CROSS. At the meeting of the above branch of the League of the Grose on Sunday evening the Chaplain, (the Rev. H. B. Gore) gave a reading from the works of Edwin Waugh. The rooms were well filled with an appreciative audience, and a hearty vote of thanks to the reverend gentleman was proposed by Mr. J. WhAan, seconded by Mr. J. Caskey, senior, and carried wic.h acclamation. Evidences are not wanting that there is a revival takin4 place amongst those formerly in the League, and these weekly re-unions should do much towards bringing the members into closer touch with each other, and with their pastor. _MINCORN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.—ANAL TEA.</p>
        <p>TEif-i;ii-n—,i-ai";;Wt"TT;linounced in another column to take place next Wednesday, in the Co-operative Hall, Church-street. Tea will commence at 630 p.m. and afterwards a grand concert will be given in the Public Hall, Runcorn. Mr. Thurlow H. Johnson, F. 0.5., the well known humorist, who holds many valuible prizes and who is styled on the placards as the Grossmith of the North, has been specially engaged for the evening, together with a number of other well known artistes. If Wednesday-a performance comes up to those of former years there is a rare evening's amusement in store for those who attend. Other particulars will be found in our advertisement columns. A WIDNES DIVORCE CASE.</p>
        <p>In the Divorce Division on Friday before the President (Sir F. Jenne), a case was heard in which Walter Rams- bottom,a police constable stationed at florwich,near Bel- ton,and formerly of Widnes,sued for a divorce from his wife Eliza Ramsbottom on the ground of her adultery with William Henry Benson,also a police constable. The suit was undefended. Mr. Barnard appeared for the petitioner, who in his evidence stated that he was mar- ried to the respondent at Bury on the 15th February, 1885. He was now stationed at Horwich. There had been two children of the marriage,and lie lived happily wlth his wife until he had a quarrel with her about a man,which was passed over. In September,lB96,he had to take charge of a station at Widnes, where there were three other unmarried constables,and his wife had to do the washing and cooking for them. One of them being ill,it was the duty of the constable on the beat to call and see him every hour. That duty fell to petitioner and on going in at three o'clock on the morning of the 6th November, 1897, he found the co-respondent and his wife together under circumstances which admitted of no doubt that adultery had been committed. Other corro- borative evidence was given, and the President then granted a decree nisi for the dissolution of the marriage with costs.</p>
        <p>RUNMRN VOLUNTEER CONCERT.</p>
        <p>In coainection with the Runcorn detachment of the 2nd V.B Cheshire Regiment a concert was given on Monday evening in the Drill Hall, Greenway-road. The hall had been most tastefully decorated for the occasion the walls being draped in colours,while the platform was covered with a number of choice plants. The room was crowded long before the entertainment commenced. Shortly after half-past seven the Volunteer Baud gave a selection of music. Mr. George Grossmith, the great humorist,cornmenced his unique entertainment at eight o'clock, and fir the space of nearly two hours kept the audience in uncontrollable laughter. At the interval Captain George F. Ashton announced that owing to a little misunderstanding between them and Mr.Grossmith in connection with the arrangements, they were unable to have the distribution of the cups and long service medals. Lady Brooke was announced to distribute the prizes, and the follewing, are to receive them :—Long service medals—Sergeant W. H. Harvey, Sergeant J. A. Rushton, and Corporal G. Shingler ; Jubilee cup (pre- sented by Lady Brooke), Sergeant Harvey; challenge cup (presented by Miss Barclay).Sargeant J.A.Rushton ; Lady Brooke's challenge cup for recruits. T. W.Shaw.</p>
        <p>HALTON OLD WOMEN'S TEA PARTY.</p>
        <p>On Friday evening the 25th annual Lea party to the aged women of Halton, was held in the schoolroom. The room had been charmingly decorated, and the attend- ance was very gratifying. There were about 60 present, the oldest being 84 years of age. A substantial tea was provided consisting of hem and tongue sandwiches, cakes krt., and the old people thoroughly enjoyed the feast. The following ladies presided at the tables Mesdames Wray, Wright, Percival, C Baguley, and the Misses Johnson, Elsie Wright, and Baguley. Regret was expressed at the absence of tne Vicar through indisposi- tion, and the hop 3 was general that he would soon be rewtored to health. After tea a miscellaneous entertain- ment was given in another portion of the school, songs being contributed by Mr. Charles and Miss Jennie Liva- ker, a stump speech in character by Mr. Rowland Jeaeock, song by Mr. Harry Walton, and an exhibition of the phonograph by Mr. Johnson, of the Mount. The latter was quite a novelty to the old folks. They were greatly astonished at its capabilities, more particularly when it reproduced a song by Miss Linaker and a speech by Mr. Stokes. The entertainment was followed by musical chairs and country dances by the old people. On the motion of Mr. Wright, seconded by Mr. Stokes, a hearty vote of thanks was acc3rded to Mr. John Son for kindly arranging the entertainment. On leaving for home each old woman was the recipient of /lb of tea. SAD ACCIDENT AT SEA.—A SAILOR'S FOOT ToRN AWAY. There arrived in the Snip Canal at Runcorn on Moo- ' day afternoon, the screw " Hershell," owned by Messrs. Lamport and Holt, Liverpool, from New York, having 'on board, Emile Lyoberg, a carpenter, a native of Fiu- land,who had been very seriously injured on the voyage. It appears that the steamer left New York on the 21st January and four days later very reugh weather was encountered. Lyoberg was engaged regulating the steer- ing chains when heavy seas were shipped. The chains snapped and part of his foot was torn away. Those on board bandaged the limb as well as they could and on reaching Eastham a telephonic message was despatched to Runcorn requesting the officials to have a doctor in waiting. On the arrival of the vessel at Runcorn Dr. McKenzie saw the man and ordered his immediate re- moval to a hospital. Runcorn not being the possessor of a hospital the doctor communicated with Mr. W. Shaw, seamen's missionary. The latter in company with Mr. W. Banks a member of the Runcorn branch of St. John Ambulance Assoeiation, went to the boat but found it impossible to remove Lyoberg that night. It was arranged that he should be taken the following morning to Liverpool. The two gentlemen named con- veyed him on an ambulance by the first train and he was admitted into the Royal Infirmary, Liverpool. On the bandages being taken away, the foot and toes dropped off and it was found necessary to amputate the limb. The operation was successfully performed by Dr. Parker. After the operation Mr. Shaw and Mr. Banks saw the patient who appeared to be fairly well. He ex- pressed his thankfulness for the help rendered to him by them. He also said that he had expected to be home in New York in a month or so when he should have been married. The arrangements were being made during his absence.</p>
        <p>paired to the hall, where they were accommodated with front seats. The accompanist was Mts.Kenyon Davies ; conductor, Mr. W. R. Davies ; hon. secretary, Mr. Westbrook. The room was effectively deco- rated with bunting, the platform having been draped in a most artistic manner be the stage Manager (Mr. W. H. Maßugs, Runcorn). The audience were kept in roars of laughter all the evening, and one and all appeare,l to enjoy themselves thoroughly. Ap- pended is a copy of the programme,all acquitting them- selves with credit :—Opening chorus, the Troupe ; song, " Mother's Darling," Master Jack Rutter ; comic song, " The Insurance Man," Mr. W. H. Malings ; song, " SJ shy," Miss Edith Jones ; comic song, Mr. Vs. R. Davies ; song,Miss Emily Sewett ; humorous duet, " You Shan't play in our Yard," the little Misses Kitty Rutter and Hattie Jones ; song, " Cabin Bey," Mr. Sewett ; " The Whistling Coon," Mr. Dutton ; finale, " Carry me Home," Mr. G. Moorfield ; stump speech, " Women's Rights," Mr. J. Davies ; impersonation, Mr. W. H. Malings ; school sketch, Mrs. W. R. Davies; selection, (by string band), trz)upe ; skipping rope dance, Miss Edith Jones ; "impersonation of baby land." Mr. W.R. Davies ; prize Midget Dance," the little Misses Kitty Rutter,Hettie Jones,and P. Sewett ; sketch, " The Bet- ting Trick," Messrs. W. H. Malings, F. Crowther, G. Casson, and W. R. Davies ; " God save the Que. n."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>MINSTREL ENTERTAINMENT.</head>
        <p>MINSTREL ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>A large and ar preciative audience assembled at the Church Room, Stockton Heath, to witness a perform- ance by the Runcorn Charity Minstrels, combined with the Runcorn Prize Amateur Midget Minstrels,on Wednes- day evening. The room was cruwded to excess. Prior to the entertainment, tea was provided for about fifty women in the Church Room. The object of the enter- tainment was to pay off the bonus on t tie clothing club and also to entertain the members of the mothers' meet- ing. It is gratifying to nete that it was a complete success. The following ladies presided over the tables, which were abundantly laden with a choice supply of edibles :—Mrs. Gray (the vicarage), Mrs. Woods (Walton House), Miss J. Taylor, Miss S. A. Taylor, Mrs. Richard- son, Mn. Laithwaite and others. The refreshments were arranged by Mrs. Pickton. After tea, the members re-</p>
        <p>The Public should be on their guard again,t " doctored ' cocoas, of which there are many in the market. CADBURY'S Cocoa, being absolutely pure, atands all tests, the Medical profession and Press proclaiming its superiority as a delici- ous beverage and a nutritious food. The Medical Annual says : " perfectly pure Cocoa, of the highest quality. The name CADBURY on any packet is a guarantee ;,of purity.— Advt. (5)</p>
        <p>MEM</p>
        <p>MEE</p>
        <p>WM</p>
        <p>ISM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WLONES TEACEIERL' v,soCIATION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WLONES TEACEIERL' v,soCIATION.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEEEING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The annual .neeting of the above Association was held on Saturday last in the Victoria-road Board Schools, Runcorn, the retiring president Mr. J. S. Snoddy, head- master of the West Bank Board Schools, Widnes, in the chair. There was only a fair attendance of members. THE SECRETARY'S REPORT. The Secretary's report was presented by Mr. T. Philips of the Warrington-road Board Schools, Widnes. It showed that the As,ociation had now a membership of 80,being an increase of four during the year. Deputa- tions had been receir-1 during the year from the Asso- ciation by Mr. J. S. Gilliat,M.P. for Widnes, in London, and Sir J. T. Brunner, Bart., M.P. The former gentle- man was interviewed by Messrs. Snoddy and W.Kerman ' and the latter by n-asrs. B. Crosby and J. F. Moulton. The report was considered hi;hly satisfactory and was ' unanimously adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>TREASURER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE TREASURER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>_ The Treasurer's report was presented by Miss Kilburn and was considered very satisfactory. It was adopted. The Benevolent and Orphanage reports presented by the local secretary,Mr. J. F. Moulton showed that at the last conference held in Swansea the surn of £45 was presented on behalf of the local Association making a total since the formation of the Association of upwards of £lOO. While the report was considered on the whole to be very satisfactory it was felt that if each teacher would do his or her share in this matter the contribu- tions to the forthcoming conference would very largely exceed those of fors.sr years. lhe secretary was in- structed to issue a circular requesting the co-operation of all the members of the Association on behalf of the charitable funds. The business of the year having concluded the retiring president vacated the chair and in 50 doing introduced in few appropriate words the new president Mr. B. Crosby F.E.I.S. head master of the new Wesleyan schools at Garston and late headmaster of the Brunswick-street Board School, Runcorn. Mr. Crosby then took the chair amid applause. In the course of an address on educational topics in general, Mr. Crosby, spoke of the vast importance of the work in which the teacher of to-day is engaged and of the unworthy recognition which he receives in return. As an instance he stated the fact that in the Federations of Church of England Schools under the Voluntary School Act of 1897 only 21 per cent of the elected members wer 46 Having got through the preliminaries of the consti- tution of these Federations there came the important commented were of e o n teaching thisas profession.follows j The " Schoolmaster " question of the representation of the expert. Here was a grand opportunity, for the church especially, to utilise ' the garnered wisdom, and reward the self sacsifice of those patient yet powerful lay " associates in its work—the day school teacher. R splendid opportunity has been thrust aside the statistic mentioned attests. The loss is not so much ours. It is the church which will suffer more than its strongly emphasized his conviction that it should be the constant endeavour of teachers to more fully and lwe tdhe r se onwtetins°t— thus elementary teachers rnay treat mere care to realise." Mr. Crosby completely equip themselves for the important work which devolves upon them, this work, the training and drawing out of the mind requiring treatment as delicate and as skilful as the most delicate operations in any —p branch of the scientific world. There was a time, not far distant in educational history, when the broadest view taken of elementary teaching was that within the narrow limits of percentages. And tho highest view taken of the teacher by the public was that of a grant earning machine. But an enlightened and more scienti- fic system had brought with it greater demands upon the teacher as an educationist in the highest amuse tot the term and teachers now-a-days must not be con- tented with a limited knowledge. T IS necessary that they should know a great deal mad know it well, and consequently they should never cease to be diligent students. (Applause) ELECTION OF OFFICERS.—VOTES OF THANKS. On the motion of Mr. Devonald, seconded by the President. a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Snotidy for his services as president during the past year. Mr. T. Phillips was thanked for his services as secretary and was appointed to the vice.presidency mt. J. Bms,sh headmaster of the Brunswick Board School, Runcorn, was unanimously appointed secretary and Mr. Josnson of the Victoria Road schools, Runcorn, assistant secre- tary. Miss Kilburn received the hearty thanks of the ass )ciation for her services as treasurer and she accepted the unanimous invitati or to serve again in a similar Capacity. A like compliment was paid to Mr. Moulton for his services as local secretary to the charities but as he expressed kis intention to withdraw from that office, Mr. J. S. Snoddy was elected in his stead. Mr. W. Kaman was also thanked for his services as Parliamen- tary secretary, and being unable to accept office for an- other year, Mr. Moulton was appointed to the position. The following were appointed the Committee :—The Misses Dwerryhouse, and Tyriee Messrs. Smith, Calland, Devonald, and Showan. The following were appointed as delegates to the forthcoming teachers conference to be held at Cheltenham during Easter week, Messrs. Crosby and Brown together with the Misses Dwerry- house, and Tyrie and Messrs. Philips, Smith, Snoddy and Moulton. It was staued that the conference would be the first in the history of the National Uaion of Teachers to be held at the special invitation of any local governin • g body, the Town Council of Ch,ltenham hav- ing sent a unanimous invitation to the last conference which was accepted. Messrs. Crosby and Brown were also appointed to represent the association on the Coon- cii of the Cheshire District Union of Teachers for the ensuing year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WHOLESALE THEFTS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>WHOLESALE THEFTS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>5.30 the e andr eat da v it eeeYds.t that At the Runcorn Police Court on Friday morning last before Mr. F. J. Norman, John Delaney alias John Smith and William Woods, was charged on reined with hav- ing, on the first Of February at Runcorn. stolen a coat to f prosecutor, tA hnet h:a—George Moore- field,at on Tuesday vn edi n a vest, ss t produced were banging just inside the approach to the show. On look Mg through a mirror in the shop he saw someone take the things from the doorway. He went to the door,and in consequence of what a man told him he followed the prisoner up Bridge-street, and caught him by the stalls ae flis then about to o goods near the Market. He found that he had the coat and vest in lie possession, and he took them from him and brought him to the Police Station.—Sergeant Gill de- posed that at 530 en the date named he received the prisoner from the last witness. He charged him with the theft and he replied Yes, I took them."—The prisoner, who reserved his defence, was committed for trial at the nest Quarter Sessions. On Saturday, before Mr. F. J. Norman and Mr. W. Timmins, Euoch Walker, 20 years of age, was charged with stealieg an overcoat valued at 15i, the property of Joseph flunt,at Weston Point,on the 3rd inst. He was further charged with stealing a pair of trousers, a gold ring, a shaving brush, and a pair of shoes, of the value of U. 145 61, tee goods of W. H. Liddy and others, at Runcorn, on the 4th February.—The Bench committed the prisoner to gaol for two months' hard labour.—The Chairman said when the prisoner came out of gaol he had better take the superintendent's advice and enlist. He would not get off so easily next time. Before Mr. Walter Timmins, on Tuesday,Ellen Gandy, was charged en remand with stealing a watch and chain value £ll from the person of Thomas Mather, tinsmith, of Bold-street, on Saturday night in Merser-street, Run- corn.—The prosecutor said about eleven o'clock on the day named be was going to the Boathouse Public House when the prisoner stopped him. She pulled him to her. They then parted and when he wanted to know the time he found his watch had gone. He ran after her and charged her with stealing his watch. About two o'clock on Sunday morning he was brought to the police station and identified the watch and chain as his, and also the prisoner. Be did not give the prisoner the watch. He bad about 18s. in money in his pocket.— P.C. Wylie said from information received he searched for the prisoner and found her in a closet in Water-st. Witness searched her and found the watch and chain in her pocket outside her dress. In reply to the charge she said "I did not steal it,he gave it to me." At this stage of the case the prisoner was remanded for a week in order that she could call the landlady of the Boathouse Inn where the prosecutor and herself had had, she said, some drink. Mather denied that he had any drink with the prisoner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>NOTES ON GA NLES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GA NLES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>As an ardent Swintonian was leaving the field on Saturday he was heard to remark " Well they have had an easy thing on; I don't know what they were doing to let this lot beat 'em at Warrington ? " Few will deny that Swinton secured an easy victory, but to compare Saturday's team with that which beat the S vintonians at Warrington earlier in the season is an egregrious mistake. In this column last week, in com- menting on the team selected to play at Swinton I ven- tured to make a prediction—a very dangerous pro- cedure, I admit, when lreferring to football results—, but heie it is : " The players chosen by the committee for the important battle appear to spell 20 r Rints to nil of a licking all over." The result was just three points short of the number predicted,but the Swintonians fully des rved 20 points, and a little luck in goal kicking would have enabled them to have exceeded it. This is revenge with a vengeance, and takes all the glitter from the victory of Warrington on their native heath by ten points to three. .</p>
        <p>Against such a brilliant cc inbination as Swinton it would have been the wildest possible nonsense to have indulged in any hope of the victory of the Warrington fifteen with the team selected. On the face of it a three quarter line composed of Carey,lieesom, Isherwood,and Moores was no match for such a fine quartette as Lewis, Messer, R. Valentine,and Badger. It was chiefly in this department where Warrington were let down. At full back BO3COW was superior to Goodman, and Bate was the best half back on the field, although much of his work was rendered futile through lack of support.Duck- worth was hurt during the asacti, and did not play as well as usual. In the forward division Swinton are ex- ceptionally strong, but there was little to choose b: • • tween the competing eights on S )ter..iay, ei,uii it, heeling out Swinton may be said to have been more evidence. This was probably due to the method of plat:- ' ing the ball into the scrimmage by the :Swinton half backs, which, to say the least, Was (A very shady character on many occasions. The refereo, nowever, failed to see any infringement—excep , of course, when Warrington indulged in similar prac- tiees—and probably this accounts tor Swi aton's partia- lity for Yorkshire referees. He certainly showed gleater favour to the dark blues, and was, of eonrse,not blamed by the homesters. Whilst on this point, it may be n-entioned that one of the touch judges made a palpable error to the cost ...)f Warrington. Carey made his mark near the touch line, but the man with the lag ruled that he had been in touch, and Swinton made good use of this error of judgment.</p>
        <p>But it was in the threequarter line where Warrington were completely outclassed. Even when an opening presented itself, there was no player in the Warrington quartette who had sufficient speed. Isherwood showed up prominently on several occasions, but in comparison with his speedy opponents he was but as a cob to a race- horse. Moores made a serious slip when be failed to touch down near the posts but b!yond this be played his usually clever game;and saved a certain try in the second half. Be was too well watched by Lewis to do any scoring, and whenever he got possession his progress was decidedly stopped. Efeesom and Carey seldom got away and there was nothing of a brilliant nature in the play of either maa. In fairness to Carey it should be stated that he showed far more confidence, and played a better game than against Leigh. Heesom's play was marred by his slow movements. Lewis and Messer were the two wing men in the Swinton line, and whenever they were in possession a score seemed imminent. To make matters wors, the tackling ot the Warrington men was feeble in the ex- treme. On several occ salons Lewis and Valentine slipped through the hands of the Warrington men in a most sur- prising manner, and but for the determined tackling of Boscow, who played a capital game, the score would have been much larger. The substitution of IL Valen- tine in place of Ws distinguished brother was a success, and he showed something of the old warrior's dash and go as a centre three-quarter.</p>
        <p>Of the game itself little need be said here. For the first quarter of an hour the Warrington men did themselves I justice. By vigorous forward play toe Swinton men I were given a taste of what they experienced at War- rington, and when Deakin passed all opposition with the ! ball at his toes the spectators were ser.ously alarmed. I The honour of opening the scoring account was not, I however, to fall te Warrington, for when Deakin kicked I over the line his brilliant effort was foiled by Badger ! who raced up and kicked the ball dead. Cheered on by the spectators Swinton rose to the occasion in fine sty le,and alter the first try had been scored there was only one team in the running. By half time three tries —one of which should have been prevented—had been ! scored by the horn team,but curious to say not a single goal was kicked, so that Warrington re,:oinmenced toe second half only nine points in arrear. Warrington re-, started well,but au injury to Naylor caused him Co leave the field for about a quarter ot an hour,and this seriously handicapped their efforts. Thee anuttier opts &gt;de oc- curred which acted like a tonic on the Swinton News arrived that Salford were beaLing Oldham,,iiiu im- mediately there was a great ahuut by the spectators. ! The enthusiasm spread to the Swinton players who seemed to redouble their efforts,and if the cup and me- dals bad b2en brought on the field at that moment there could not have been much greater enthusiasm, Poor Warrineton struggled gamely on to the close,which no doubt culla as a welcome relief to them. The difference between the two teams is represented by toe score, and in acknowledging defeat no blame can be attached to the Warrington players who dii their best under the circumstances.</p>
        <p>A change in the three-quarter line is absolutely neces- sary if Warrington is to maintain its position in the League Competaiin or to pull through successfully the first round in the Northern Union Cup Competition. By their defeat on Saturday Warrington have dropped one step in the league ladder, and have been supereeded uy Wigan. Both Runcorn and St. Helens are still below Warrington, but as they havo piayea ~ureti matches less they have yet an opportunity of giving Warrington the go-by.</p>
        <p>The unexpected defeat of Oldham at Salford has caused Swinton to loom large in the eyes of the pro- phetic as the probable winaers of the championship of the Lancashire Senior Competition, and they ought to feel deeply grateful to their old rivals for so friendly a turn. On Saturday's torn Swinton are almost irresist- able, and have now the best chance of finishing at the top. Of course,much may happeo yettand there is that eventful meeting of the two CitlJS at Oldham on Febru- ary 19 to take into account, but at present Swinton possess the beat chance, and are certain of the premier position providing they can win all their matches. Con- sidering they have to meet Widnes twice and Oldham once, this is, of course, a large order, but there is no getting away from the brilliant form of the" dark blues."</p>
        <p>Third place is at the mercy of Widnes, unless they go off altogethet , the two points gained on Saturday having given them a commanding lead over Broughton Rangers, who have had a long spell without defeat up to invadink the chemical town. Wigan were a trifle lucky in win- ning at Runcorn, but Stockport made no mistake with Tvldesley, and Leigh gained a very decisive victory at Morecambe. Rochdale Hornets were a few weeks ago in great danger of capturing the wooden spoon, but they have now altered all that, and by a meritorious win at St. Helens placed themselves quite free from danger, though a dozen points out of twenty-two matches played is not much to brag about.</p>
