      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FRODSHAX</head>
        <p>FRODSHAX</p>
        <p>MUTH OF MRS. HOLFORD OF MAIN-STREET.—On Sun- day morning, Mrs. Holford, wile of Mr. John Holford of Main-st.' Frodsham, died very suddenly whilst fol- lowing her household duties. She was in the bedroom having only left the kitchen a few minutes, when the Rev. T. W. Dickenson, one of the curates at the Parir,h Church, who resided there, heard a noise as if someone had fallen. An alarm was raised and on going into the bedroom be found Mrs. Holford prostrate on the floor and in difficulties. Dr. Burton was sent for and arrived I before she expired. It is thought that she had over exerted herself and ruptured a blood vessel. She died an hour or so after the arrival of Dr. Burton. The funeral took place on Wednesday afternoon at the Frodsharn Parish Church, and was largely attended. FRODSHAM AND FRODSHAM LORDSHIP JOINT SEWERAGE COMMITTEE.-A meeting of this committee was held in the Boys' Schcol,Overton,on Tuesday at 7-30 p.m., Mr. T.Earlam in the chair. There were also present—Messrs. , C. Reynolds, W. E. Shore, P. Riley, H. Tiley, R.Bate,T. Lewis, G. F. Ashton (clerk for the day), VV.Diggle (sur- veyor), and J.Farrington (inspector).—The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board with reference to the extension of the sewer from Overton to Five Crosses, and stating that an inquiry would be held shortly. The Surveyor reported the progress made of the construction of the sewer In Townfield-lane. Notices were ordered to be served on the owners of houses on the south side of Townfield-lane to remedy the sewage outfit!' from their property.—The Inspector reported the result of an interview that he had had with Mr. Frank Bickerstaffe, of Five Crosses, with reference to the drainage of his premises, when it was resolved that the matter be left in the hands of .the Inspector._ _</p>
        <p>THEFT AT FRODSHAM.—At the Frodsham Police Court on Monday before Messrs. A. Thomas and Reynolds, IRichard Weston,groom lately in the employ of Mr. Youd of the Bear's Paw Hotel, was charged with stealing one pair of leggings value Is. 61 on the 3rd inst and also with being drunk in Ship-street, Frodsham on the 4th inst. He pleaded guilty. For the first offence he was discharged and Ler the second he was fined 10s. includ- ing costs or 14 days with hard labour. FRODSHAM LORDSHIP PARISH COIINCIL.—A meeting of this Council was held on Tuesday evening,at 8 o'clock in the Boys' School. There were present Messrs. 11. Tiley (chairman), Reynolds, T. IL Carter, Davies, Percival, Hulse, Lewis, R. Bate, J. Okell, and J. H.Davies (clerk). The Clerk read a circular letter pointing out that the 14th March was fixed as the annual parish meeting. The Chairman Signed a precept for the sum of LS to be made on the overseers for expenses in conuection with the Council. FRODSHAM LORDSHIP PAR "CHIAL COMMITTEE.—A meet- ing of this committee was held on Tuesday evening in the Boys' School, Overton,Mr. Henry filey in the chair. There were also present Messrs. Charles Reynolds, J.P., T. B. Carter, J. Percival. J. H. Davies, T. Lewis, T. Hulse, R. Bate, Mr. G. F. Aehton (clerk for the day), Mr. W. Diggle (surveyor). and Mr. J. Farringtou (in- spector). The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board conferring powers on the Rural Dis- trict Council with respect to the lighting of the Fodsham township and Frodsham Lordship. The Inspector re- ported that as ordered by the committee at their last meeting he had taken proceedings against a resident of Overton for contravention of the Public Health (Water Act,Section 8. The case was heard at the last sessions and a conviction was recorded with a fine of 6. and Bs. 6d. costs. In reference to overcrowding at Marsh Green it was decided to leave the matter in the hands of Mr. Farrington.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE -COUNTY COUNCIL. POLLVTION OF THE DEE. THE COUNTY FINANCES.</p>
        <p>The last meeting of the County Council of Chester, as presently constituted, was held on Thursday afternoon at Chester Castle,under the presidency of the chairman, Colonel George Dixon. Owing to the death of Alderman P. Green, the Council proceeded with the election of a gentleman to fill the vacancy. Councillor John Thomp- son, J.P., of the Chester Castle Division, was ap- pamted:</p>
        <p>An addresq, prepared by the Council chairman, giving a mood of the work of the Council during the past three years was handed in pamphlet form to the members of the years,</p>
        <p>The deputy chairman (Mr. T. W. Killick) said the report presented by the chairman was a very satisfactory one, and he did not think there was a County Council in England which could show a better one. He moved a resolution to the effect that the Council thank Lolonel Dixon for his address, and for the courtesy and ability with which be bad discharged the duties of chairman during the past three years. (Applause.)-111r. Duncan Graham secluded, and the motion was carried unani- mously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>AN APPLICATION FROM PRODSHAISE.</head>
        <p>AN APPLICATION FROM PRODSHAISE.</p>
        <p>A memorial was received from the Frodsham Parish Council asking that the parish of Frodsham might be created an urtnn district.</p>
        <p>kir. T. W. %Wick moved that a committee be ap- pointed to hold an inquiry. Dr. Hewitt thought there was not sufficient evidence in favour of the proposal to justify the Council holding an inquiry. It was very undesirable, he said, to create weak governing bodies, with small resources and small ideas. fie moved that the proposal be not entertained. After some discussion, Mr. Killick's motion was carried.</p>
        <p>LIMY RESERVE M.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the War Office asking the Council to do what It could to obtain employment in civil life fer army reserve men, of whom about 20,000 annually retire from active service.</p>
        <p>It wail decided to send a copy al the letter to the various local authorities in the countv.</p>
        <p>THE FINANCES OF THE COUNTY.</p>
        <p>Dr. Atkinsen (chairman of the Finance Committee) submitted a statement as to the finances of the county. He said the general loan debt had latterly considerably increased in consequence of the new works at Upton and Parkside asylums, Chester Castle, and the Dee Bridge, and it now stood at £104,269, against £30,000 three years ago. There had been a gradual increase in the amount of the claims from the various local authorities for contributicns out of the Exchequer Contributioa Account. These claims now amounted to £s3,7s2,against about £48,000 eight years ago, which was an important increase. It was well for the Council to thoroughly under- stand this, because unless the moneys received from the Government increased above what they were now the surplus annually carried to the credit of the county rate account must decrease accordingly, and in the result the amount of the annual county rate would necessarily increase. During the nine years the Council had been in existence its cash income from all sources had been £2,055,097, and the total cask payments £2,050,985, or an average annual county expenditure of £227,884. The county rates levied during the nine years had amounted in the aggregate to 3s 1011 in the pound, or an average of 5 1-8 d in the pound per annum —Old in the first year and 40 in the present year. The Police rates had amounted in the aggregate to is 511 in the pound or 2a in the pound per annum. It was estimated that when the Council went out of offir!e in March they would leave their successors a balance in hand of about £5,800, after discharging all liabilities to the close of the financial year. By the Agricultural Rates Act the county had received from the Government dur- inggf u h£d12,i3h7711 e4us . 2hdf levyingwhich thhaids been am amount fdr yearoton now tho hewe ratepayers acutl hoe closing yan y ers of the county.</p>
        <p>ISOLATION HOSPITALS. Dr. Hewitt, in connection with the minutes of the Public Health Committee, called attention to the con- ference between the sub-committee and the urban dis- tricts of Northwich, Middlewich, and Winsford and the Northwich rural district on the proposal to combine these districts into one hospital district. The sub-com- mittee had not been able to get these districts to agree to the scheme, and they recommended that the matter be adjourned for six months, to give the districts an opportunity of again considering it. This was agreed to by the Council. THE POSITION OF SCIENCE SCHOOLS. The minutes of the Technical Instruction Committee contained a letter hem the Committee of Council on Education, in which they say it has CO/110 to their know- ledge "that in several of the schools of science recently established the proportion (A students who continue their studies into the second and third year is so eiccoedingly small that the school cannot with any justice be con- sidered as a school of science at all. It must be remem- bered that a school of science is essentially organised to give three or four years' progressive course of instruction and the special grants made to such a school are arranged with this view. When students do not remain more than two years, neither the prescribed course nor the grants are approximate, and the students should not be admitted to the school of science unless there is a reason- able prospect of their remaining for the full course. In- structions have been given to the inspectors to report to the department any case where 25 per cent of the first year students leave the school at the end of the year, or more than that per centage of the second year students leave at the end of the second year. It will be a question whether such school should continue to be recognised as a school of science." Dr. Hewitt said that if the department acted on the lines suggested, the effect would be to punish the wrong people. He was sure the Technical Instruction Commit- tee were most anxious to keep their students as long as possible, but they had CO compulsory powers. The action the department proposed to take would not have the effect they desired. Be moved—" That this Council compulsory powers e schools, the Depart- punish the managers n toh voeepnret ttobhfea st ,c ne childrenn tchee and tatbeAsnerdnti cnegwoi sfl to in the manner foreshadowed i a their letter." Mr. C. H. Roundel' seconded, remarking that the pro- posed action of the Department would press very hardly on the children of the working classes. The motion srs9 RrioDtvd.</p>
