      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WAIOUNeTuN TEAM AT SWINTON.</head>
        <p>THE WAIOUNeTuN TEAM AT SWINTON.</p>
        <p>A DEFEAT AVENGED. When those who take an interest in football in Warrington became aware ot the team which had been selected to play in the return engagement at Swinton, very faint hopes of the success or the War- ringt.on team were entertained. In fact many seemed to regard the match as a walk over for the all conquering Swintonians, and commenced pros- pecting on the probable number of points which would be recorded against Warrington,which it may be remembered, is the only team which has suc- ceeded in beating the Swinton fifteen. Considrir- able interest was centred in the engagement in the Swinton district,due to the fact just mentioned,and it was not surprising to find over 7,000 spectators present. The Swinton captain—the well-known Valentine—was still an absentee from his team through injuries, but a capable substitute was found in his brother who appeared at centre-threequarter in his stead Barber was, of course, still absent from the Warrington fifteen, but he was not expected to appear as he was not placed on the team list. Of the players who were,Barratt and Ltallam were absent, and their places were taken by Jolley and Carey. The tearuk. faced each other as follows :</p>
        <p>Warrington :J. Boscow, full back; F.Moores, D. Isherwood, P. Carey, and F. Heesom, threequarters; J. Bate and J. Duckworth, half backs; J.Deakin,J. Taylor, J. A aylor, 11. Robey,J.Swift, and J. Mason, forwards. Swinton : J. Goodman, full back ; J. T. Lewis,R. Valentine, 0. Badger, and R. J. Messer,threequarter backs; W. Pearson (captain), and F. Morgan, half backs; F. Ainsworth, J. Evans, P. Greenha_gh, G. Harris, J. Johnson, J. M. Owen, B. Tickle, and Wonhington, forwards. Referee: Mr. Raynor (Batley). Touch judges : Mr. E. H. Flower (Wigan) and Mr. R. Clegg (Stock- port). Operations were commenced at three o'clock, when Mr. Raynor, of Batley,gave the signal to Pearson to kick off. Warrington played uphill during the first half, and another difficulty to contend with was the large Tian- tity of chaff which lay several inches deep from one end of the field to another. Pearson's kick was returned by BDEICOW to midfield where from a mull bv it. Valen- tine, the visitors made an attack. Deakin, Mason, and Taylor were prominent with some fine forward work, and Goodman was called upon to defend. A round of passing between Duckworth, Bate, Isberwood, and Carey carried play near to the Swinton line, where Messer saved grandly. Free kicks 'o Swinton relieved the pressure. but the visiting forwards carried a scrim. mage and rushed the ball inside the Swinton quarter flag. Evans charged down a free kick at goal taken by Heesom, and immediately afterwards Deakin secured possession and dribbled past all his opponents but kicked too hard, a dead ball resul ing. Even play continued for some time, the Warrington forwards putting in some hard work. The home players then had a turn of pressing, and a passing ren between Badger and Messer ended in m.esser getting over the goal line, but the ball was ordered back in consequence of a for- ward pass. Not to be denied, the home players con- tinued the pressure, and after Pearson, Badger, and Morgan had tried.° score, Lewis gained possession, and making a timely kick across the goal mouth, Evans secured, and scored a try. Goodman failed to increase the points. The drop-out kick was returned, and War- rington were again hard pressed on their goal line. Moores failed to touch down when the ball was kicked over the line,and Pearson racing up dropped on the ball under the posts. Again the goal kick was a failure. Stung by these reverses Warrington played up with vigour, and for a time kept the ball confined to the Swinton half. Bate, Moores, Isherwood and Heesons put in some clever play, but lack of speed I told against them, and they were grassed when becoming dangerous. A passing run between the Swin- ton backs, in which Messer, Lewis, and Valentine were prominent, forced the Warrington players back to their quarter flag, and Lewis was almost over when B scow made an effective tackle, and temporarily averted the danger. The quick and clean passing of the Swinton backs severely tested the Warrington defence, and another breach was found just before half-time, when Evans gaining possession near the Warrington line passed to R. Valentine who had no difficulty in adding a third try for the home team. Badger was entrusted with the kick, but like his predecessors, he was unable to place the ball over the cr es bar, and when the teams ' changed ands, Swinton were leading by nine points to nil: toTsobreerwesttbaertbwalal st orlltandee I be yf t ,Tkai cykl oe dr , it hboe ,hi inn edn dhei amv.o u y.r ilnagy swpaese dyofanadn execveelnlencthacroainctbeirnatfioorn saotmnoengtismt et,hebuthotnahee backs once more told a tae, and the visitors were. hard pressed,Boscow once morie coming to the relief of his side. Duekwork initiated a passiug movement which transferred play to midfield. Another passing run be- tween Bate and Isherwood ended in the latter player following up a kick and collaring Goodman before he could return the ball. At this stage,Naylor had to retire for a fay/ minutes owing to injuries, and this seriously handicapped the visitors. As the result of rapid passing between the Swinton backs Badger threw the ball for- ward,and Boscow securing,punted finely into the centre. Badger was early prominent with a good run, and once again Warrington were placed on the defensive. Moores just succeeded in saving a try by dropping on the ball over the Warrington lire. From the drop out Swinton made a most determined effort to increase their score, and Messer was tackled by BaCOR when only a yard or two off the line. From the ensuing scrimmage Bate got possession, and by a clever run and short kick he relieved the pressure. Lewis, however, got possession, and carried the hall to the visitors 25,the tackling of the Warrington men being noticeable for its tameness. Shortly afterwards leadger irAna ngn exchange xot ceh ab Messergll e to that naktei acpkr thesa ybe orWarrington gobaelaltienne,,. and tptisesr- ween crossed Johnsont resuming, ended in favour of the Warrington custodian._ ts°cno wl I°:ta. the pressure Lewis made another run along the touch I line, and passed to Pearson who dodged past Boscow, and scored the fifth try of the match. Badger on tine, ' but Lewis speedily ran the ball back again. Continuing, occasion from an easy place kicked a goal. 'The War. heiVaaordri nßog rington team now seemed hopelessly stuck to their task admirably, and several snatches of good play were seen. Boscowen particular, showed up splendidly, and his long kickisg opened up the play con- siderably, giving Warrington a footing in Swinton terri- tory. Several abort passing movements enabled War- tington to get near the Swinton line, but just when they were becoming dangerous Badeer smartly intercepted a pass from Bate t ) Carey and sent the ball to mitifield. Swinton again renewed the pressure, Valentine, Lewis, and Badger being prominent, but no furth3r scoring took place, and Swinton retired victors of a well-con- tested game by one goal, five trite (17 points) to War. rington nil,</p>
        <p>• • • :,•• • • . ,</p>
