      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BEFoRi. THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFoRi. THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>BRUTAL ASSAULT BY AN IRISH LABOURER. Last Friday morning at the Warrington Police Court before the Mayor and Mr. J.Hepherd,a powerfully built Irish labourer named Michael For, Sankey Hall Farm, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Liver. pool-road at ten minutes past eleven on Thursday night; be was also charged with assaulting Price Dayus at the same time and place.—Price Dayus, 7 Regent-street, a labourer at Sankey Lead Works,whose left eye was much discolouredand his head wrapped in bandages,said he was coming home along Liverpool-road abou: ten past eleven and when near Bank Quay Mis&amp;on he met prisoner,who asked him the way for Bewsey and complainant diiected him as well as he could. Complainant was smoking a pipe and prisoner asked for a smoke. Complainant re- plied that he allowed no one to smoke his pipe but him- self,and prisoner took it out of his mouth. When asked to return it prisoner butted complainant with his head, knocked him down, and afterwards kicked him on the head,causing a severe cut. Prisoner had had a drop of beer. Complainant became unconscious and had to be taken to the Infirmary.—William Ratchford, 57, Upper- man-street, Liverpool, said that shortly after eleven on Thursday night he was coming down Liverpool-road when he saw prisoner knock complainant down. Witness picked him up and prisoner knocked him down again and kicked him on the head.—P.C. Cooper deposed to finding complainant lying on the font path unconscious and bleeding. He assisted him and afterwards went after prisoner. The latter said " If you come after me I will kill you." Witness overtook him and arrested him. He was drunk.—Prisoner said that on the day in question he got wet through and took some whiskey. He did not know how the row started.—The Chief Constable said the West of Ireland labourers were a blood-thirsty lot. He did not know that he ever heard of a more dastardly assault,—Tke bench gave prisoner the maximum p,milty of a month on the first charge and two months on the second charge.—The witner.s Ratchford, who is " on the road," was granted 55. from the poor box for his assistance. A BATCH OF BOY GAMBLERS FINED.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor and a full bench of magistrates,William Parnell, William Clewes, William Sutcliffe, Joseph Sut- cliffe, James Lee, Joseph Watson. and Thomas Heath, boys whose average age was 14, were charged with playing cards for money in a public passage near Wat- kin-st., on January 30th.—They pleaded guilty.—P.C. Clough said that on the Sunday in question he was on duty behind Watkin-street with P.C. Monks in pain clothes, when he saw the seven defendants playing cards in a passage between Watkin-street and Phillip-st. They were playing for money. Witness caught Parnell and got the names of the others.-1 similar charge was brought against John Edward Gallep,Joeeph Birchall, C. Hill, John Sampson, and Thomas Hopwood, for playing in Eldon-street, on January 30th. —They pleaded guilty.—P.C. Clough deposed to seeing the defendants playing cards under a gas-lamp.—The Chief Constable said the gang in Eldon- street were an organised lot. Complaints were rife from all parts of the town about this Sunday gambling, and the police were having a hard try to put it dawn.—The magistrates dealt with both cases at once. Ail the boys la the first case, with the exception of Clewes, who is 19 years of age, were fined ss. Clewes and the defendants in the second case were each fined 7s. et, or an alterna- tive of 3 days' and 7 days' imprisonment respectively. THEFT FROM THE Ma RICET.—A PENYETH WOMAN FINED. . -</p>
        <p>On Monday at the War-ri;;;,7o.ri Borough Police Court, before the Mayor (Alderman Wright) ad other magis- trates, Mary Lannehan, Warrington-road, Penketh, was charged with having on Saturday, stolen three pieces of annel, value 3s. 5i., from Mr. Glories Williameon's stall in the market, and a pair of boSts value 5. lid., from Mr. Joseph Frieby's stall.—Mr. Percy Davies appeared for the prisoner, who pleaded guilty.— The Chief Constable (Mr. Talbot) said this was one of those painful cases in which a woman hitherto respect- able seemed to have givea way to a sudden impulse. At half-past five on Saturday evening the prisoner went to Mr. Frisby's stall in the market. She there purchased two pairs of boots, which were parcelled up and handed to her. She then asked Mrs. Bower,the manager's wife, to allow her to leave her bag on the stall. Permission was granted to her, and she put the bag down near a form Inside the etall.She then went outside and brought a pair of boots from the front of the scall and tried them on. She said "They are too little" and kicked them un- der the form behind her back. She immediately left the stall, and Mrs. Bower saw her go relied ,the market and stop at Mr. Williameon's stall, the owner of which was temporarily absent. She snatched up three lengths of flannel, concealed them under her cloak, and walked away. On her return to Mr. Frishv's stall she rook up her parcel and the boots she had rejected, and put them in her bag. Mrs. Bower followed her as she left the stall and brought the boots back. When charged with steel- lag the articles in question she at first iteiignantly denied it, but now threw herself on the ni-ecy of the Court. She said she yielded to a sudden impelse.—Mr. Davies addressed the Bench at some length on behalf of the prisoner, dwelling upon her previous good character, and said the woman yielded to:a sudden temptation. A fine of 203. including costs was imposed in each case.</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES. FRIDAY.— Before the Mayor (in the chair). Me L Hepherd. A Blarnatt-nt-Law COMPLAINS OF ILLATSLAGR.-HRLIDAh Kelly, 90 Hardy-st., summoned Michael Oraghan. 10 Heaton- at„ for assaulting her on January 31th.—She said that on the date in question she was in defendant's house and remon- strated with him for allowing his brother to use bad langu- age. He got up from his chair and struck her on top of the head and she fell to the ground. She had bruises on her left leg.—Hannah Kelly, jun., deposed to seeing defendant hit her mother on top of the head. Mr. C. H. tinsworth' de- fended and said this was the old case of the mother-in-law interfering.—Defendant hod told her to keep out of his house, and on this occasion ordered her out. She refused to go and he put her out.—The case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED A.ssAuLi.—Bdward A4dison, 48 Fennel-et was charged with assaulting Jane Lawton, 25 Oliver-st. on luau- ary 29b.—After bearing evidence the magistrates 'dismissed the Case. QUARREL ABOUT a PIGEOL—Henty Clare, Flag-lane, was charged with assaulting and beating William Buckley, et. James'-street on February 2nd.—Mr. H. J. Longton repre- sented defendant and pleaded not guilty.—William Buckley said that on February 2nd he was coming down Flag-lane when Clare came to him and accused him of keeping a pigeon which he knew nothing about. He ended by hitting complainant in the face and they had a struggle on the floor. —Henry Whitt tker deposed to seeing Clare hit comp!ainant in the face, and they then fell to the floor.—Mr. Longton said that the men had sharp words together and had a struggle. Neither was hurt.—The magistrates ordered Clare to pay</p>
        <p>Costs. SQUABBLE BETWEEN NEIGHBOURS.—Kate Ward summoned by Annie Ladder, both of Golden Grove-square f" using threats to .her on January 30th.—Mr. U. if: Unsworth appeared for complainant and said that the quarrel arose through defendant having told complainant's husband that his wife had had a gentleman In her house</p>
        <p>YOUR ATTENTION, PLEASE ! Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago. Vies, Pains in the Bask Dropsy. Wind and Water Complaint, Disease' of Kidneys Bladder, Stone, Sciatica. Rheumatism, and Gout will find a positive Cure in Hoidroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small boa and if not satisfied your money will be returned. Prices is. lid., of all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps, from ROLDROYD'S, Medical Hall, Checkheates, Yorks. Sold at 35, Bridge-street, Warrington. Don't be put off. If you cannot get then write to the Proprietor and a horgwilnateottent next post.—Advt.</p>
        <p>at night. This, the husband disproved, and since then defendant had constantly annoyed complainant and challenged her out to tight. On Sunday she put her head In at complainant's window encl said she would give her a blow which would knock her into eternity.—Annie Ladder corroborated, her story being supported by her husband, Miss Naylor, and Mrs. Walton.— Defendant denied using the threat and brought a cross-summons against Mre. Ledder for threatening her.—The magistrates lsound Mrs. Ward over to keep the peace for six months in surety of ES, and pay costs 15s. MONDlY.—Befars the Mayor (chairman), Mr. F. Monks, Alderman Blackhurst. Mr. T. Bird:tall, Councillor J. Shaw Green, Councillor Smethurst, Mr. J. W. Hayward, and Mr. S. Wright. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Catherine Conway, an elderly woman of bad repute, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Guardian-street, at 11-45 on Saturday night.— P.O. Edwards deposed to finding prisoner ringing the bell and kicking the door of the Workhouse. She became violent when he apprehended her.—Prisorter was sent to gaol for one moath's hard labour. —Two similar charges were brought against Late Ann Jones, 6, Angel-yard, who made her 16th appearance and pleaded guilty. —P.C.'s Edwards and Sankey proved the two cases and prisoner was given 14 days on each offence.—William Dunbobbin, 78, Napier-street drunk and disorderly in Wilderspool-road on 2nd February, fined 5s and costs. OBWENlTT.—ltichard Weaver,private in 0 Company Son'h ' Lancashire Regiment was charged with using obscene lan- guage In Lynse-street. on January 29th.—P.O. Barnett de- posed to finding prisoner in Lyme-street with a crowd round him, die was swinging his bgt round him and using obscene language. Witness had to take him to the police station.— An °fiber from the Barracks said that defendant had only been with them six weeks and had had no charge against him.—A lino of 55 and costs was imposed. Pinartxo.—John Harper,33, Town Hill,and John Stirrup, Pinner's-brow, pleaded guilty to a charge of fighting in Tan- ner's-lane on February lat.—The Chief Constable said that the affair was "after the bail," and the defendants were lighting about a lady.—They were hound over to keep the peace for six months and pay 7s costs. W•ItTED A NOSE I —The Rev. F. Slater was summoned for allowing his dog to be at large unmuzzled in Wilderspool-road on February Ist—P.C. faxenclale said he noticed the dog running about unmuzzled and chased it home.—Mr. Slater's ton said they had already had four muzzles made for the dog. It was a thoroughbred pug and had no nose to fasten the muzzle on (Laughter.)—A fins of 2a 6d was imposed. TUESDAY.—before Mr. R. Garnett (in the chair) and Mr. T. fireball.</p>
        <p>Tit Err PROM • STABLE-Y•RD.—An elderly man, named George M'Cluire. was charged with stewing from the stable yard of Edward Conner. Bank-st.. on Monday evening eight corn sacks value 4s.—Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—Detective Inspector Monaghan said that at 5'40 on Monday evening he saw prisoner coming down an entry leading from Cloth Hall-yard, where Mr Connor's yard was. He was carrying the bags and took them to Chorley's marine stores, and offered them for sale. He said he had bought them from farmers in Cheshire, but could not give their names. Wit- ness found his statements to be generally untrue —William Gilmour.horse keeper for Mr. Connor,deposed to missing two bags from the stable in Cloth Hall-yard, Bank-st. and he identified them among those In prisoner's possession.—The Chief Constable said they id not allege that he had stolen the other six.—John Bridge, a youth in the employ of Mr. Burrows, Bridgewater-st , a butcher, said that on Monday prisoner tssisted him with some work, and witness gave him six bags for it —Prisoner, who had been previously con- victed, was sent to gaol for 14 days. HELPLESSLY DRUNE.—Bridget Lally made her 42nd ap- pearance and was charged with being drunk in Iriam-street, at 20 minntes to 12 on Monday night. —P C. Marsh deposed to finding her lying helplessly drunk at the entrance to the Police Station at the time stated He locked her up for safety.—Mr. Birchall : It is a wonder to me how these women get h -Ip- lessly drunk. —The Chief Constable, The drinking done by women in Warrington is mostly done in private houses. Prisoner was fined 6s. and costs.</p>
        <p>iii;;;;Esply.—Elefore Mr. JobaFairelough (In the chair), Mr. J. Geddea,-anditi. B rola all. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Mary Jane Curley was fined ss. including costs, with the alternativ6 of seven days' im• ptisonment, for being drunk and disorderly in Horse- market-street on the previous night.—P.C. Carter proved the case.</p>
        <p>THUILSDAY.—Before Alderman iutton (chairman); and Alderman Pai rclough</p>
        <p>OccenoNALlLiciosc,—Mr. lUnsworEh, solicitor, applied for and was granted an occasional license at the Parr Hall on Friday evening on that occasion of the Cricket Ball. The licensee was Mrs.Brovrn,ot the Coffee House.</p>
        <p>A SEPARATED Wm.—Elizabeth Ellison made her 17th appearance before the magistrates, this time on a charge of being drunk and disorderly in Cambria Place on the previous night, and further with assaulting an aged woman named Sarah Carlyle —P.C. Bruen stated that he was on duty in Horsemarket itrest about six o'clock the previous evening when he received information of a disturbance which was taking place at Cambria Place. He went there and saw the prisoner who was drunk and a crowd of people around her. Hrs. Carlyle complainedtohim of having been knocked about by the prisoner. She was very disorderly on being removed to the Police Station.— Sarah Carlyle, of 4, Cambria Place, deposed that about 6.30 she heard a disturbance and on going to the door she saw her daughter being struck by the defendant. Witness went between them, but the prisoner knocked her to the ground, and in falling she caught her back on ast no step, and also hurt her head.— The Chief Constab'e said the prisoner was a very drunken and violent woman. Her husband was In the employment of the Corporation, and had obtained a separation order from her. paying 7s. a week towards her maintenance. She had been sent to homes. but she repeatedly came back to Warrington and caused a distur- bance at her husband's house.—Evidence corroborating Mrs. Carlyle's statement with reference to the assault was given by Emily Burgess wife of William Burgess, a plumber, and by Clara Carlyle.—Tho defendant who was very excited con- tended that Mrs. Carlyle's daughter was the cause of the disturbance. She denied that she was drunk, and made allegations against her husband.—Emily Clare stated that the defend Int did not assault Mrs. Carlyle. The magistrates committed the prisoner to gaol for one month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PE ['TY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>THURSDAT.—BPfore Mr. T. J. Gillespie. (chairman). Mr. C.</p>
        <p>B. F. Borron. nnd Colonel Wood.</p>
        <p>A DISPUTED CASE 01 DRUNERNNESS.—MiehaeI Fagan was charged with being drunk and disorderly on January 23rd in Barlestown. - He pleaded not guilty, and was represented by Mr, Stuart—The case had been adjourned from last court day for further evidence to be brought.—P.O. Aspden said that on the night in question he saw defendant in Legbest., Earlertown. He was drunk, swearing, and had a gun in his hands with which he threatened to blew somebody's head off. Witness followed him to his home and said he should report him.—P.C.'s Duncan, Schofield. Clarke, and Brindle, all de- posed to seeing defendant on the night in question in Legh-st., in a state of drunkenness and behaving in a disorderly man- ner.—Blizabeth Ann Robinson also stated that she saw defendaat on the night stated. He was under the influence of drink and brought a gun out with which lie threatened to blow a policeman's heat elf.—William Robinson corrobo- rated.—Mr. Stuart said the evidence was the same as before. His information was that the police were mistaken, and that Vegan was in bed at the time stated.—Michael Fagan. the defendant nee sworn, and stated that there was Brow in the street that night. He went straight home about 11 o'clock and went to bed at 11.30, and did not get up until Sunday morning. He never left his bed or took any gun out that night.—Daniel Conroy said he was with the defendant on the night in question at 11 o'clock and went home with him, Def-ndant was not drank. There was a row in Legh-street, and there were several constables about. Witness did not see the defendant again and if he had come out in the street he should have seen him.—Thomas Price, 103, Legh-street. said Fagan lodged with him and he had always been a steady Irian. Witness went to bed before eleven and heard defendant come to bed about half-past. He had a gun In his house but It had not been tAken out. There was dust on it yet.—There was no previous charge against defen- dant. The Bench said they had no doubt that defendant was guilty and he would be fined Si ant coats. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY —The following persons were fined for being drunk and disorderly on dives dates :—Eliz- abeth Fleming, 29th January in Barlestown, find 2s. 63. and costs ; James Baker. 18th January in Winwick, fined 2s, 63. ' and costs; Josiah Corless, drunk only, in Newton on Feb- ruary sth, fined 2s. 64. and costs; Jeseph Naylor some offence on February sth. fined 51. and orate, Elizabeth Clare. Winwick. on February sth, 2s. 6d. and costs. Michael Joyce, drunk and disorderly in Golborne on January 22nd, fined 10s. and costs or 14 days.—He was further charged with a similar offence on February sth, and also with assaulting P.C. Shaw.—The latter proved the case, and said that defendant struck him in the face when spoken to and afterwards kicked him about the body.—Furtoer fines of 103. and eosts in both the latter cases were imposes. Patrick Rock, drunk and disorderly on January 29th in Golborne, fined 2s. 63. and costs. DOG, AT LARGE, Percy M. Beesley. dog at large in Barlestown. on January 281 b, fined ds 64 including costs; , Joseph Marks, same offence, on January 31st. in Wlnwick, lined 39. 64. ; George Robinson, same offence in Haydock on Sunday last, fined 3s, 6d. A B/SCER SUMMONED —James face, Lowton, was slim. moned for not having a correct scale on his cart on February 4th.—He pleaded guilty.—lnspector Sheppard said that he met defendant's son in charge of a bread cart in Croft. He had scales with him which were not correct. - Defendant uo of 181. oh. . d et. e and cnawwaraesoimf tpohese condition of the scales.—A fine FIGHTING —William Henry Jones and Henry Critchley were each bound over to keep the peace for six months for committing a breach of the peace by Ighting in Golborne on January 29th._</p>
        <p>OBSCLISITT.—Mory Jane Charles was charged with using obscene lan ova in Golborne on February 4th—P.S. Young proved the case and said defendant had used obscene language to a gentleman who had called the Police In consequence. She was fined 58. and Coats. OCCASIONAL LICSNSE.—AIfred Thompson was granted an occasional license to sell in the Town Hall, I7ewton on February 16th, the occasion of the Conservative ball.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. J. L. Davies and Mr. J. Ockleston. CRITELTY TO A HORSE.- Obadiah Wooding, boatman was summoned for causing a horse to be worked whilst in an un- fit state, on the 20th January.—lnspector Greatrex stated that about 2 p.m. on Thursday, 2lth last month, he was, on duty on the canal bank at Runcorn when he observed a small boy driving a horse attached to the boat "Pansy." He no- ticed that it was very lame and it was not in a fit condition to work. Witness stopped the lad and asked who was the cap- tain and he said Wooding. The defendant then came up. He examined it and found it to be an aged grey gelding It was in poor condition and very lame on the off fore-leg and Was suffering great pain from badly strained tendons. Witness asked defendant if he knew the horse was lame, and he said he did, and he had a rubbing bottle for it, but it was no use rubbing it when working. He advised him to take it to the stables. Next day witness called at the stables and learned the animal had beast taken to Staffordshlre.—P.C. Carrington said he went in company with the inspector to the stables in Percival-lane on the date named. The animal In question was very lame and standing on three legs. The feltock was swollen. Weodin said he had been rubbing it for corns--Defendant said he was 74 years of age and he had not driven a horse for over 12 months. lie bad worked for the North Stafford Company 51 years and for his present employers (the Andertcn Company) 3 years.—Mr. Dunning agent for the Anderton Company, said the horse WAS not very lame when he saw it about a fortnight ago. The horse must have gone worse and the next thing be heard about it Was that the Inspector had condemned it, He had known the defendant for over 30 years and he could say that he was very harmless. He had }lye children whose ages were 15 years 13, 10, 8, a baby in arms. The eldest bad just started to work and the defendant had to maintain his wife and the rest. Defendant</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LA.NCASHIRE LADY CURED.</head>
        <p>A LA.NCASHIRE LADY CURED.</p>
        <p>The good which may often accrue not only to indivi- duals, but families, by the utilisation of opportunities when they present themselves,is strikingly illustrated in the case of Mrs. Kitchen, wife of Mr. Elwin Kitchen farm bailiff,Price's Cottages, Birch's-row, Aughton, near Ormikirk. To investigate this:matter, the Ormskirk correspondent of the " Southport Visiter " called at Mr Kitchen's house. The reporter found Mrs. Kitchen,with Iher three childrer, seated round the tea table. She ooked the very picture of health. Her story was as follows :</p>
        <p>" I have been married ten years, and up to a few months ago I never knew what it was to be well. For weeks I was u nable to get about the house. I alwayg wanted to be left alone. It was a trouble for me to move. The doctors called it anrerala. My legs used to swel I the size of that kettle (pointing to the one dinging on the bar). 1 had no appetite, but was always thirsty. I never seemed to have enough</p>
        <p>4 A age , T r ' ) 4 Qii • I'', ~,", -. )1 —4l"--* i / ‘</p>
        <p>to drink. The doctors could do me no good, and, as years went on, I seemed to grow worse. At length I entered the Wigan Infirmary,and was there eight weeks: afterwards at the Southport Hospital for three weeks, but still I did not get better. One day my husband said to me, ' I hear a lot about Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People ; why not try them ?' Well, I did, and I began t) feel better a few days afterwards. In all I took three boxes, and now,l never felt better in my life. I am sure that the Pills, and they alone, have cured me. There was nothing else for it. There is no one whom I come across;who complains:of being unwell, but that I recommend them to take Dr. Williams' Pink Pills." Mr. Kitchen was as enthusiastic as his wife over the matter, and there was such a rim, of sincerity about all they said, as could not fail to impress the hearer. The reporter, indicating the children, remarked. "Well Mrs. Kitchen, you won't have to be laid on one side to attend to these."</p>
        <p>_ " No," she replied, with a smile. " But it a pleasure to work for the children when you are well, and lam all right now."</p>
        <p>bore an excellent character and he (witness) believed he had never before been in a police court.—The Bench imposed a fine of 23. 6d. and 12s. 6cl costs.</p>
        <p>RIVAL BUTOHERR.—CharIes Watson, butcher, Church- street. was summoned by John Hulse, butcher, also of Church-street, for using threats towards him on the 28th January. There was also a ceoss-summons.—Hulse sAid he was going along Church-street. on the date named when he saw Watson behind his cart. He came from behind and pushed him and used threatening language towards him. Watson said he simply pushed Hulse and told him not to say any more about him at Barnton. He said he would settle him He ieplied with a similar threat.—Huise Since opening a shop in Church-street I have had a lot of trouble with him Watson had pet oat a lot of insulting bills "Why buy your beef of a blacksmith."—Watson I Before he opened the shop he went out to Preston Brook, offering beef at a ridiculously low price, and told people that he would settle Watson.—The Bench decided to dismiss the ease, the parties to pay their own costs. DISORDERLY BERAVIOUR.—Wary White, an old offender, was committed to Knutsford for a month with hard labour in default of paying a floe of 40s. and 7s. 4d. costs for dis- orderly behaviour in Highland-road on Saturday night SLEEPING OUT.—Sarnue Lydiatt was committed to Hunts- ford for seven days for sleeping in an out-building end not having any visible means of subsistence.—P.C. Bowyer stated that morning at 12.3) he visited the kftahen of the Bridge- water Company and found the prisoner asleep on a form. Witness brought him down to the police citation and when in High-street Lydiatt snatched his throat strap and ran away. He gave chase and caught him in Bridgewater. street,</p>
