        <p>A VILLAGE MAIDENS HAPPY CURE.</p>
        <p>A reporter of the "Suffolk and Essex Free Press " writes :—I met MiS3 Annie Stehloirg at 4., Cottage-row Walter Selchamp (a charming Essex village) at the door where she sat knitting. She said in a tone of thankful</p>
        <p>nese which only those who have recovered from a dreary illness can fully appreciate : " I shall be glad to tell you all about my:recovery. In April, 1898, I became I very unwell, and gradually grew worse. I could not get about much, as I hadn't any breath, and was so weak ; I took no interest in anything, and had no appetite. You see I bad to do my work, as since two years last November I have kept house for my fatheral consulted • doctor, and he told me I was suffering from aneemia and weakness He gave me medicine and I consulted him at intervals from April, 1896, to April, 1897, but I get ao better. Well, I had frequently read in the " Suffolk and Essex Free Press" the testimonials sent by people who had been cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People and I decided to try the pills, and sent directto Dr: Williams' Medicthe Company. Before I had taken one box I could get about much better,and people said they could see quite a difference in me : and so did father. I felt greatly improved ton. I have taken three boxes altogether, and they entirely cured me, for I have felt nothing of the weakness since; in fact, I have never en- joyed better health in my life,"</p>
        <p>A DEVONSHIRE SCHOOLMISTRESS INTERVIEWED AT COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>WHAT Miss Mary Ward said to a reporter of the So Nth London Press was of more than common interest. Miss Ward is one of the mistresses at St. John's College Angell-road, London, S.W. (her home address is Holm- leigh, 3, St. Brannock's Park, Ilfracombe). She had been the subject of that most distressing of female com- plaints, anaemia.</p>
        <p>"For some years," Bliss Ward explained, "I had ex. perieneed a fooling of great weakness. It was however, only during the past year or two—l am now twenty-one—that I felt myself sink</p>
