        <p>WIGAN WIN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BOLD BID MADE BY THE HOMESTERS FOR HONOURS. The football visitors at Runcorn on Saturday were the Wigan 15. Runcorn who have been experimenting with their men of late tried Mostyn and G. Woods in the threequarter line. Toe forwards were fairly strong, but they also included one or two inexperienced men. Wigan brought a capital team and it was thought before the match that tlaev would easily make rings round theit opponents. A good gate had assembled when Mr. Dillon of Warrington, appeared on the 5C.1:18 to superintend the men who had formed in the following order : Runcorn : Houghton, full back; B. West, J. Butter- worth, G. Woods, E. Mostyn, threequarter backs; Faulkner and G. Robinson, half backs ; S. Walker, Langley, Moores, Gayter, Farmer, Darlington, Bettley, and Johnson, forwards. Wigan :J. Winstauley, full back ; Rhoden. Gainey, Dwyer, Timmoney, threequarter backs ; Halliwgll, Dixon, half backs ; J. Brown, McCarthy, Rigbey, Peat, Turner, Yates, James Winstanley, and W. R. Brown. J. Brown started, Robinson making a good return to centre. Runcorn forwards rushed up and a scrimmage was ordered in the visitors 25. A free kick enabled Wigan to get to midfield where Butterworth made his mark. He punted across to the left wing and the ball was carried into safe quarters. Front a scrim- mage Faulkner again received and passed to Houghton who was in a favourable position for a drop at goal, but his kick pro ved unfruitful. A neat,bit of passing was started with good sdhoess by the borne backs, bat on its being thrown to Butterworth the latter slipped, Rhoden securing it, and passing Houghton, easily scored a try, the kick at goal being unsuccessful. The visitors re- turned Runcora's drop-out and entered the latter'e halt. Farmer kicked out, the leather coming in contact with Timinoney's face. Farmer who had followed the ball at once grassed the Wigan man and a scrimmage resulted. An anxious time was this, for Farmer was doing his utmost to score but the visitors kicked the ball dead. The Cestrians were again dangerous. The visitors made racks for midfield but Houghton stayed progress and returned the oval. Scrimmage after scrimmage took place on Wigan's lines. Runcorn seemed determined to score but Rigby got nicely away and kicked the leather to Timmoney who eluded Houghton and scored an, them try. J. Winstanley kicking the goal. From a sorimmage Faulkner and Robinson carried the oval to close quarters but wh n they passed to Butterworth he failed a,gain. The ball rolled a little distance and West gathered up quickly and scored Runcorn's first try. The goal kink failed. The home forwards then played splendidly and carried hoitilities to Wigan's half. Ex- change kicks were afterwards 'now the order, until Darlington took possession arid scored. The kick at goal being unsuccessful. Half-time =lied with Wigan Faulkner , points. Farmer started and a movement coleamdimngenbeey twdboy Robinson and shared in by. Butterworth, Walker and Woods took the oval to. within a yard of the visitors' line. A grand kick by the Wigan fullback relieved the pressure. Rhoden rushed up and dribbled nicely and scored a try. Winstanley improved the score. Houghton did some gracd work by eliding three men and getting well into his opponents quarters before he was grassed. The ball appeared to be in a safe position but Winstanley cleared the danger and Woods receiving almost let them in. Houghton was again very active and tricky, and baffled his opponents to ths delight of the spectators] West came on the scene when Brown was making tracks for the home goal, and inked into touch. Ante's- bus with a goad run took the ball to the Wigan lines where the homesters operated fpr some time. A wild pass by Butterworth almost let the visitors in. ape game became very quick and °acting, Runcorn maintaining their position in the visitors half. Farmer at length broke away and scored. J. Winstanley made a sprint a short distance, Mostyn bringing him to the ground. Walker got the oval at centre and scored. The 'kick at goal taken by Houghton being a failure. Runcorn were now only a point behind Wigan land strenuous efforts were made to.equalise. Time after time the horns men invaded the visitors territory, but there they stuck. and could not cross the line. Wigan defends] well, and kept their opponents at bay. The last few minutes of the game were watched with keen interest, the game being very lively. When time was called Runcorn were press- ing. The score was : Wigan two goals, three tries (13 points), Runcorn four tries (12 points).</p>
