        <p>WIDNES v. BROUGHTON RANGERS. I Played at Widness on Saturday, before a good gate. 1 Widnes starting against the wind, and a glaring sun. The ball was landed into touch, and Hampson made a fine kick up. The Rangers rushed forward, and a score looked imminent, when Hampsen saved. The game was oarried under the home posts, where scrimmaging en- sued. Widnes returned to mid-field, but Baty was com- pelled to kick dead. Re-commencing, Widnes rushed up, and the leather vras transferred into touch. The visitors received from the kick out,and Brennan tackled finely. Operations were now carried on in the Widnes half, where a free kick was allowed the home team. Considerable excitement was caused by 'lampoon being held up on the visitors' lines. A shot at goal was effected by Baty but failed. Continuing a splendid game of dash, Widnes continually penned the visitors to their own half. Operations were suspended for a few minutes owing to injuries to Barrett. Baty was loudly applauded for a magnificent save when the visitors appeared to be dangerous. Widnes continued to play en the aggressive, and Heslop was knocked into tonal when almost over the line. A scrimmage ensued in the visitors' ' half near the goal, when Duck broke away, but was stopped short by Baty. The Rangers now pressed near the home line, where Chapman made a fine attempt at try. Murphy kicked up, and relieved. The Broughtonians still continued on the aggrassive,and Douglas received from a scrimmage, and passed to Nevitt, who handed to Peters, but the latter'a kick was charred down. Later, Whale received from a pass, and looked like a scorer, when Baty tackled in smart style. Half-time arrived with no score. C leminson restarted the game, and Heelop returned. Pressing the game down' on the visitors, Widnes obtained a free kick, from which Baty landed a magnificent goal. Resuming, the visitors pressed, and the spectators were in a ferment owing to the numerous attempts to make matters equal. A passing movement between the Broughton threequar- ters looked exceedingly dangerous for the Widnes- lane, but Whale was knocked into touch. Douglas passed to Murphy, and he handed to Rispau but the latter was tackled. A. sure try was missed by Grace failing to see the ball in time. Play was near the visitors' goal, when Brennan performed a marvellous drop,and to the astonishment of everyone the ball sailed over the bar. Skelson was injured at this point, but was soon able to resume. Steele, for the visitors, was Prominent with a spirited run up, but was effectively tackled before becoming dangerous, Fluctuating play ensued in the home half, and Murphy made his mark, which gained ground for the "Chemicals." Rushing up, some splendid passing was engaged in by the home side, and ,Johnson looked over but the ball was ruled back. Scrimmaging took place on the line, but the ball was kicked up and Skelton was tackled beford he could re- spond. A few minutes before the final Heslop was ordered off the field for an alleged foul play. The referee's decision met with the marked disapproval of spectators. The final arrived with Widnes winners of a keenly contested game by four points to nil. The follow- ing were the teams :—Widnes: Baty, full back ; Bren- nan, Rispan. Nevitt, and Peters,three-quarters ; Murphy and Douglas, half backs; 'lampoon, Runt, Donnelly, Fieslop,Grace, Handley, Johnson, and Travies,forwards. Broughton Rangers ; Skelton, full back; Cbapraan,Duok, Whale, and Barratt,three-quarterS ; Thompson and Nel- son,half backs ; Steele,Mumberson, Blacklock, Lazenby, W. Robinson, W. P. Robinson, T. Clerninson, and S. Deakin, forwards. . .. . . .. _. .</p>
        <p>WARRINOT )14 " &amp;" V. SWINTON " I."—The above teams met in their return encounter on the Warrington around on Saturday, before 2,000 spectators, in bsauti- ful weather. Swinton were assisted by Bevan and Set- way, two of the first team players, while Warrington tried Radcliffe, a local recruit, on the right wing. The teams were composed as follows :—Warrington : Jones, full back ; Radcliffe, Hallam, Cross, and Crank, thrce quarters; Scott and Bate, half backs ; Hatton, Cunning- ham, landrev-s, Wilcock, Morrison, Royle, Fairhurst and Eden, forwards. Swinton : Simkins, full-hack ;R. Wil- kinson, T. M. Bevan, It. Eckerslev, S. B. Whitting ham, threequarters; Y. Selway and A. Williamson, half backs; F. J. Berry, T. Baker, C. Point, J. Preston, J. Dunn, J. Shepherd, J. Sharpies, and T. Woistenholine, for wards. The visitors started the game, Boyle returning well, and Cross almost scored after a good dribble.Warrington overplayed their opponents at once, and Cunningham scored from a loose rush. Scot t failed at goal. Smart work by the home backs ended in Cross getting over but his try was disallowed. Swinton rd l ieved to centre, when Bate got the leallout and handed to Scott who gave to flal!am the latter putting in a good run before pass- ing to Radcliffe,who scored a fine try. Hallam's attempt at goal failed. Bate mulled and let Swinton into the home half where Radcliffe made a daring save. Cross and Fairhurst were both prominent with fine individual efforts, and Morrilon worked well rirward. Scott had another shot at goal from Hallam's mark but failed. Radcliffe centred well and Cross ran to the line, Wilkin- son grassing him. Just before the interval whittingham made a grand dash down the touch-line, Jones checking</p>
        <p>How IS IT TlikT CIA-RICE'S BLOOD MI,CPURE h 33 Obtained such great popularity" is a (motion which haa perplexed many. The answer is, that it is unquestionably the finest Blood Purifier that science and medical skill have brought to light. Thousands of wonderful cures have been effected by it. For Scrofula, Scurvy, Eczema, Skins,and Blood Diseases, Bad Legs, Pimples and Soros of all kin( its effects are marvellous. Sold everywhere at 2s. 9d. per bottle ware of worthless imitations and substitutes.—Advt. 2)</p>
        <p>him in the heme half. Half time, Warrington Z tries ; Swinton nil. The second half was by no means so fast, the teams being on more level terms. Morrison had hard lines in not scoring for Warrington, and then a penalty was given under the posts, Cross took the kick and missed by a few inches. Play rested at half way for a time,and Bevan kicked a splendid goal for S.vinton from a mark. Radcliffe was nearly a scorer on another occasion. Jones giving him a pass in the corner. The game ended in a win for Warrington by two tries (six points) to a goal (two points). I ReNeoax v. WIGAN. (A Taus).—Played at Wigan on I Saturday. Both teanss were representative, but there was only a sparse attendance. Chesters started for Run- corn, who immediately began to press, and after fine passing Nickson and Halewood were almost in. Grad- ually Wigan forced them back, and after a lot of ex- change kicking Webster scored, but no goal secured. The interval arrived with the home team leading by one try to nil. The eecoad moiety was distinctly in favour of the visitors. Wilson dribbled half the length of the field, and Cheaters completed the try, Hai!wood failing to add the major points. Runcorn continued to press, but missed several easy openings, the final result being a draw of one try each. NORTHERN RUGBY UNION (3an DIVISION). WARRINGTON ST. MART'S T. WHITWORTH.—The above teams Ist and 2nd in league met on St. Mary's ground ,on Saturday, the home team being without Clarke and Boscovv.Deatrin and Povey filling the vacancies,the visi- tors having their strongest team. Davies v. on the toss, Whitworth starting with the sun in their eyes. Higgin- bottom returned and a scrum was formed at half way. The visitors got possession and kicked into St. Mary's quarter,where Hughes made a mark. Exchange kicking followed which resulted in a scrimmage in Whitworth's quarter. Virlaitworth were awarded a free kick,but Hig- ginbottom returned well and following up had hard lines,knocking on when in a good position. The Sainte pressed,the visitors forwards kicking across the field, but the ball was sent in touch and from the kick out the Saints rushed.the ball over the line,the visitors full back grounding the ball in the nick of time,a minor only ac- I cruing. From the kick off,Higginhottom made a mark. The ball was returned to touch at half way. Savage stopped a rush by Whitworth. The visitors' forwards again got away and Hutchinson mulling over the line, Law grounded the ball, the kick at goal failing. On restarting the ball was returned to touch near the Saints' line, but Hutchinson kicked well down the field From a scrummage the Saints' got possession and kicked down the field.quick following up by Corless preventing a return. Whitworth were penalized, but the kick was returned and Corless was tackled with the aall in his I possession. The home team then forced a minor, and from the kick out D.Warburton got possession,and after. wards passed to Higginbottom, who transferred to W. Savage and this player raced over,D.Warburton making a good attempt to add the major points. On restarting Corley; was prominent, but Whitworth were awarded a free kick. Savage ana Corlese commenced well. The Marys' forwards got away, but Law kicked to touch at half way. Taylor made his mark, and prevented Hughes putting in a return. The Saints were awarded a free kick, which was returned and Savage mulled but after- wards recovered and kicked well down the field. A re- turn was made, and Johnson was tackled before he could get rid of the ball half time was called with each team having three points. On restarting no return was I made,and Whitworth was soon defending. The visitors' forwards tried to get away, but Gregory saved. Whitworth were awarded a free kick. Warburton mak. lug a mark. Higginbottom kicked in touch near the Saints line, and from the kick out the ball was sent dead. The kick was well returned, D. Warburton kicking to touch. Play now rested in the Saints quarter, and the league leaders were dangerous, until Deakin brought re- lief; but Taylor kicked back and Higginbottom made a mark. Whitworth were again awarded a free kick,and Taylor failed at goal. The Mary's defence was severely tested during this half and Carless kicked to touch. The Saints forwards rushed away, but knocking on spoiled them. A scrimmage was formed at the Mary's 25 and getting possession, the visitors half back tricked his opponents and scored, the try not being improved. The Saints now had a turn and rushed away but a forward pass spoiled them. Higginbottom got away. but Law knocked the ball forward Warburton was conspicuous with a good run but failed to score, and after a splendid game Whitworth won by the narrow margin of three points. Final result: Whitworth 2 tries. Warrington St. Marys one try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>JUNIOR _LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR _LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCRAM RESERVE y. RUNCORN RECREATION.—This fixture took place at Bowdon. The home te,m was strongly represented, while the Recs. had the assistance of one sr two old Runcernians. The Recs. tried hard, but were clearly out-played, and when the time came were defeated by 21 points to nil. RUNCORN HORNETS V CROSFIELD'S SOAP WORKS.— Played at Runcorn. The Hornets kicked off. Midfield play ensued,the visitors' forwards rushing the pack anti using their weight with advantage. The visitors' half backs getting the ball, began passing, but they did not gain any advantage and were soon repulsed. The home team then attacked for some time,the forwards sticking to their work well. Half time—no score. Soon after</p>
        <p>the:restart a forward kicked the ball from midfield, and the Hornets' back thinking it had gone dead allowed the visitors to score. The kick at goal failed. After this Dutton dropped a goal. The home men hay. lug a peaalty, Deane dropped a .goal. Final—Soap Works, five points ; Hornets, two points, LATCHFORD VICS. T. PARYER-STREET JIINIORS.—PIayei at Latchford on Saturday, and ended in a win for the home team by three goals, three tries (15 points) to ne. thing. Tries were scored by Pord,Southern,and Wilkinv son,Ford converting one, and goals were dropped by Jacques and Wilson.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>_ LANCAHIRE SENIOR COMPRTITIOW—A meeting of tke committee of this competition was held at Manchester on Tuesday night, when three charges against playerel were heard. Williams, the Salford captain, was MB. ended for a L.rtnight for striking an opponent against; Oldham last Saturday, and Dwyer, of Wigan, was mew pended for a week for charging an Oldham opponent oni January 21. Price, of the Tyldesley (A) team was elm. pended for a month for kicking an opponent a;.,v.ainst Rockdale Hornets. Salford had two points deducted fee playing R. Thomas, an ineligible man.</p>
      </div>
