      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ACTION AGAINST MESSRS. CROSFIELD</head>
        <p>ACTION AGAINST MESSRS. CROSFIELD</p>
        <p>AND SONS.</p>
        <p>SOLICITORS AND THEIR COSTS.</p>
        <p>- - -- In the Chancery "Court of Lancashire, sitting at Man- chester on Monday, before Vice-Chancellor Hall, the suit of Villain v. Joseph Crosfield and Sons was called. In this action in which the plaintiff, a French- man, living in this country, claimed certain sums of money from the defendants,who are soap manufacairerS at Warrington, the parties came to terms before the trial, and the defendants paid to the plaintiff a sent agreed upon. The case now came before the Court me an application by Mr. Grant, on behalf of the plaintiff's solicitors, for a declaration as against the defendanta that they were entitled to a lien upon the money agreed to be paid to the plaintiff for their costs.charges,and ex. penses.Mr. Grant submitted that the defendants had paid the plaintiff behind the backs of his solicitors, and that the latter,by virtue of their lien upon any sum whicla might be recovered by the action,were now entitled to enforce pvement of their costs from the defendants. He admitted the actual notice of the lien was n)t given to the defendants until they had paid the plain' iff, but ar- gued that the lien of the plaintiff's solicitors was in ex. istence when the defendants agreed to ;say the amount of the damaires,and that the defendants must be deemed to have had notice of it.—Mr. Mabeely, for the defend- ants,pointed out that there was Lo allegation that hie clients had acted fraudnlently, and submitted that be- fore the present application could succeed there must be clear evidence of collusion to deprive the solicitors of their costs.—The Vice-Chancellor stopped counsel in hitt argument, and said the action had been compromised before judgment in a bona fide manner, not for the pur- pose of defeating the solicitor's claim for costs, and not after notice of the lien for costs had been given. The application must, therefore, be dismissed with costs. Were he to decide otherwise it would be to say in effect that no action could be compromised before judgment unless the party saw the solicitors for the other side and discharged their claim for costs.—Mr. Grant asked for and obtained leave to appeal.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. GEORGIE G. BUSS EY AND CO. have issued their vrell-known Cricketers' Diary for 1898, this being itg h year. The neat little book contains the results ot all first-class matches last year, batting and bowling averages, records and all manner of interesting and use. 1121 information. There are also memorandum pages for each day in the month. The price of the diary is 6d., and is well worth the money.</p>
      </div>
