      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>DISORDERED LIVER.</head>
        <p>DISORDERED LIVER.</p>
        <p>and FEMALE AILMENTS.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL SALE.SIX MILLION BOXES</p>
        <p>141130xes:r9id.,111s.1 ltd., and Vs. 9d. 'each, twith_a_lll °directions. The Is. _lid. Boarsontains 56;:ri1ls.</p>
        <p>PREPARED ONLY BY TKEPROPRIETOR- THOMAS BEECHAM, St. Helens, Lancashir e.</p>
        <p>CLAIM AGAINST THE VICAR OF DARESBURY. AN ALLEGED VICIOUS DOG.</p>
        <p>Before his 11.on—eur Judge Ffoulkes at the Warrington County Court on Thursday last week a claim made by W illiam Green, Tetley, Sheffield, late under-gardener in the employ of Mr. W. C. Jones, of the Oaklands, Preston Brook,against the Rev. H. T. Clark, vicar of Daresbury, for £5O damages for injuries alleged to have been re- ceived by being bitten by the defendant's dog on August 22nd was heard. Mr. Arthur Browne represented the plaintiff, and Mr. Collingwood Hope, barrister (in- structed by Messrs. Greenall and Buckton) appeared for the defendant. Mr.A.Browne in stating the ease said that on Sunday, I August 22nd, his client and a friend named Chandler I were walking along the high road from Preston Brook I I to Daresbury,when they met two domestic servants from I the house of the Vicar of Daresbury in company with two dogs. One of the animals—a sable collie dog—flew at the plaintiff and bit his hand between the thumb and the first finger. He proceeded to Dr. Bower and had the wound cauterized. The plaintiff continued at work for some time, but owing to the wound becoming serious he went to Dr. Carruthers, of Halton, who advised him to stop work. He went to his home at Totley,near Shef- field, and was attended by Dr. Fex. Owing to his absence from work the plaintiff was discharged from the Oaklands. Preston Brook, and had since been unable to get employment. It was a custom not to employ gardeners who had not been in their last situation 12 months,and as the plaintiff Was only four months at tne Oaklands he would be obliged to find employment at some nurseries at a reduced wage. William Green ,under gardener, Totley, near Sheffield, stated that he was formerly employed at the Oaklands, Preston Brook, and on Sunday, August 22nd, he was walking on the road at Daresbury when he met the two servants of the Vicarage in company with two dogs. The collie dog flew at him, and bit his hand. It also flew at a friend named Chandler,who was in company with wit- ness. The schoolmaster of Daresbury (Mr.Wright),was ' also coming in a contrary direction, and the dog flew at him. Witness went to Dr. Bower, and had the wound attended to. He wrote to the Vicar, the Rev, H. T. Clark,but did not receive a reply. On the following Monday night witness met the same two servants from the Vicarage, and had a conversation with them. He went to see the Vicartand asked him what he was going to do in the matter. The Vicar said he was not going Is destroy the dog, and stated that the dog had not bitten anyone else. Witness reminded the Vicar that the dog had bitten him (the Vicar) and the Vicar afterwards admitted it. The Vicar asked him what he wanted, and witness told him that he did not want anything so long as the dog was destroyed, and his hand went on all right. The Vicar, however, gave witness five shillings, and he kwitness) afterwards gave it to Chandley as compensation for the damage to Isis trousers. For some time witness did not feel any ill effects from the bite but it did not heal up properly. He suffered a lot of pain from the hand, and on the 23rd October, a blister sud- denly appeared above the wound. He consulted Dr. Carruthers, of Halton, who gave him the certificate pro- duced, and advised him to leave his work for a short time. Witness went to his home at Totley, and was attended by Dr. Fox. He was at home a month, and lost his situation at the Oaklands in consequence of being away. Be was receiving a wage of £1 a week at the time he was bitten by the dog. He had never been able to get another situation. There was a custom that unless gardeners had a twelve menths'character from their last empleyer,they were unable to get another place—Cross- examined by Mr. Hope : He paid four visits to Dr. Fox. He did not say to Dr. Carruthers that two weeks previ- ously he had been bitten by a dog, nor did he admit to the doctor that he had been bitten on ether occasionst He was certain that the Vicar of Daresbury told him tha. he had been bitten by the dog. George Chandler, under gardener at the Oaklands said he was present with the plointiff on the date named. He saw the dog fly at the plaintiff. He afterwards saw that the dog had bitten a deep wound which was bleed- ing fast. The dog also got hold of his (witness's) trousers and tore them. Witness went with the plaintiff to see the Vicar on August 27th. . _ .</p>
        <p>James Rafter, vaninan in the employ of Mr. Monks, grocer, Warrington, and living at Ellesmere-street, said he had occasion to deliver goods at the Vicarage, and he remembered the collie dog fgetting hold ot his jacket sleeve, and attempting to bite him. It would be in June or July of last year. It flew at him on three occasions in the presence of the servants. For the defence Mr. Collingwood Hope did not dispute that the dog bit the man, and therefore the question turned on the two points, whether the defendant bad knowledge of the dog's viciousness and the amount of damages sustained. The plaintiff worked from the 22nd of August—the date on which he was bitten—until the ' 3lst ot October. He received his usual wages up to that time. The doctors who saw him had not been called by the plaintiff, but they would prove that the bite was a scratch,and nothing more than a superficial scratch. It was only on the 26th October that the plaintiff went to the doctor with a blood blister on his hand. When opened by the doctor the ordinary black serum escaped: For some reason or another the plaintiff told Dr. Carruthers that he had been bitten within the last fort- night,and the doctor thought it wise to give the certifi- cate whichlhad been produced. If the doctor had known that it had occurred eight weeks previously he would not have advised the plaintiff to leave work. The doctors failed to trae.e any connection between the formation of the blood blister and the wound of eight weeks previously. With respect to the loss of wages he submitted that no satisfactory evidence had been given with respect to the custom which was alleged to prevail. There was no evidence of damages for which they were liable. _ .</p>
        <p>Rev. H. Taplow Clark, vicar of Daresbury, said his collie pup was nine months old at the time the plaintiff was bitten. He was away from home at the time. The , plaintiff called on him on the 27th August, and showed his hand and said it had healed, but it had been bad. It had tbe appearance of the scratch of a pin. Witness had never been bitten by the dog, and had never stated that it had bitten him several times. Witness gave plaintiff Si. but witness said he did not want anything as he had been bitten three times previously by a deg and once by a at, and did not think much of it. Be had parted with the dog when he received a letter from the plaintiff. —Cross-examined by Mr. Browns: Witness did not re- member giving a boy who had been bitten by the dog, 21. ed. last summer. He could not remember that any- boy complained of being bitten by the dog. He had strictly forbidden the servants to take out the dog when he was away from him. Be never heard about the dg flying at the witness Rafter. He had never heard of the dog having bitten anybedy. His Honour closed the case after having heard this witness. He said that the defendant could not be res- ponsible for what the dog did in his absence. The evidence of the plaintiff showed that the servants wanted to pay the amount of damage, because they had been forbidden to take the dog out. He must therefore give judgement for the defendant. He quits agreed with what Mr. Browne had said that it was a pity the law was not altered with respect to the liability of owners of dogs. He had not often been called, upon to decide in such cases.</p>
      </div>
