      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL AND WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE LANCASHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL AND WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>We see from the proceedings of the Lancashire County Council on Thursday, that the report of the Parliamentary Committee of the Council was approved, in which a reference was made to the Warrington Electric Lighting Order. It appears from the report that the Town Clerk of Warrington has agreed to give the County Council the ordinary clause for the protection of the County and Hundred bridges within the Borough,and in referring to this matter the committee state " within such area (Warring- ton) is the county bridge known as Warrinizton ton bridge which is narrow and incon- venient, and in regard to which negotia- tions are now proceeding with the Corporation of Warrington for the purpose of widening or re- building it, as well as other bridges repairable by thelinhabitants within:the Hundred of West Derby." In the report of the Main Roads and Bridges Com- mittee of the County Council no reference whatever is made to Warrington Bridge or to the visit of the deputation from the Warrington Corporation which waited upon the committee at Preston.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S,A. SOCIETY,</p>
        <p>A well-attended meeting Of this society was held in Wycliffe Church on Sunday afternoon. Mr. C. Eccles, of Warrington, presided, and in a short address, he spoke on "Christ and Religion." He said they might have different conceptions of Christ. but in one particu- lar they were all agreed, and that was that they could never think of Christ as belonging to any particular church or any particular denomination, nor associate him with the priestly garb or anything appertaining to that office. Christ's religion took more of the universal order, and they must not confuse Christianity with the church nor Christ with the Church. It was only by following Christ that they could attain the goal which they were all making for.—Mr. Whalley, of Manchester, delivered the address, and enlarged upon the subject which had been introduced by the Chairman.—A solo was very efficiently song by Miss F. Booth. The scripture lesson was read by Mr. John Atkinson.—The Rev. Jobn Yonge intimated that in consequence of the innovation of the church, the services wouli be conducted for at least three Sundays in the Winwick-street Co-operative Hall.</p>
        <p>The Board of Trade returns issued on Monday show that the imports for January amounted to £39,916,491, compared with £39.775,688 for January last year.show- mg an increase of £140,823. The exports for January were £19,231,404, against £19,786,236 for the corre• sponding month last year, showing a decrease of £554,032.</p>
      </div>
