      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Reiars to tbe Obitor.</head>
        <p>Reiars to tbe Obitor.</p>
        <p>_ _ TOCKTON IMAM CLOCK. Sir,—Would you kindly allow me through your columns to say a few words in regard to a oaragraph which appears in the" Stockton Heath Parish Magazine" in regard to the church clock.lt may interest the parish- ioners of Stockton Heath to know that since the clock was put up 23 years ago it has not cost the wardens one penny excepting for new ropes, and during that time I have taken it to pie-es and cleaned it several times free of eharge,the last Liao being about two years ago when I put on new roes. Po do tbis the pulleys were taken down, when they, as ell as the clock, were thoroughly cleaned, and, turther th • 'hat, for a week after I left it went quite correctly.—Y furs truly. JOHN HALLOWS. Rose Lea, Stocktm Heath, February 9, 1893.</p>
        <p>THE WIDENING OF BRIDGE-STREET. you kindly enable me through your columns to continue the remarks I desired to make in reference to this question at the meeting of the Town Council on day evening Tut last, when I was ruled out of order by : the Mayor,—and just let me say without wishine, in the eightest degree to appear distespectful to the "Mayor, who, I must admit, very reluctantly interf ?red, that after the admission that some portion of the minutes ie- lated to the question I consider I was perfectly justified in introducing any matters relevant thereto. . . .</p>
        <p>-t I.* My whole in en ion and object throughout was simply to explain to the Council the unreasonable delay which had taken place in carrying out the widening of the por- tion of Bridge-street from Friars-gate to Rylends-street, and to the continually repeated complaints made to me and others, not only by the owners and tenants but by the public generally, to say nothing of the oft expressed dissatisfaction of many members of the Corporation that they should be kept in ent;re ignorance of what was going on in reference to important puolic matters. Both owners and tenants have for a period of two or three years been kept in a state of trying suspense and uncer- tainty as to what they were to do, the anxiety of the tenants in particular, whose livings entirely depend on their businesses and the chances of being able to secure ' equally desirable premises, may be better imagined than described. It is quite true that the Corporation has obtained com- pulsory powers to purchase the properties but that is no reason why lanelords or tenants should be unjustly dealt with, most of whom I venture to say would much prefer to remain as they are than be compelled to sell at almost any price.and I put it to any one of the Commit- tee empowered to negotiate for the purchase how far he would in a similer case of his own property he disposed to consider anc thing he thought unfair to nimseif. I think I know • eiyone of them too well to have any difficulty in an,wering such a question. I do not for one moment presume to question the right of the com- mittee to refuse to agree to exoraitant demands fur any property but, on the other band, lam ready to support their action in resisting any attempt to obtain more that what is just; at the same time I meintain that it is also the duty of such committee to tieat with respect the offers made to them and give everyone the opportunity, if he so desires, of 'consulting them with We view of modifying the terms and if possible arriving at an amic- able settlement and thus avoid the heavy expenses which an arbitration must entail on either the owners or ratepayers. Instead of this usual and proper curse be- ing ado, ted the committee I contend have treated every- body with the utmost contempt and discourtesy, never ondescending to furnish information to anyone but allowing it to go forth to the public, aesisced by corres- pondents through the press,that the owners and tenants 71 every case have been guilty of aishonest attempts to rettort from the ratepayers four or five times the value of the properties. Now permit me in justice to myself as a trustee for one of the properties in question, not only to deny the accusation as far as the claim made by myself and Co- trustees is con erned which was basea on the valuation of the meet exper:eneed and competent valuers in War- rington but also to deny in the most emphatic manner Councillor H. Rooerts' insinuation that I am acting, or have ever acted,as an agent of the property, or that I have been actuated in the slightest degree by any mer- cenary motive in the action I was taking, and I appeal to any member of the Corporation, or to anyone who knows me in Warrington,to say if they ever knew me to take advantage of my pesition as a member of the Town</p>
        <p>Council to be-nefit m;ss'elf. Sly endeavours have always been to promote to the be A of my 5b111t7 the interests of the ratepayers and in this ca ss Se satisfy those who con- sidered They were bring unfairly deslt with, and if I am not,is a trustee,entitled to speak on the subject I should like to know who is,and I was surprised that Councillor Roberts, of all ot-aera, should have so far forgotten him- self as to prefer a charge which he was quite unable to substantiate. I never think it worth while to notice the remarks of a certain member who from the moment be entered the Council was impressed with the idea that he knew everything and who after continually jumping up like a " Jack in the box" to " pints " of order and magnaai rnously trying to teach old and experienced member- what they ought to &amp;Aries to seek for applause outside So I dismiss him altogether from my mind.—Yours, Warrington, 3rd Febtaary, 1898.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>THOMAS H. SUTTON.</head>
        <p>THOMAS H. SUTTON.</p>
        <p>SABBATH OBSERVANCE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Kindly permit us to have a few parting words with R.G.L. as our final contribution to this contro- versy. As we do not wish to be considered ungrateful for any mercies that may come in our way we desire, through you, Mr.Editor,to thank him for directing public atten- tion to what he calls our want of familiarity with the Bible. This, was hardly needed, however, as we have never yet professed to be experts in the Divine Oracles. Neverthele3s we have been readers of the Bible all our life. But having been brought up from our childhood • I regard the whole moral law as of universal applica- tion it never occurred to us to search the scriptures for proof that the fourth article of that law had been abol- ilhed long ago. We have often read the passages upon '---Which your correspondent relies to prove his contention that the Sabbath law no longer exiets,but somehow their never struck us as having the meaning he attaches to them. But this failure to comprehend a most important trutn, if truth it be, would probably be due to oar lack of the critical acumen which distinguishes R.G.L. In his last letter, now before us, your correspondent with great candour and fairness says,"We do not dispute nor have we disputed that God gave the Mosaic Law of which the fourth Commandment ;si only a portion." We are thankful for this admission, for it proves that its author is,"ke ourseives,a believer in the Divine origin of the fourth commandment, while at the same time it strengthens our view that only the Divine being could set it aside. But the words we have quoted are only , part of a sentence:the remaining portion of which reads thus ; " but we do assert most emphatically that that law is ended." The outspokenness of this proposition is truly retreshing,and the statement is either true or false. If it is true then, not only the fourth, but all the other commandments have disappeared:if it:is false how utterly untenable is the position which R.G.L.has taken up. Fo- rtunately for us we are net left to our own unaided efforts to decide its truth or falsehood ; the Scriptures are at hand to guide our decision. Turning to the seventeenth, eighteenth,and nineteenth verses of the fifth chapter of the Gospel by Matthew we find that the statement is ab- solutely at variance with the teaching of Christ. la the verses referred to we read, " Think not that I (Jesus)am come to destroy the law,or the prophets; I am not come "9 to destroy, but to fulfil. For Lverily, I unto you,till heaven and earth pass,one jot or one tittle shall in no wise pass from the law, till all be fulfilleff, Who- boever therefore shall break one of these least c,mmand- ments,and shall teach men so,he shall be called the least in the kingdom of heaven." We think that this plain and unequivocal declaration of Christ should be suffi- cient to establish the permanence of the fourth Com- mandment as a jot or tittle of the law which shall last till heaven and earth have passed away. But having stated in one place that the law is at an end your correspondent in a strange freak of inconsi, tency, tells us in another "that nine of the Command- ments are obligatory on those who are under the law of Christ," and that because Christ reiterated their. strange reasoning this. We admit at once that Christ did reiterate most of the Commandments in anwer to • questions addressed to Him, but in doing so He was not formulating a new code, but merely emphasising the old law then in existence. But surely His omission to re- 'tate the Fourth Commandment is no proof that he con- sidered taat Commandment as dead, seeing that it formed a part of the law which He declared to be iii- mutable for all time. The law of capital punishment is on the Statute Book of England and will remain there until repealed by competent authority. But its efficacy does not in acy way depend upon repetition as the man- slayer knows to his terrible cost. And if Jesus did not restate the Fourth Commandment His attitude tower is it was to say the least,very remarkable. Not only did Be rest from His labours on the Sabbath Day, but we find Him joining with His countrymen in its spiritual observ- ance as well. All this surely indicates that He attached great importance to it as a part of the Law of God. Nor do we Ind anything in His conduct or teaching to war- rant us in believing that Be ever intended to set it aside We argue, therefore,that the whole moral law continues in force as a schoolmaster to bring us to Christ, who is “the end ot the law for righteousness to every one that belie"eth." Were we to be deprived of that law we should have no knowledge of sin and, consequently, no desire for a Savour. We have now done with dal controverßT, but before we lay down our pen we would thank R.G.L. for the manner ia which he performed his part of it. At the outset he displayed some of the disagreeable features which usually accompany the discussion of religious topics, but as time wore on better thoughts prevailed. -.lf he has not converted us to his views we gratefully acknowledge that his enunciation of them has t:Rik marked by fairness and moderation.—Yours trials?, lON A.</p>
        <p>Cosastr. —Cement for broken articles. 6d, and is. P. .3. ;..oisleyrr. . Home and abroa4.-4avt.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL NOTES.</p>
        <p>Never in the history of the club was Runcorn kaown to be in such low water as it is at present. This year the spell of ill-luck has clung to them for over ten woks and from all appearances it is likely to remain till the close of the season. What they are going to do in the Cup Competitions one would very much like to know. Toe players themselves are confident of doing something, but when and where they have not yet de- cided. The committee seem to feel thei: position very acately,for they are practically the same committee as were in power when the t)am won championship honours two years ago. They are determined to get some sort of a team that can play together,and they are at pr:sent experimenting. Fresh lads are being tried ' every week, and while agreeing with the committee in trvinrg, to do their best, I not think one trial suffi- cient to prove a man's competency or incompetency.</p>
        <p>There does not seem any possibility of winning any more League matches unte is it be the one with War- rington. The latter are pretty much in the same boat as Runcorn although the Lancastrians have a point or two more to their credit. The match which is looked upon as one of the best on the fixture card is already creating a considerable amount of interest and Runcorn after their display on Saturday against Wigan have fa- vonrable anticipations that they can rise to the occasion and win at least another match and that against their friends,the Warringtonians.</p>
        <p>It was Wigan's first encounter with Runcorn on Sat- urday. True they cams with great etpectations of winning but when they meet again I think their expec- tations will be greatly modified, after Saturday's show. The game was a splendid one to watch and never have the home spectators been treated to a finer spectacle. From the start to the close of the game the homesters held the upper hand. The way they kept Wigan penned in their quarters was marvellous, and if one had not been present one could hardly have believed it.</p>
        <p>True, it was not due to the threequarters that Runcorn played such a grand game. Mostyn and G. Woods two A team men appeared not to realise their positions, whilst Butterworth during the first twenty minutes of the game missed several opportunities. West played better than he has done for some time. The half-backs did some fine work and made the openings for the three- quarter line which might for what came of them have been ignored altogether. Too much cannot be said of the forwards for it was due to them that Runcorn in- vad-.d tho vriit•+rs q lartfirs el often. floughtoh deserves all praise for besides being fullback, at times he assisted the threequarter backs.</p>
        <p>Something must be said of the result of the game viz*, Wigan 13 points to Runcorn 12. This 5'1107:4 that the Cestrians had the hardest work in scoring tries and if they had known a little about the art of goal kicking the game would have been theirs. Out of four tries scored not a goal was kicked. Wigan who only scored three tries secured two goals. It is exceedingly "hard lines" for Runcorn to have the most of the game and score most of the tries and then not to be the winners after all. But perhaps this will teach them the lesson that the at of kicking goals is as much needed on a football field as anything else.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>THE MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>It is pleasing to chronicle the unqualified success of Tuesday's concert in the Parr Hall, given by the Warrington Musical Society, both from a musical point of view and in regard to the public patronage ar,corded it. A. most interesting miscellaneous pro- gramme was performed by one and all concerned in such style as to arouse considerable enthusiasm on the part of the audience,the applause being at times deafening in its insistent vehemence. The most important item in which the Society's united forces were concerned was the contata "The feast of Adonis," by Adolph Jensen, a singularly tuneful and pleasing composition of a pastoral character, in which three ladies of the chorus—Mrs. Pearson; Mrs. Dobson and Miss Jeans—were heard in the principal parts. The excellent singing of all three in the concerted music and the ability and taste with which Mrs. Pearson interpreted the solo pass- ages (almost all of which fall to the lot of the soprano principal) fully justified the Musical Society in the experiment of putting to the front vocalists taken from the ranks of its members, and we hope to record further successes in the future in the line of this new departure. The chorus singing ' in the cantata'was,like that of the three ladies,both ' bright and effective, a refreshing variety of light and shade in tempo and volume of tone, as well as good precision in taking tip the parts, being noticeable. The Spinning Chorus from " The Flying Dutchman "and The Pilgrim's Chorus from " Tann- hauser," gave opportunity to the collective body of female and male voices respectively to distinguish themselves, and although the .male voice .Chorus is the more grandly impressive of the two, there was a freshness-and sparkle about the ladies' chorus which entitled them to an equal division of the honours wan in both performances.. The viva- cious chorus "The Gallant Troubadour" completes the list of the choral nambers,to the successful ren- dering of all which the really excellent playing of the society's orchestra largely contributed. The or- chestra further dealt with the difficulties of the " Peer Gynt" suite (Grieg), in an admirable manner, and although in the first movement especially there were passages for the violins which were somewhat obscure, there was so much that was praiseworthy that the unusual demand of the audience for a re- petition of the concluding movement was not alto- gether surprising. The second and third numbers of the suite received undoubtedly the finett treat- ment, and the stringed instrumentalists who are here solely concerned deserved full credit for a highly picturesque performance. Mrirank 11.Cross- ley appeared at this concert in the dual capacity of conductor and solo violinist, the Romance in G (Beethoven) for violin,and orchestra, and two other items(a)Arie(Durantel6S4-1755)and(b) "Kujawiak" (Bohm) being set down to his name. All of these received from Mr. Crossley an adequate and musi- cianly interpretation, though there was throughout a noticeable thinness in the volume of the tone pro- duced. And we are not sure that the energy which Mr. Crossley. always throws into the onerous task of conducting did not on this occasion account by mere physical exhaustion Zor a certain lack of verve in the" Rujawiak "at least, which, along with the arie, was performed in the second part of the pro-, grainnie. Some Irish sings were charmingly sung by Miss Morence atbiCh tier fine contralto, voice and aniniated styfelio eminently fitted her to render effectively. The Meister Glee singers made their second appearance in Warrington at this coat rcert'ind, as on; the last occasion, their marvellously: 1, Pe. 'Tect' concerted singing was listened to with rapt attention, and the conclusion of each ,pleeti grilete with a storm of applause and clamourings for addi-; tional items. Mr: Webster Norcrossand Mr. Gregory Hest, both sang songs in their own inimitable style. but particular mention should be made of the latter's, rendering of the pathetic song " River of Dart " a touching, little story fittingly set to mug&amp; vocalised with masterly ability. Mr. Caldwell and ably officiated at the organ, and Mr. F. W. Reynolds accompanied the songs and Solos with his usual good taste. - .</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON AORICOITCTRE AT STRICTTON. The second of n series of lectures on agriculture Wd9 given by Mr. W. Edwards,lecturer on agricul- tura, appointed by tho County Council of Cheshire, in the 6choolrcoca,oo :londay evening. Mra,Baster CC, occupied the chair, and the subject was "Arti" ficial manures,their origin,value,ancl adulteration." There wai a large number of farmers' and othetis in- te :ested in agriculture present. The lecturer treoited the subject in a thoroughly'practical manner. ' The lecturer strongly advised all farmers to organise, and to pracure the ingredients at the lowest pos. able price, arid not be liable to adulteration. At the conclusion, questions were asked, to which the lec- turer rtplied. A hearty vote of thanks was given to him for his able and instructive lecture,and a simi- lar compliment was given to tbe chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>SPIRITUALISM.</head>
        <p>SPIRITUALISM.</p>
        <p>At the Spiritualist Society's meeting in the Aca- demy-street Temperance Hall on Sunday afternoon and evening there were very good audiences,especi- ely in the evening, when the room was crowdsd. The President made feeling reference to the "pass- ing on "of one of their members, whose remains were interred in the cemetery before the afternoon meeting. Mr. Ernest Marklevv, of Manchester, oc- cupied the platform, and at each meeting took questions from the audience as the bases of his re- marks. On Monday evening he gave an address on "Spiritualism, a Science and a Religion," claiming for the system recognition as both a science and a religion. At the close of his address questions were invited, when one gentleman well-known in local religious circles challenged some of Mr. Marklew's statements and a lively discussion of about three- quarters of an hour's duration ensued.</p>
        <p>CUTICL RN REMEDIES are the most wonderful skin cures, blood purifiers, and humour remedks ever com- pounded. They may be used en the youngest infant and most delicate invalid with gra" ;Eying and unfailing success. Warm baths, with Cr/Timms Soar, gentle anointings with CuTrcustA,purest of emollient skin cur: , followed by f.-11 doses of Cunctras. RRSOLVENT. greatest of blood pir'fiers and humour cures, will afford instant relief and point to a speady and permanent care in the most torturing and disfiguring of itching, burning, and scaly sk4n yid se-'p tw-noe,s whpr. .1. fr</p>
