      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, .</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, .</p>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DIS min 2 COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held in the Board room at th.‘ Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday afternoon. C. B. F. Borron, J.P., C.C., presided, and there were also present : Dr. Patterson, Messrs. W. Coiling- wood, J. W. Ernm,,tt, J.P., P. McMahon, J. Simpson,W. Thompson, T. Ormand, E. T. Evans, J. Siee, with Mr. W. W. Shir:ey (clerk), Mr. Brierley (surveyor), and Mr. A. Bowes (assistant s voycr).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>LANCASEILLE COUNTY COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The term for Borron, the County Councillor for the Newton division was elected.expires in March. Notices have already been issued for the election of a gentleman to fill the vacancy, and all propositions must be deposited by the 23rd nst, at the Town Hall, Earlestown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>, WESLRY.LN HOYE MISSIONS.</head>
        <p>, WESLRY.LN HOYE MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a meetiog to further the cause of the Wesleyan Home Missions was held in the Brunswick Wesleyan Chapel,Earlestown. There was a fair attendance and addresses were delivered by Mr. J. H. Goodman, and the Rev. P. R. P 4143- more. A collection vras made in aid of the funds. CONCERT AT NEWTON. On Monday night, at the Town Rah, Newton, a well attended meeting was held under the auspices of the Educational department of the Leigh Friendly Co-operative Society. Mr. W. T. Prescott presided, and an address on the subject of " Co- operation" was given by Mr. T. Ainscough. A musical programme was given by a number of qualified artistes from Leigh, and Mr. Metcalfe supplied the humour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>VULCAN BAND OF HOPE.</head>
        <p>VULCAN BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening an entertainment under the auspices of the above was given in the Vulcan Schoolroom. Mr. G. E. Warburton presided,and an address on temperance matters was given by Mr.W. Pearson. An interesting programme of songs, reel- tations,violin solos, Ste., was given by members and friends of the Earlestown Sons of Temperance, and the gathering was most enjoyable.</p>
        <p>CONVOrtSAZIONE AT BARLms.rowx. The members of the Young Men's Bible Class con- nected with St. John's Church Sunday School, held their second annual conversazione in the Mission Earlestown, on Wednesday evening. There were about 1C) present, and the proceedings com- prised games and dancing. Messrs. J. Warburton and W. Pierpoint acted as M C's, and Mr. J. Pep- low's band supplied the music. Dancing commenced at 8.30, and was kept up with spirit until next morning. During the evening several humorous songs were given by Mr. John fiuddleston.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH OF A CHILD.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF A CHILD.</p>
        <p>The police have had reported to them the death of Cie four months' old daughter of Joseph D.Lonce, of 10, Newton-terrace, Earlestown,which took place very suddenly on Wednesday afternoon. It appears that the child had be m healthy since birth; but about three weeks ago it was vaccinated. The mother took it to bed about 12 o'clock at noon, apparently all right, and about 2.30 thinking it had slept rather long,she went to look at it and noticed it looked ill. She brought it downstairs into the kitchen, and bathed its head with warm water, and afterwards went for Mrs. Leatherbarrow, a neigh- bour, who came in and took the child, and it died immediately. Dr. Valentine was sent for, and he came about 4 o'clock. The doctor expressed the opinion that the cause of death was convulsions, probably through vaccination.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SID ACCIDENT TO A CIISLD.</head>
        <p>SID ACCIDENT TO A CIISLD.</p>
        <p>_ I On Tuesday afternoon, a boy named John Lilmas 5 years of age, son of John Lomas, greengrocer, of Viaduct-street, Earlestown, wet with a very, serious injury through being kicked by a horse be- longing to his father. The horse, which has a wicked temper was in the stable, and the boy who was in the-place playing about, is supposed to have touched the horse, which struck out and kicked him on the forehead. When the lad was found he was, unconscions and bleeding from the wound. Or. Mounsey was sent for, who on making an examine- tioni said the ca•ie was a hopeless one, and ordered the'boy's rernqval to the Infirmary at Liverpool. Information was received on Thursday afternoon that there was no improvement in the boy's con- dition, the skull being fractured. HOPE OF EittLIISTOWN LOOP-% LO G.T.' _ • •</p>
        <p>At the Co-operative Hall on Monday 'evening a good gathering was present at the above meeting. Bro. T. Cutler presided,anel two candidates were ad- mitted to menalieiship. Bro. Nicholson reported that a member bad Promised to present a book prize to the person who secures the most members . next quarteT. The evening being the ending of the quer- ter,the lotts. arepeicaderbeieorditryietating that 121 'mem 'eriwere in good standing, an increase oz the quarter of Atx.::-F The P.S. etated that the lodge had a good balance in hand on the quarter,the mar- shal afterwards presenting bistreport, The D.S.J.T. said that„thejetriple-bs,Jiluiran increase of 21 on the quarthOnd that theie Nitta a good balance. Fire representatives were appolWed. for District Lodge, the election of ofßzers afterwards taking place as follows :--.0.T,8r0. T. Cutler; V.T,Sister R. Warbur- ton ; L. secretary, Bro. B. Barker; F.secretary,Bro J. Taylor; treasurer, Bro.J. Warburton; P.C.T, Williams ; chaplain, Bro.W.Nicholson; marshall,Bro. Kirkham ; D3l, Sister Jenkinson; guard.Bro.Wake- field ; sentinel, B. Davies ; A. secretary, Sister L. Hall; pianist, Bro. W. Willis. Various com- mittees were appointed, and thanks were tendered to tbe installing,ofilcers,and a very interesting even- ing was then brought to a close.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S ENTERTAINMENT AT E.Antmsrowx. On Tuesday,evening on the invitation of Hr. and Mrs. i. IV; &amp;matt, 225 children over • the age of seven years, drawn from the Brunswick Wesleyan, Earlestown District, Forgave., and St. Mary and St.,John's mixed school' s were present at an enter- taihment.,given in the Viaduct Institute, Earles- town. the invitation to the children also included their parents, so that \vith the teaching staff from the schools named, the•wrinisters of religion -and other friendly:, there were :altogether, about 500 present. So. far M. ME be gathered the object WAS to encobrage the children in habits of clean- liness as conducive td health, regular attendance at. school, and , goo Leouduct, and the,aelection of 'those privileged to attend the entertainment, was left to the discretion of the head masters and mistressee 'of the -sohools, the guiding principles being as stated above, and a certain number of marks attained in this direction'entutled such children to the privilege. For the edification of,the youngsters and their parents a first clitss.pro-' 1 gramme had been g)t together, thEi various items- being most efficiently Subrnitted by capable adistst who hailed chiefly from Wolverhampton. 7, he Earlestown Prize Band opened the proceedings 'with' the overture" La Favoric rt.'" and later on they'sub- mittad " Gems of Victorian elidy," and" lnspirja- tion." Miss Osborn e Raynor sang," for ning ; and "The' Dull Plaid Shawl," in her inimitable Hair' style, the excellency of the rendition calling forth a recall on each-occasion. A ventriloquial sketch by Mr. Harry Leslie caused intense amusement and was cleverly given, the songs "The Old Brigade," 1 and" Daddy," beingintrodaced. The humour Of the evening was the capable betide of Mr., , ton, whose contributions were thoroughly enjoyed and of course re-demanded,. An item hi the Pro- gramme was an address by Dr. Thompson, wne directed his remarks to the parents and children showing the invaluable advantages which would accrue from the observance of the elementary prin- ciples of the laws which govern good health. in this way they woald be better men and women,and better able to take in the knowledge whith the masters and mistresses were only too anxious to impart to them, hut if their minds were not in a good and healthy condition it was impossible to do that. At the Close of the vocal contributions, the Bev. T. R. Matthews moved-awl Mr. .George.Bar- gess seconded a hearty TWA et thanks to, Mr„ and 205.-Emmett,on behalf of the parents and Children who had enjoyed their hospitality that evening,and the motion was carried with acclamation. Sir.Em- nnitt suitably responded remarking that the enter- tainment that evening was chiefly the idea of Mrs. Einmett,and if the children in attendance at the schools assisted the masters and misstresses in go- ing-with clean hands and faces and making a general advancement in knowledge.he had no doubt that a similar treat to. that would be given them 12 months hence, but it would depend entirely upon the,:!hildren awl their parents. A lime light exhi- bition was the concluding item. A number of local views, humorous sketches,and the photographs of Mr. and Mrs. Emmett were thrown on the sheet by Mr. GralTatd. The singing of ':thei' 'National Anthem;in which Miss Osborne Rayner led,brought the entertainthent to a close. As the"Ohildren left the ball they'Were each-presented with a bag con- taining an orange and,,' cake.</p>
