      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>FEBRUARY 11. 1898</head>
        <p>FEBRUARY 11. 1898</p>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Cornoaittee recommended that the sum of £B5 be recommended to the Council for payment to the Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Committee, being a grant from the Council for the Session 1897-98, in aid of Technical Instruction for this district.—Tlao minute was appr)coil on the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. McMahon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>GAS MANAGER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>GAS MANAGER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swann, gas manager, reported that the heaviest part of the winter being now over as regarded the con- sumption of gas, the Csuncil was again able to make all the gas that might be now required in toe district. In the water department they load repaired two broken pipes in Emmett's Brow, and two drawn joints in Legh- street. The hydrants had all been tested, and were now in good working order. The consumption, &amp;c., of water as per rogiste "ng of meters was as follows : Newton Bridge meter, 4,470,000 gallons, Viaduct Works meter, 2,030,000 gallons, total 6.500,000 gallons. corres- ponding month last year, 5,102,000 gallons, increase 1,398,000 gallons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>GAS AND WATER COMAUTTEE.</head>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMAUTTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee recDmmended that the tender of Mr. J. El. Dutton for cottage pendants, as may be required by the 13as Committee daring the year 1893, at the rate of 433. 63. per dozen, nett, be accepted. That the tender of Mr. J. H. Dutton for incandescent burners and mantles, as may be required by this committee during 1898, at the rate of Bs. 61. each, no' t (complete with governor, by-pass anti lever chain) be accepted. The above is in lieu of the offer of the Incandescent Gaslight Company accepted on December 20th, 1897.—&amp; letter, dated January 17th, was read from Mr. J. Stringer, of Earlestown, requesting the Council to reconsider the question of a reduction in his water rate.—The Clerk was directed to reply stating that the matter will be con- s;dered at the proper time in conjunction with the ques- tion of a reduction to all consumers of water within the district.—Letter, dated January 24th, was road from Mr. G. F. Clarke, on behalf of the Baptist Church, re- questing the Council to supply and fix a street lamp opposite to the chapal in Crown-st.—lt was resolved that the application be accede I to, and the Gas Manager is hereby authorised accordingly.</p>
        <p>REPORT OF THE MEDIC-AL-OFFICER OF HEALTH. , At the meeting of the Highways and Sanitary Coin- ' naittee, Dr. Watkins presented his report for the last quarter of the year 1857. There were revistered—births, 59 males, 54 fernales,deaths, 31 males, 20 females, excess of births, 25 males, 34 females. The births were at an annual average rate of 31.1, aad the deaths 14..8 per 1,000 of the estiinit,l population. The infantile deaths (under one year of age) were at an annual average rate of 22'1 per cent, The chief causes of the mortality were pulmonary diseases to which the deaths of eighteen children under five years of age, and five other persons above that age, and 23 deaths in all or 425 per cent, of the total deaths were attributed. These deaths added to 11 other children registered as due Go" other causes," led h!rn:to believe tha,. the open and mild weather of the autunan tended to make some people neglect the custom- ary precautions of the season of the year. The deaths of of 33 children under 5 years of age were very abnormal —Bll per cent, of the total .umber of \deaths. Twenty-one cases of infectious disease were notified,and were thus distributed :—Viaduct ward, 9 ; Town Hall ward, 5; Crow Lane ward, 2; WV crave ward, 3; New- ton ward, 2; total 21.. Six of these cases were removed to the Isolation Hospital.. ,</p>
        <p>SANITARY Issrxeron's MONTHLY REPORT. Mr. R. Brierley reported that the work was going on as usual in the sanitary department, but they were really shoet-handed, and r'3rl I very soon have to get more strength. He .s v_..y pleased tie be able to re- port that the district was quite clear from all infectious diseases. They had two patients in hospital, but they were expected home in a few days. Since his last re- port 74 notices had been secved on owners,and occupiers of property to abate 'lab nuisances, and they were be. jug fairly attended to. During the five weeks, the tolls for street hawking had amounted to £7 163. 91; market and etallags, £33 63. 61; total, £4l 3s, 3,1., against £26 4s. 11d, last year. TRH SURVEYOR'S MONTI:MY REPORT. Mr. Brierley reported that since the last meeting the workmen had been engaged in further macadamising Warrington and Bolton-roads,Crow-lane and St. Helens- road ; relaying the il.ggie..; in Market-street and Earle. Street . metalling and sundry repairs in Wargrave-road ; continuing the concrete flags in St. Helens-road towards the Common, and cleaning out the West outlet sludge tanks. During the following month the macadam work on the main roads would be continued, as the material arrived,which was supplied very irregularly.He propose] also so proceed with the suttees drain along the easterly side of Common-road from the canal. The general Sca- venging of the district was also receiving attention.</p>
        <p>NEW BUILDINGS. ' The Highways and Building Committee resolved that plans for eight cottages and one house for Mr. Edwin Mullineux,of Congleton, Clieshira,proPosed to be built in Wargrave-road,be approved and passed. THE HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS COHMITTEE. At a meeting Of ttils conitnlttee cue Clcris we diroot,Da be write to the Medical Officer of Health of the Local Government Board with reference to the powers of the Council relative to the deposit of offensive manure im- ported into tbe district.-4 letter was read from the Local. Government Board stating that they had sanc- tioned the reappointment of Mr. Brierley as inspector of nuisances for the district for a period of one year, from the srd day of Januarv,lB9B,to the 2nd day of January, 1899.—fhecornmittee resolved that the Surveyor be au- thorised and directed to at once proceed with the work of seweLing the whole of Athol, Albion, and Clarence- streets,the cost thereof to be apportioned amongst the several owners of premises frontini, adjoining,or abutt- ing thereon. The Chairman reported that the sub- comm mittee appointed to consider the question of alloca- tion of the duties of the Surveyor's and Sanitary Depart- ents, had decided to recommend that the question be deferred with respect to the Surveyor's Departnient, but ' with respeet to the Sanitary Department the sub-com- mittee recommend that Mr.Brierley give personal attea- Wm to the duties of that office. The Clerk reported that on Thursday.the 13th day of January, 1898, Mr. R. H. Bickaell,one of the Inspectors of the Local Givern- ment Board had held an inquiry at the Town Hall, Earlestown,into the subject matter of the application of the Council for sanction to borrow the sum of £15,000 for works of private street improvements. —lt was resolved that a suitable :lock be purchased and placed in the Board room, aad that steps be taken to Secure a better ventilation of the room. —The Surveyor submitted his estimates of the expenditure on ordlnary maintenance and repair of the main roads in the district for the year ending 31st March, 1899, amounting to the sum of £750 and of the estimate of the cost of special works of improvement daring the same period, amounting to the sum of £358 183. 81, making a total of ZIASI 18s. B.l.,which were approved, .and it was ordered that they be forwarded to the County Council. A letter Was read from Mr. A. L. Collinge, of Inca, having reference to the notice which had been served upon him to sties,cause why a stable erected by him on land adjoining 14. Wargrave-road, Hades- town, without plans having been submitted to the Coun- cil. should not be taken down, stating that he had ob- tained the consent of the Council's Surva 'P vor prior to ia- erection. After consideration by the Cadimittee the Clerk was directed to re ply stating that they had care- fully considered his statement and ba.tdocided tn sus- pend putting the resolution of December 20h, 1897,int0 effect if he would give an aasurance that he would at oncetake steps to pat the stable in a thoroughly fidAl tary condition. The Chairman moved, and - Mr. Mollahon seconded the adoption of the committee's minutes. . Mr. J. W. Emmett called attention to tbe Stat 4 of the gagging, inEarle-street, and pointed out tha; after a' saower of rain it was with difficulty that pedestrians could walk alon2 the footpath. Mr. Brierlay acknow, lodged thatthe flags needed lifting, but he was ouly tinkering with the job at present, and lifting one here and there, instead of going from one end to the other.— Mr. Collingwood said he would make it his duty to in- spect the place, and see what could be do:ie.—lle. Emmett: I wish you would.—The minutes wars then passed. Thibwas all the business of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
