      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WATER SUPPLY OF NEWTON DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>THE WATER SUPPLY OF NEWTON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>REPORT OF THE SPECIAL WATER COMMITTEE. SUGGESTED WATER WORKS.</p>
        <p>The appended report of the Special Water Committee appointed by the Newton Urb tn District Council was submitted to a meeting of the Gas and Water Committee on Monday, January 24/. " On the first day of February, 1897," says the report, " you deemed it advisable to app9int Mr. Borroo, Mr. Collingwood, Mr. Stone, Mr. Stec, and Mr. Thomp9on a special comm;ttee to exam:ne certain specifications and estimates, which were then laid before you,having refer- ence to the suggestion of establishing township's water- works,and with power (in the event of the specifications and estimates, after examinatioe, being found reliable) to negotiate for a suitable piece of ground,with further powers to spend a sum of about £250 for the purpose of constructing a borehole, and to test the quantity and quality of water therein ; and were also authorised, if considered to be necessary,toobt tin expert evidence on the subject. Your committee,at the outset,desiro to ex- plain that any apparent delay in presenting this report is owing to circumstances over which they have had no control,and beg to say that no delay whatever on their part has taken place,and they feel assured that on such an important question as this the committee will readily understand that numerous difficulties have had to be considered and overcome from time to timo.</p>
        <p>THE FIRST STEP which your committee took was to obtain ex- pert evidence as to a site,and one which,from the geolo- gical formation of the strata, would give an ample yield of water as well as at good quality, and also which would be of such an elevation as would entail the least cost to the Council in forcing the water through the mains at such a pressure as the inhabitants residing at the highest point in the Council's district would have an abundant supply of water, and also taking, into account the least cost of connecting the new supply to the exist- ing water mains. Messrs. Timrnins and Sons, of Run- corn, water engineers, were Instructed to visit the district and report upon a site. They accordingly did so, and made a careful examination of the geological maps of the district, and also viSited certain parts of the locality, and afterwards reported to the Committee. Your Committee carefully considered the report, which, though a very admirable one, Atilt lelft Mildly difficulties to be overcotre. In the first place. THE SITE RECOMMENDED BY MUSES. TIMMINS was too near the present pumping station of the London and North-Western Railway Company and Winwick Waterworks, in addition to being in an almost direct, line between these two boreholes, and the Committee were anxious not to get in too close proximity to either of them. Secondly, the adoption of the site, recom- mended by them would have involved eonsiderable ex- penditure in the laying of large water mains from Parkside to the existing mains, in addition to the diffi- culty of crossing the railway bridge in Parkside-road with a large water main ; and, thirdly, your Committee were already in possession of a report, dated 1870. from Mr. Hill, water engineer to the Manchester Corporation (and who stands well in his profession), recommending another site which, he reported, pointed to an abandant supply of good water being obtainable, as it had a large area of red sandstone to draw upon ; and your Commit- tee did not feel justified in disregarding a report from so high an authority. They therefore felt themselves placed in a very embarrassing position, and they decided (real- log sure that the full Committee would acquiesce in doing so) to call in further expert evidence on such an important matter as the question of a town's supply of water. Mr. D. Gaskin, M.1.C.E., water engineer to the Corporation:of Notfingbam,and who was formerly water engineer to the St. Helens Corporation, and well as- quainted with the geological formation of this district, was then called in by your Committee, and after attend- ing before us and discussing the whole subject, examin- ing geological maps, and visiting the locality, reported in due course. The Committee Mee carefully consider, d this report in conjunction with that which they had already received from Mesa% Titninins,ef Runcorn,and, after due deliberation, ultimately decided to adopt the site recommended by Mr. Gaskin, that is, ff a" plot of land, containing about one statute acre, on the north silo of Southworth-road, in Newton-in-Mak,rfield. im- mediately west of and an the Wig= and Winwick Railway (L. and N.W.), and felt EVERY CONFIDENCE IN ADOPTING MR. BASKIN'S RECO3I.</p>
        <p>MENDATION, because of its being the very site •recommended in MO by Mr. aii, the water engineer to the Mancheste! Coy. poration, and which, in the opinion of the Committee, is the very best site in the district for the nurnose there- fore the weight of evidence was in favour of this selec- tion, and your committee accordingly deci led upon this soot, and venture to hope that they will have no cauge to regret having done so. The nest oiestinn to be decided was the terms upon which the lend could be ob- tained for the purpose of making the test, as both Lord Newton, the owner, and Thoreas Blither, the tenant, had to be considered. However, after further negotia- tions, satisfectory terms were made for the site. Tenders were then invited for the construction of the borehole, and, as you are aware, these matters all take up a considerable amount of time, but eventually the contract was let to Mr. John Thom, of Patricrofr, who the Committee regret to say, was behind time AO 130 tha completion ot the rim part of his contract. We now append</p>
        <p>MR. BRIERLEY'S REPORT as to the work of boring to the depth stipulated ty the first part of Mr. Them's contract I beg to report that the contractor (Mr. Thom), in his first operations, bored a 6-inch hole to a denth of about 36 feet, beyond this, a 3-inch hole to about 60 feet from the surface to supply water for feeding his steam boilers. He then commenced to bore a 20. inch hole to a depth of 152 feet. The greater part of the boring Was through a soft yellow sandstone of a loamy nature, the last few feet being into A hard grey sandstone interspersed with streaks of red sandstone, which, in my opinion, is of a good watere bearing nature. The contractor, having sunk to the limit of your instruetione for proving the water, the boring was diaContinued; a 14-inch pume and lining was fixed capable of raising 20,000 to 25,000 gallons of water per hour, to ascertain what quantity of water it would yield ; on computation, this has been proved to give about 8,000 gallons an hour, and after about 10 days' pumping the samples were taken and forwarded to the county analyst, who reports the water to be soft and clear. The water in the boreholes rose about 10 feet after pumping operations load been diacontinued,and has since stood at a normal level of about 18 f ,t below the surface. I have no doubt whatever that, if boring opera- tions are dontinned through the pbrrectan beds, an abun- dant yield of water will he obtained. I therefore suggest that the contractor be instructed to proceed with the boreholes as originally tendered for in his schedule of prices, to such depth as you may find necessary, which, in my opinion, should be froM 300 to 450 feet, of which the previous trial beringe will form part. From my long knowledge of the district, supported by the recommen- dations of Bar. Enn and Mr. Gaskin, the two experts, who have reported favourably on the site. I consider the Council will be quite justified in securing it for a per- manent ',Aping station, feeling assured you are now safe for a bountiful supply of good water." THE COUNTY ANALYST'S SEPOR: . From the foregoing report it will be obit- red says the committee, that a sample of water, both f rem the 152 feet borehole and tbe 60 feet borehole (the latter used for supplying the steam boiler with water) was taken after somelo days' pumping, and submitted to Dr: Campbell Brown, the county analyst, for his examination and re- port, and your committee are pleaeed to learn that the quality of the water compares very favourably indeed with, the water at present supplied in bulk to the Coun- cil by the London and North-Western Railway Company. The following is a copy of the Analyst's (Dr. Campbell Brown's) report :—I have analysed the sample of water repeived from your Council oa the 22nd day of December 1897, with the following results expressed in parts per 100,000 : 'No. 1 Well, No. 2 Well. 60ft. Boiler Fe :Br. 201n.Borehnle, 130 f t. Total solid matter:ln solution 11.6 172 Organic Carbon f Traces only. Traces only. Organic Nitrogen Ammonia .004 .0)5 Ammonia from Organic .005 •CJS matter by distill ,tion wih alkaline permanganate. Nitrogen as Nitrates .37L Combined Chlorine Hardness B} deg moderate. 7 d moderate. Clear Lie ir. Free from organisms. Few organ:sms. No.l is excellent water. No. 2 is not bad. THE COMMITTEE'S DEDUCTIONS. After giving a comparative statement of analysis of the samples of water from the present supplies the re- port continues :—Tite water pumped from toe Council's borehole is a slightly bolter quality of water than what is at present being supplied to us by the Railway Co., and is 136 degrees softer. As regards the quantity,your committee are advised that a yield of 8,000 gallons per hour,from a depth of only 152 feet fro.n the serface,ie a certain indication that at a depth of from 300 to 450 feet(the Railway Comyany's boreholes are 457 feet deep) a yield of water will be obtained of such a quantity as will be more than ample for the requirements of the inhabitants of this district for many years to come. Your committee therefore having regard to the very favourable analysis of the water by the analyst, and of the certain indication of an abundant supply at a lower depth, and, considering that there cart be no more in- taut sanitary duty that an Urban District Council has to perform—and ought to perform to the best of its ability, regardles of cost—than to insure a pure,whole- some, and ample supply of water throughout the whole of is district (though not complaining of the quality ef the water at present supplied to the Connell by the London and North Western Railway Company), and the only way, in their opinion, to do this in a thoroughly and highly efficient manner is tc place the control of the water supply absolutely in the hands of the Council. They are therefore, unanimous in recommending the General Water Committee of the Council to at once take the necessary steps for proceeding with the whole scheme for establishing townships, wa erwarke on the site recommended, and u • • as been</p>
        <p>made. The work already done will, of courAe, become part of the permanent schome. Your committee also desire to add that they have every confidence in stating that, if the work is proceeded with,the cost of the water , per 1,000 gallons to the Council will be considerably re- duced in comparison with the price now paid to the Railway Company. It would therefore naturally follow that on completion of the scheme, and of the necessary 'addition water mains,that the question of a correspond- ing reduction to the ratepayers could then be con- sidered."</p>
        <p>THE DECISION Or THE WATER COMMITTEE. After a discussion it was (by elevin votes to three) resolved that the report of Special Water Committee now read be approved and adopted. It was also resolved that the Special Water Committee be reappointed, viz., Mr. Barron, Mr. Cohim:woad, Mr. Ston,,,, Mr. Slee, and Mr. Thompson,to take such further steps as may appear to them to be necessary for proceeding with the scheme of establishing township's waterworks, with power to obtain estimates for the most suitable works and plant, and again report to this committee as early as con- venient.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Council on Monday afternoon the resolution was unanimously passed on the motion of the Chairman (Mr. Borron), seconded by Mr. McMahon.</p>
      </div>
