      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODEST BAZAAR AT</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODEST BAZAAR AT</p>
        <p>EA RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the effort which is being made by the Primitive Methodist body of Earlestown to raise funds for clearing dff the debt which still remains upon their chapel and school, a three days bazaar was on Thursday opened in the Tewn Earlestown, by Mr. T. Ormand. The object as set forth in the introduction to the programme is stated to be in aid of the Trust Debt Extinction fund. Nearly three years ago Mr. W. P. Hartley and a friend generously offered to subscribe one-fourth of the debt of £530, then upon the premises, on condition that the remaining three-fourths be raised by about March IEO3. Te meet this condition, corn- bined with what is needed for renovating and beautify- ing the premises,a sum of £2OO is still required. On Thursday afternoon there was a very large number of people present,principally ladies. The Rev. E. R.Davies presided, and he was accompanied on the platform by the Revs. T. R. Matthews, P. R. Passmore, and F. E. Miller. After a preliminary hymn and prayer The Chairman said very few words were needed from him before calling upon Mr. Orrnand to open the bazaar. He thought they could congratulate themselves upon arriving at that day, and he had no doubt some of them would be more glad to see next Saturday night, or Sun- day morning when they hoped to have a different tale to tell. From certain events they were led to expect a fair measure of success: They had a good cause, and that was half the battle. They had not only a good cause, but their friends associated with the church and con- gregation, and friends outside had been working for a long time, and harmoniously and when the result was known there must be success as the outcome of all this work.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ormand said the object was one with which they had been connected and interested in for some years. He asked what were they going to raise? They wanted at least £258, and whether they raised that amount or not was in their bands. Three years ago at the end of February they had (for them) an enormous debt of £875 on the premises. In the third week in February, 1895, they had a sile of work which realised £145, leaving a debt of £530. A gent,em in came for ward , and with Mr. J. P. Hartley promised that if the 'congregation were successful in raising three-fourths in three years, they would pay the re- mender. The time was now at hand Wi1.312 they had to redeem that undertaking. The friends were equal to the ocoasion,and two years ago the net proceeds of a bazaar held in 1898 was £2BO, which with a dona- tion oy £5O from the gentleman named, .enabled them to pay off £330, leaving a debt ;of £2OO. The ladies sewing meeting had raised £2O, and the gentle- . men already referred to contributing another £5O they were able to pay off another £7O, leaving £l3O which had to be paid in order that the church might redeem its promise. Thus they wanted £l3O to pay off the debit, to clear the incumbrance, and they wanted £7O to put the school and chapel premises into a thorough state of repair, and beautify them externally and internally, and .250 for refurnishing the . Minister's house. He taen formally declared the bazaar open, and business was at once proceeded with.</p>
        <p>The hall was very tastefully decorated, and the stalls being ,well laden with all manner of articles usually seen at bizsars, a very pretty effect was obtained. Ladies sewing mecting.stall, No. 1, was in charge Mesdames Ormand. W. Austin, Joseph Austin. Ladies' sewing stall, No. 2 : Mesdames George, Torrilimson,John Austin and Bagnall. Young ladies stall : Misses Newton, A. Dickens, Sephton and L.Pickens. Gentlemen's stall: Messrs. W. Austin, Joseph Austin, F.B ignall, Dobson,A. Smith, R. Sproston, P. Tomlinson, and J. Webster. Re- freshment stall : Mrs. Anton and. Mrs. Holleyock. Children's stall : Mesdames Pendry,Arrowsmith,Austin, Garrett and Itswlinson. Flower stall : Miss N.Dickehs and L. , Tither. Crockery stall: Miss Clark and Miss Stronach. The side shows consisted of an, exhibition of prize birds and fowls togetbn with a collection or curios, in charge of Messrs. J. Younger and J.Gilmour ; the cinematograph by Professor Baron of Wigan ; the phonograph, galvanic battery, a magical illusion, aria Professor Palmer, the popular palmist and phrenolowist-' The half hoot OunCOTC On Thursday nigh's, was arranged by Miss E. L. Greenall, the at tistes being Ms 3 0. Roberts, L. Smith and Berry, Messrs, Webster and R. Porter.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee consed of the whole of the members of the Church and congregation, but there was an Executive committee, the President being. Rev. E. R. Davies, hon. treasurer, Mr. F. R. Bag- nail, hon. secretary, Mr. W. Banton, hon. assiatant secretary, Messrs. E. J. Mullins, J. r. Banton, and A. Banton.</p>
        <p>OLD FOLK'S TREAT AT EA.RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that in October last, a cyNre parade was held in Earlestown, the object being the raising of funds towards providing a social evening for all old people over 60 years of age in Earlestowe and Newton. The whole affair was worked by an energetic committee, and with the aid of donations from residents' jp the district Sufficient funds were got together to en- able the committee to issue their invitations to the old people. The treat was.held in the Co-operative Hall, on Thursday evening when about 280 old people were pre- sent. The catering was undertaken by the Eeriest° wn Co-opetative Society, and It is cnly fair to say that a most excellent tea was put upon the tables and ample justice was done to the substantial fare provided. After the tables had been cleared, Mr. ' W. Gilmour chairman of the committee, presided and he was supported on the platform by the committee. The Chairmen in opening said be felt certain that every individual present would remember the treat the Cycling Club gave them a short time age,and the treat that evening was the result. That eveuieg would.be a red letter day, and would be long remembered by every one in the room. (Applause) A musical programme Was afterwards submitted, the opening item being an instrumental selection by AfeserS. J. Anderton,LtAppleton, M.C.Walsh, and I.:Efuthileston. " Darby and Joan" was very prettily sung by Miss A. Mee and Air. Peplow followed .with a violin solo. "The Village Blacksmph was ably , rendered by Mr. W. H. Kay, , and "Kathleen Mavour- neen " was submitted in an exceptionally Clever and efficient manner :by Miss. Beatty: Messrs. Kay and Coupe were most successful in the duet "Excelsior," and , Mr. Huddleston brought the first part of the programme to a close by the humorous song "I couldn't do anything elso,"and ot course he was recalled when he gave "Kiss- ing cup's story."At the interval the Rev. T.R. Matthews moved and Mr. Gosling seconded a Vote of thanks to the committee, artistes and ladies who presided at the tables, thelollowiug being the names :—Mesdames Haste, Millington, D. Berry, Lamb, Batiks, Womager, Coombs, Wareing, Bowes, Austin; Chorlton, Glover, Davies, Scott, Low,Crossley,Appleton,Anderton, Misses Coonebs,Thompson,Preston, Jameson, L.Conway. This vote was very enthusiastically acaorded, and the Chairman responded on behalf of the Committee whowere Messrs. Gilmour, Preston, Austin' J. E. E dens:Coombs', Crossley, Porter, Lamb, • Clough, Dickenson, Clarke. Anderton, and T. Waring (secretary.) Each of the oid people were afterwards presents i with an orange: and the prograrruce was proceeded. with 89 follows. :—Quar- tette, band s6pg, "Anchor's Weighed,". Mr. Coupe ; cornet solo "The old folks at home," Mr. J. Appleton ; song, " The better land," bales Beatty ; violin solo, Mr. Peplow •, seng, "Robin Adair," Miss Mee; humorous song ; Mr. J. Huddleston. At the close the old people dispersed thoroughly, pleased with their entertainment, and those who were unable to Walk were cogyeyed home in wagonnettes. in thesame way that they were brought to the hall.</p>
        <p>We might add that as an appropriate•wind up to the concert, Mr. J. Traverse sang " The Jolly Wasoner," and the oldest man in the room, Gs )rge Jones, 96 years ef age, sang "Home sweet borne," the old people join- ing in.</p>
        <p>CAMDEN SUNDAY SCIE,OL, RUNCORN. A grand concert in connection with the above Sunday school, was given on Thursday evening, in the school, Lowland-road, Mr. A. R. Norman, J.P.. presiding. In the course of a short addriss Mr. Norman spoke of the value of Sunday school work, and gave the teachers a , few encouraging words to stick to their guns. Speaking to the scholars he warned them that they would be more- lit for life's hard battle after a good Sunday school tra-- log. The following programme was then gone through: —Overture, "La Chevalier Breton," 'the Orchestra ; humorous song, " When I was a by at School," Mr. J. Berkett ; cornet sao, serenade, Mr. 'l'. Knight ; 800:, " The PromisJ of Life," Mies Kirkham ; duett, " Excelsior," Messrs. E. Worrall and 'l'. Johnson ; song, " Queen of tho Earth," Mr. R. Irrj; song, " Angus Macdonald," Miss Sinakin ; air, " Arm, Arm ye brave," Mr. T Johnson ; song, " Maid of the Mill," Mr. E. Worrell ; duett, " Two Merry Hearts," Mines Kirkham and Simkin ; song, •• Every inch a Soldier," Mr R. Morris ; humorous song, "A. little very often means a lot," Mr. J. Birkett ; selection (Shepherd' Dance), The Orchestra; duett by Miss Simkin and Mr. Morris ; "God save tho Queen." Accoinp trust : Mrs. W. B. Kay. Oa the motion of Mr. C:ark. a vote of thanks was accorded to the chairman and artistes for their service. Tire proceeds are to be handed C 1 er to a school funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-02-11_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT IN A FOOTBALL MATCH, Whilst playing in a football match at Perike'.;b,aa Saturday afternoon, Mr. Lionel Fairclough, son of Alderman Fairclough,was accidentally charged from behind,and,being violently thrown to the ground,he sustained a fracture of the thigh. We are glad to learn that he is progressing as favourably as can I , expected. The match was between the Warrington A.P.O Reserve and the Friends' School,Panketh. CJNSERTATIVE WORKING MEN'S BALL. The annual Conservative Working Men's Ball was held in the Conservative Club Assembly Room on 1 Monday when about 240 were present. Mr. Percy . 1 Randales band supplied the music, which gave the highest satisfaction. There was a wall-laid supper table, and light refreshments were provide". Ar. and Mrs. Watkins were the caterers. Al t•-r nail Sutton, Couuciller Smethurst, and 11r. C. H. iruci, were most capable M.C.'s. Mr. E. T. Dainticli dis- charged the duties of secretary. . WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB., The seventh meeting of the winter session was held on Friday evening Week, ' when the Rev. H. i Brierly read an able introductory paper on A !gas, describing the principal features of the four great strikingly y Plants Which in structure are parallel wllleoltlo the fungi. The most satisla t I roilp yC so l a ef ph i tfte atio a of Algae is based upon the various. - modes of reproduction, which range from simple fission to a method as truly sexual as that of 'the highest plants and animals. Mr.Brierly illustrated' .1 this difficult subject with sketches on the black board, and at the conclusion of his paper wcs warmly congratulated on his contribution to the proceedings of the club. Dr Bowden then lectured A on the physiology of respiration.. He begau with a brief. but admirable description of the machinery .i by which human respiration is'effected, and then I proceeded to deal will the chemical cocaposition of 1 alveolar air and venous blood, and the means by A ..1 which an interchange of their gases takes place. His . clear description of the probable nature of a 'some- . what obscure process was much enjoyed. .. THE P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A. ROOMS. • , Mr. W. Roberts occupied the chair at the meeting of this society on Sunday afternoon, and was sup- ported on the platform by the acting vice-presiden:, • Mr: W. Gregson. 3•lr. Roberts spoke in terms of A warm appreciation of the services during the last five years of Mr. T. S. Kent, the retiring president of the P.S.A , and in introducing the newly-elected president, Mr. Hickman. to give his inauguvl , address, expressed the confident belief that under the able and judicious guidance of the new presi- dent the splendid success of the past five years would be fully maintained and the usefulness of the society largely increased. Mr. W. Hickman paid.a very high compliment to his predecossor,Mr. Kent, and urged every member to do his best to help on the work of this greatehristian movemen'.. Hiss H. Perkins rendered two soloq, which were miteh.appieciated. Mr. Frank Hickman was the accompanist. Mr. G. B. Holford presided at the organ, and Mr. Woods and the Secretary(Mr. Clegg) also took part in the meeting.</p>
